{"raw": {"https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Associate-Professor-of-Practice--Construction-Engineering-and-Management_R0153872": {"title": "Assistant/Associate/Professor of Practice, Construction Engineering and Management", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The School of Civil, Chemical, and Environmental Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno, invites applications for a for a nine-month, non-tenure-track faculty position. The successful applicant will be appointed as Assistant/Associate or Professor of Practice in Construction Engineering Management (CEM), depending on the applicant\u2019s qualifications, terminal degree, and level of relevant professional experience. This position entails teaching, curriculum development, student mentoring, industry engagement, and service responsibilities, as well as current industry expertise and support of student-facing professional engagement, including clubs, competitions, travel, professional development and networking, and other experiential learning opportunities. \u00a0 Based on the University\u2019s mission as a land grant institution, faculty duties will include undergraduate and graduate teaching, supervision of graduate students, leading development of an ABET accredited CEM degree program, and university and professional service. The intended start date for the position is July 1, 2027.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Required Qualifications Candidates will be hired to the appropriate level based on education and experience. An M.S. degree in construction engineering and management, construction management, civil engineering, or a closely related field. A minimum of 5 years of relevant industry experience in construction management or engineering. Demonstrated establishment of network connections within the construction industry, and a track record of collaboration with companies, organizations, and professionals. Commitment to fostering an inclusive environment and promoting diversity in higher education. Preferred Qualifications In addition to the above-required qualifications the following qualifications are preferred. Licensure as a professional engineer or demonstration of an intent to become registered. Demonstrated experience in the teaching of relevant construction engineering and management courses including laboratories at the undergraduate level. Strong communication and presentation skills. Experience in a leadership role. \u00a0 Compensation Compensation is competitive and commensurate with rank, education, and experience, consistent with the appropriate faculty compensation grade within the Nevada System of Higher Education. This is a 9-month, \u201cB\u201d contract appointment. The salary schedule can be found here: Salary Schedules and Faculty Overload | Human Resources under \u201cAcademic Faculty Engineering (nine-month contract) salaries. \u00a0 \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options .\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 School of Civil, Chemical, and Environmental Engineering Shape the future and advance your career at the School of Civil, Chemical and Environmental Engineering at University of Nevada, Reno College of Engineering. With a faculty and staff team of more than 40, CCEE offers ABET-accredited undergraduate programs and nationally recognized graduate programs in civil, chemical, and environmental engineering. Faculty and students advance sustainable infrastructure, environmental stewardship, biotechnology, renewable energy, water resources, and earthquake resilience. Home to the Earthquake Engineering Laboratory and leading water research centers, CCEE offer strong connections to industry and access to world-class outdoor recreation. \u00a0 College Information - College of Engineering Join the College of Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno We value faculty and staff as partners in our mission to deliver outstanding education, research and engagement. Our college seeks individuals committed to: Engaging in impactful outreach Providing state-of-the-art education Fostering respect, inclusiveness and diversity Nevada Engineering is a fast-growing college, with research expenditure that has doubled over the past nine years. Supported by an Engineering Research Office, the college recorded $24 million in expenditures in FY26 and offers professional development resources to ensure success. We offer bachelor\u2019s, graduate degrees and certificates through three schools: Civil, Chemical and Environmental Engineering Computer Science, Electrical, and Biomedical Engineering Aerospace, Industrial, Materials, and Mechanical Engineering Our specialized facilities include the Center for Civil Engineering Earthquake Research, Cybersecurity Center, the Nanofabrication Lab in the Davidson Foundation Cleanroom and the Nevada Water Innovation Institute. Located near the Tahoe-Reno Industrial Center and offering ready access to the bustling California economy, Nevada Engineering collaborates with leading tech firms and advance research in robotics, AI, battery technology, aerospace, water systems and sustainable energy. In Reno, you will find world-class outdoor recreation opportunities \u2014 including skiing, hiking, biking and water sports \u2014 in nearby Lake Tahoe and throughout the northern Nevada region. Learn more at unr.edu/engineering Contact Information: Search Committee Chair, Adam Hand ( adamhand@unr.edu ) Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note that once you submit your application, the only attachments viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachments to the cover letter, references, and other documents sections of the application will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachments will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments before the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . \u00a0 Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for three professional references 4) Teaching statement \u00a0\u00a0 Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by January 15, 2027. \u00a0 This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received, or a hiring decision has been made. \u00a0 Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Atmospheric-Sciences_R0153456": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Atmospheric Sciences", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Physics, Atmospheric Sciences Program, at the University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) is seeking applications for a tenure track (9-month) Assistant Professor position with an expected hire date of July 1, 2027 or after. \u00a0We are seeking candidates with innovative research agendas that complement and expand our current atmospheric science initiatives. Competitive startup funds are available. Example Research Specialties Include: Atmospheric instrumentation development. Cloud and aerosol microphysics, including wildland fire smoke and ash generation and propagation. Artificial Intelligence applications in mountain meteorology, weather forecasting, and fire science. Climate implications of water and energy use for powering AI. Mountain meteorology, especially downwind of the Sierra Nevada. Atmospheric Science education in the age of AI. Development and use of fast-physics for meteorological and climate models. Forensic meteorology. Position Responsibilities: The successful candidate will be expected to mentor undergraduate and graduate students in research and academic advisement. Teaching responsibilities will include core courses such as Atmospheric Physics, Instrumentation, Dynamical Meteorology, and Radiation Transfer, with the possibility of teaching additional Physics courses. There are also excellent opportunities to utilize and oversee established observational infrastructure, including a NASA AERONET sun photometer, a NASA MPLnet lidar, and multispectral photoacoustic instrumentation for measuring aerosol light scattering and absorption. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Atmospheric Science, Physics or related field. Preferred Qualifications Postdoctoral or equivalent experience. Demonstrated ability to pursue external funding. Demonstrated commitment to student success via teaching and/or mentoring. Compensation Grade Assistant Professor, 9-month To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty (9-month). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR! Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits but, in order to be eligible, children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada.\u00a0 Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information Housed within the Department of Physics, the Atmospheric Sciences program offers both undergraduate and Ph.D. degrees, as well as an accelerated M.S. degree for advanced undergraduates. Established at UNR in 1965, the graduate program is jointly administered by campus faculty and the Division of Atmospheric Sciences at the nearby Desert Research Institute (DRI), offering rich opportunities for collaborative research. Strong alignment and engagement with the broader Physics program's students and faculty is highly valued. For more information, please visit: https://www.unr.edu/physics .\u00a0 Location & Lifestyle The city of Reno is situated just downslope of the Sierra Nevada mountains, offering exceptional opportunities for mountain meteorology research as well as an outstanding quality of life. The region provides world-class outdoor recreation, including hiking, skiing, and swimming in world-renowned Lake Tahoe, all while maintaining easy access to the vibrant San Francisco Bay Area. Contact Information Beth Jacobsen at 775-682-8787 or jacobsenb@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu . Application Materials: The following materials must be uploaded with your application: 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Research Statement highlighting past research and future interests (4 pages maximum); this statement should include the types of research projects envisioned for graduate and undergraduate students and likely funding sources. Please include one (1) lead author publication that the applicant would most like the committee to read to help assess originality of ideas and science communication skills. 4) Teaching Statement (2 pages maximum). Discuss prior teaching, mentoring, and/or outreach experiences. Describe your teaching philosophy and educational goals at UNR, specifically stating the Atmospheric Science courses you would prefer most to teach and any you would prefer not to teach. \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 5) Contact Information for three professional references Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by October 22, 2026. Posting Close Time This posting will close at 12:00 am on the date listed below. The posting will no longer be available to apply to after 11:59 pm the day prior. Posting Close Date 01/1/2027 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Teaching-Assistant-Professor--Nutrition_R0153910-1": {"title": "Teaching Assistant Professor, Nutrition", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Nutrition within the College of Agriculture, Biotechnology and Natural Resources (CABNR) at the University of Nevada, Reno is seeking applications for a Teaching Assistant Professor. This is a full-time position based in the Department of Nutrition at the University of Nevada, Reno. The role involves 90% teaching and 10% service. \u00a0 Responsibilities The Teaching Assistant Professor will serve as an integral member of the Department faculty and help maintain existing excellence in teaching and instruction as well as best practices in collegiate instruction in nutrition. The role will also support Department programs and contribute to the development of future academic programs, including a doctoral program in Nutrition. The successful candidate will be expected to:\u00a0\u00a0Develop and deliver high-quality, in-person and online instruction in department courses in Nutrition at the undergraduate and graduate level. Mentor and evaluate departmental graduate teaching assistants. Mentor and supervise graduate students, and/or serve on graduate committees. Contribute to a vibrant and inclusive academic community through collegiality, cooperation, and collaboration. \u00a0 Required Qualifications Ph.D. in nutrition or a closely related field. A strong track record of effective teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Experience in developing and evaluating courses at the undergraduate and graduate level. Experience with developing online and distance learning courses. Experience working in an interdisciplinary environment. Proven ability to work with diverse audiences. Demonstrate leadership skills. \u00a0 Compensation Teaching Assistant Professor, Nutrition \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty. \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 Department Information https://www.unr.edu/cabnr Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Please attach the following documents to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for three professional references 4) A statement of teaching interests and philosophy (two pages maximum). \u00a0 This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will remain open until filled, but applications will be reviewed beginning November 20th . Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Educational-Equity_R0153805": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Educational Equity", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Educational Studies at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for an assistant professor of educational equity, beginning July 1, 2027. This is a 9-month, tenure-track position. Responsibilities include, but are not limited to, teaching undergraduate, masters, and doctoral students, pursuing a substantial and relevant program of research, advising students, and continuing professional development. The faculty will have an interdisciplinary focus on educational disparities and systemic inequalities, equity, intersectionality, cultural analysis, and social justice.\u00a0 Appointment includes online and/or face-to-face teaching in both graduate and undergraduate programs.\u00a0 Faculty will also be expected to contribute to growing and evolving programs in a variety of capacities including, but not limited to, curriculum development and innovation, collaborating with colleagues on programmatic structural revisions and program evaluation, instructional design, research, collaboration with school districts and other community partners, and working closely with undergraduate and graduate students. Required Qualifications Earned Doctorate in Education or a related field by August 2027 Evidence of scholarship in topics related to educational equity Preferred Qualifications Experience in teaching undergraduate and graduate courses related to educational equity or closely related fields Experience in teaching courses relevant to the education of multilingual learners Experience in teaching asynchronous and synchronous online courses \u00a0 Experience in working with populations from diverse backgrounds Experience in working with or advising graduate students Experience in grant implementation and/or obtaining external funding Experience with communities and/or organizations to promote educational equity and advocate for historically marginalized populations Compensation Assistant Professor ($80,000 - $85,000) \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty. \u00a0 \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefit E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 Department Information THE EDUCATIONAL EQUITY PROGRAM is a key part of the Educational Studies Department at the University of Nevada, Reno. The program offers an online Master\u2019s degree in Equity and Diversity, certification in Teaching English to Speakers of Other Languages (TESOL), and an doctoral degree strand in Equity, Diversity, and Language Education. Program faculty teach and advise MS and PhD students and teach undergraduate students seeking teaching licensure. We seek a tenure-track assistant professor to help further develop and expand the program.\u00a0 Contact Information Please direct questions to the search chair, Dr. Lynda Wiest at wiest@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References This posting is open until filled. Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. APPLICATION PRIORITY REVIEW DATE: 30 days after posting. Posting Close Date 11/12/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Associate-Professor---Full-Professor--Social-Work-Director-of-Faculty-Development_R0153646": {"title": "Associate Professor / Full Professor, Social Work/Director of Faculty Development", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Director of Faculty Development is a key member of the Dean\u2019s Office. This position provides strategic leadership for faculty development and advancement across the School of Social Work, strengthening support for full-time faculty in teaching, research, and service while fostering a culture of continuous improvement, innovation, mentorship, and professional growth. This permanent academic faculty position is 12-month and on-site at the University of Nevada, Reno.\u00a0 Position Responsibilities In collaboration with the Dean\u2019s Office, the Director will provide leadership in the following areas: Faculty Development and Mentorship: Design, implement, and evaluate comprehensive faculty development initiatives aligned with the School\u2019s mission, institutional goals, and strategic priorities. Develop structured mentoring and professional development opportunities that support faculty growth, retention, and career advancement.\u00a0 Faculty Evaluation: Lead the School\u2019s annual faculty evaluation processes, including annual performance reviews, ensuring consistency, transparency, fairness, and alignment with School and University policies and standards. Faculty Orientation and Professional Growth: Develop and implement comprehensive faculty orientation programs and ongoing professional development and training opportunities for faculty across all ranks and career stages. Promotion and Tenure: Support tenure-track faculty in navigating the promotion and tenure process, including providing guidance on portfolio development, documentation, and preparation for retention, promotion, and tenure reviews. Provide similar career advancement support to non-tenure-track and teaching faculty. Facilitate the school's tenure and promotion processes. Career Advancement and Mentoring: Provide direct mentorship to junior, teaching, and other full-time faculty, with particular attention to professional development, career planning, advancement, and successful integration into the School. Policy and Academic Leadership: Collaborate with the Dean and Associate Dean on faculty-related policies, procedures, and initiatives that strengthen academic programs and promote effective faculty engagement and success. Assessment and Continuous Improvement: Contribute to a culture of assessment, innovation, and continuous improvement by evaluating faculty development initiatives and using findings to inform future programming and School-level priorities. Will also be engaged in Council of Social Work Education (CSWE) accreditation-related assessment and activities. Teaching Responsibilities In addition to administrative and faculty development responsibilities, the Director will contribute to the School\u2019s instructional mission by teaching undergraduate and graduate Social Work courses across program modalities, including in-person and online instruction. This position offers an experienced faculty leader a unique opportunity to shape the professional development and career advancement of faculty across all ranks while contributing to the School of Social Work's continued excellence, innovation, and growth. Required Qualifications PhD in social work or related field. Master of Social Work (MSW) from a CSWE-accredited institution and 2 years of post-MSW experience. 5 years of progressively responsible academic leadership experience in higher education, with demonstrated experience in faculty development, mentoring, evaluation, and academic personnel processes. Tenured or tenure-eligible. Experience with CSWE accreditation review. Record of teaching and scholarly achievement commensurate with appointment at the Associate level or Professor level. Excellent oral, written, interpersonal, and executive communication skills. Ability to manage timelines, projects, documentation, and competing priorities with accuracy and discretion. High level of professionalism, confidentiality, accountability, and service orientation. Demonstrated commitment to anti-racism, diversity, equity, inclusion, and social justice. Preferred Qualifications Knowledge of promotion and tenure standards and processes within a research-intensive university, preferably an R1 institution.\u00a0 Schedule and Travel Requirements Travel occasional\u202f Compensation Associate Professor\u00a0 Full Professor\u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit Salary Schedules and select (12-month contract) Academic Faculty.\u00a0 \u202fPerks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u202f Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u202f Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u202f\u202f E.L. Wiegand Fitness Cente r Mountain EAP \u202fsupports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u202f Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u202f Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u202f Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program College/Department Information https://www.unr.edu/social-work \u00a0 Interested candidates attending CSWE's annual conference between October 22nd and 24th in Atlanta, GA, can reach out to Dean Lillian Wichinsky (email: lwichinsky@unr.edu) or Associate Dean Eugenia Weiss (eugeniaw@unr.edu) to schedule a brief in-person meeting during the conference to learn more about the position. \u00a0 Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note that once you submit your application, the only attachments viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachments to the cover letter, references, and other documents sections of the application will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachments will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments before the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . \u00a0 Attach the following attachments to your application\u00a0 1) Resume/CV\u00a0 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References 4) Faculty Development and Mentoring Statement (2-3 pages) Describe experience developing faculty. Mentoring junior faculty. Supporting promotion and tenure. Building faculty development programming. This is probably the single most important supplemental document for this role. 5) Administrative Leadership Statement (2 pages maximum)\u00a0 Leadership philosophy.\u00a0 Experience with evaluation processes.\u00a0 Policy development.\u00a0 Managing change and faculty engagement.\u00a0 Assessment and continuous improvement This posting is open until filled.\u00a0 Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date 11/6/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Part-Time--Artist-Fellowship--Black-Rock-Press--LOA-_R0153707-1": {"title": "Part-Time, Artist Fellowship, Black Rock Press (LOA)", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nPart time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for the Black Rock Press Fellowship. This nine-month appointment offers an artist working in fine press printing, publication arts, and/or book arts, the opportunity to work within an established academic press, develop a new body of work, and engage a public audience. The fellowship runs Jan through Sept 2027 and unfolds in three distinct phases, structured to support both professional development and the creation of a new editioned work. A special word of gratitude to the Nell J. Redfield Foundation, whose philanthropy over the years has uplifted Black Rock Press at UNR. Responsibilities In the Spring 2027 semester (January-May), fellow will teach one book arts course within the department and contribute to active academic program for the department and students In the Summer 2027 session (June-August), fellow will assist with Black Rock Press facilities to develop and produce a new editioned work of their own design In September 2027, fellow will present their work at a public artist talk highlighting their practice and work during residency. Candidate will also have to conduct a public workshop in Black Rock Press, tailored to their specialty and interests in book arts, printing, and publication. Studio Equipment Provided Vandercook proofing letterpresses: Universal 1, No. 4 and No. 219, in addition C&P and Hand Presses More than 150 cases of metal type The Barbara Ann Kelly Wood Type Collection Photopolymer platemaker Hollander Beater & paper making supplies Riso Printer with 13 colors (SE9480) 40\" Board shear Screen printing available in CFA Required Qualifications Candidate must have knowledge and strong skills in book arts and/or fine press printing, with emphasis on concept, design, and craft. Candidate must have prior letterpress, book editioning, and/or fine printing experience Candidate must have exceptional craftmanship and artistic vision Candidate must be a permanent U.S. resident, current UNR affiliates are not eligible Preferred Qualifications All artists regardless of career stage are encouraged to apply Schedule 32 hours/weekly Compensation Grade LOA Salaried, Part-Time Over a nine-month period, total salary: $39,330 The candidate will receive a stipend in paid installments as well as full access to Black Rock Press equipment and facilities throughout the appointment. The fellow is responsible for personal housing, travel, and materials costs. Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 80.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachment(s) to your application\u00a0 Resume/CV \u2013 (required) List a detailed description of the major duties that you performed as part of each job. You must demonstrate that you qualify for the position and your education and experience must be clearly documented. Cover Letter \u2013 (required) Describe your interest in this fellowship and a preliminary concept for the work you would develop during the summer studio phase (No more than 500 words) Contact Information for Two Professional References \u2013 (required) Please include name, contact information, and relationship. Portfolio \u2013 (required) Please upload your portfolio with 8-10 examples of work with descriptions included, PDF or URL Link Application review will begin November 2, 2026. This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. Recruitment will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date 11/2/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Research-Scientist--Transportation-Engineering_R0153803": {"title": "Research Scientist, Transportation Engineering", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The School of Civil, Chemical, and Environmental Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno is hiring a Research Scientist for their Transportation Engineering program. This is a full-time, non-tenure track position with an anticipated start date of November 1, 2026. Primary responsibilities will include: \u00a0 Conduct and support research on traffic signal retiming at individual intersections and coordinated corridors using WaySync and other traffic analysis, modeling, and simulation tools. Guide and mentor Ph.D. and M.S. students in their research and academic activities in areas including traffic operations and control, traffic simulation and modeling, data-driven transportation applications, and transportation system resilience. Support the supervisor in managing laboratory research activities, coordinating research projects, and developing research proposals. Identify federal, state, local, and other external funding opportunities and independently lead the development and preparation of research proposals. Prepare technical reports, peer-reviewed research papers, presentations, and other research project deliverables. Coordinate and communicate with research sponsors, local transportation agencies, the Nevada Department of Transportation (NDOT), and other project partners to support project implementation and delivery. Collaborate with faculty members in the transportation engineering program on interdisciplinary research, proposal development, publications, and other scholarly activities. Support other research and educational activities within the department as needed. \u00a0 Required Qualifications PhD in Transportation Engineering \u00a0 Compensation Grade University Instructor (Research Scientist) \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules and select Administrative Faculty by Grade. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. \u00a0 Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0 \u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow! \u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous annual, sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options .\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 Department Information Shape the future and advance your career at the School of Civil, Chemical and Environmental Engineering (CCEE) at University of Nevada, Reno College of Engineering. With a faculty and staff team of more than 40, CCEE offers ABET-accredited undergraduate programs and nationally recognized graduate programs in civil, chemical, and environmental engineering. Faculty and students advance sustainable infrastructure, environmental stewardship, biotechnology, renewable energy, water resources, and earthquake resilience. Home to the Earthquake Engineering Laboratory and leading water research centers, CCEE offer strong connections to industry and access to world-class outdoor recreation. The CCEE is a teaching and research unit within the College of Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno. The school provides education and conducts research across a broad range of civil and environmental engineering disciplines. Its transportation engineering program has strengths in traffic operations and control, transportation safety, transportation systems modeling and simulation, intelligent transportation systems, transportation infrastructure, and transportation system resilience. Faculty and researchers collaborate with federal, state, and local transportation agencies, as well as other public- and private-sector partners, on sponsored research addressing current and emerging transportation challenges. \u00a0\u00a0 College Information Join the College of Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno. We value faculty and staff as partners in our mission to deliver outstanding education, research and engagement. Our college seeks individuals committed to: \u00a0 Engaging in impactful outreach Providing state - of - the - art education Fostering respect, inclusiveness and diversity \u00a0 Nevada Engineering is a fast-growing college, with research expenditure that has doubled over the past nine years. Supported by an Engineering Research Office, the college recorded $24 million in expenditures in FY26 and offers professional development resources to ensure success. We offer bachelor\u2019s, graduate degrees and certificates through three schools: \u00a0 Civil, Chemical and Environmental Engineering Computer Science, Electrical, and Biomedical Engineering Aerospace, Industrial, Materials, and Mechanical Engineering \u00a0 Our specialized facilities include the Center for Civil Engineering Earthquake Research, Cybersecurity Center, the Nanofabrication Lab in the Davidson Foundation Cleanroom and the Nevada Water Innovation Institute. Located near the Tahoe-Reno Industrial Center and offering ready access to the bustling California economy, Nevada Engineering collaborates with leading tech firms and advance research in robotics, AI, battery technology, aerospace, water systems and sustainable energy. \u00a0 In Reno, you will find world-class outdoor recreation opportunities \u2014 including skiing, hiking, biking and water sports \u2014 in nearby Lake Tahoe and throughout the northern Nevada region. Learn more at unr.edu/engineering \u00a0 Contact Information Dr. Zong Tian at zongt@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . \u00a0 Please attach the following documents to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for three professional references 4)Research Statement \u00a0 This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. \u00a0 Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Chemical-Engineering_R0153713": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Chemical Engineering", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno is a land-grant university with a vision of building global expertise in research and education to support the nation\u2019s drive to create a robust supply chain of critical materials (CM). Nevada is taking on this challenge, striving to grow its CM industry across the full value chain. The region is experiencing substantial investments in the industry, and UNR leads the Nevada Tech Hub, with $21 million from the US Economic Development Administration to further catalyze the lithium economy locally. The School of Civil, Chemical, and Environmental Engineering plays a key role in Nevada\u2019s CM growth, and invites applications at the assistant professor level. The successful candidate will develop a nationally recognized, externally funded research program aligned with the chemical engineering challenges of the full CM supply chain, foster interdisciplinary collaborations, and contribute to undergraduate and graduate education in chemical engineering. The intended start date for the position is July 1, 2027.\u00a0\u00a0 Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Chemical Engineering or a closely related field is required, and the candidate must hold at least one (1) degree in chemical engineering. The candidate must possess expertise in chemical processing critical materials, broadly defined. Candidates are requested to describe their expertise in the cover letter how their research fits under the umbrella of CM. Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates whose work advances areas aligned with the DOE METALLC initiative, including processing of brines, leachates, and secondary feedstocks; solvent extraction, ion exchange, membranes, and other novel separation processes; electrochemical and thermochemical refining; process design, optimization, and intensification; technoeconomic and life-cycle analysis; and critical element recovery and recycling using molecular-based approaches. Applicants with experience applying machine learning tools in their research are especially encouraged. A demonstrated record in teaching is desired. Compensation Assistant Professor To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty (9-month). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program College of Engineering Join the College of Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno. We value faculty and staff as partners in our mission to deliver outstanding education, research and engagement. Our college seeks individuals committed to: \u00a0 Engaging in impactful outreach Providing state-of-the-art education Fostering respect, inclusiveness and diversity \u00a0 Nevada Engineering is a fast-growing college, with research expenditure that has doubled over the past nine years. The college\u00a0 recorded $24 million in expenditures in FY26 and offers professional development resources to ensure success. We offer bachelor\u2019s and graduate degrees and certificates through three schools: \u00a0 Civil, Chemical and Environmental Engineering Computing , Electrical and Biomedical Engineering Aerospace, Industrial, Materials\u00a0 and Mechanical Engineering \u00a0 Specialized facilities include the Center for Civil Engineering Earthquake Research, Cybersecurity Center, a Nanofabrication Lab and the Nevada Water Innovation Institute. Located near the Tahoe-Reno Industrial Center and offering ready access to the bustling California economy, Nevada Engineering collaborates with leading tech firms and advance research in robotics, AI, battery technology, aerospace, water systems and sustainable energy. In Reno, you will find world-class outdoor recreation opportunities \u2014 including skiing, hiking, biking and water sports \u2014 in nearby Lake Tahoe and throughout the northern Nevada region. Learn more at unr.edu/engineering \u00a0 School of Civil, Chemical, and Environmental Engineering (SCCEE) The School of Civil, Chemical and Environmental Engineering (CCEE) is a research-intensive school with around 650 BS, MS, and Ph.D. students, 29 tenured/tenure-track faculty, 1 lecturer, 7 research faculty, and various technical and administrative staff. The school offers ABET-accredited B.S. degree programs in Civil Engineering, Chemical Engineering and Environmental Engineering, as well as nationally-ranked graduate programs in these disciplines. In 2025, CCEE\u2019s programs reported more than $9 million in annual research expenditures funded by external grants. The school fosters an inclusive and collaborative environment and maintains strong partnerships with public agencies, industry and local employers that enhance research opportunities and career pathways for students. Contact Information Search Chair, Chuck Coronella at coronella@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu \u00a0 Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Research statement 4) Teach statement 5) Contact Information for three professional references Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by November 30, 2026. This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search is open until a hiring decision has been made. \u00a0 Posting Close Date 12/1/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Lecturer-I-IV--Teaching-Assistant-Professor--Teaching-Associate-Professor-or-Teaching-Professor--Mining-Engineering_R0153772": {"title": "Lecturer I-IV, Teaching Assistant Professor, Teaching Associate Professor or Teaching Professor, Mining Engineering", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Mining and Metallurgical Engineering (MME) at the University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) invites applications for a nine-month, non-tenure-track faculty position in Mining Engineering. The successful applicant will be appointed as Lecturer I, II, III, IV, Teaching Assistant Professor, Teaching Associate Professor or Teaching Professor, depending on the applicant\u2019s qualifications, terminal degree, and level of relevant professional experience. \u00a0 This position is designed for an individual with substantial industry experience who is eager to share their expertise with students and help prepare the next generation of mining professionals. We welcome applicants from across the mining industry, including mining operations and consulting firms. We encourage the following candidates to apply: \u00a0 Professionals with leadership experience in production, planning, operations, or mine management in surface and/or underground mining (metals, non-metals, or critical materials). Professionals from the aggregate and quarry sectors, particularly those with demonstrated expertise in production, operations, or management. Technical leaders in consultative fields, particularly those with demonstrated expertise in mine design, ventilation, technical studies, or emerging technologies. Professionals with demonstrated expertise in mining data analytics, mining data management, automation, or artificial intelligence. \u00a0 Key Responsibilities: Design and deliver undergraduate and graduate courses in mining engineering, ensuring that students develop both strong theoretical foundations and practical, industry-relevant skills. Mentor and advise students to support their career readiness and professional growth. Build strong connections between the department and the mining industry through guest lectures, site-based projects, and collaborative initiatives. Engage actively with professional organizations such as the Society for Mining, Metallurgy & Exploration (SME), the National Stone, Sand & Gravel Association (NSSGA), and similar organizations while encouraging student participation and industry visibility. Organize or contribute to industry-focused conferences, workshops, or summits, as appropriate. Support departmental outreach, workforce training, and applied research partnerships. Help prepare students for leadership roles in traditional mining and aggregate and quarry operations. \u00a0 Service: Serve on departmental, college, and university committees. Support the department\u2019s strategic initiatives, including program development, accreditation, and community outreach. Participate actively in relevant professional societies. Contribute to professional activities such as journal editorial work and peer review, as appropriate to the successful applicant\u2019s background and assigned rank. \u00a0 Lecturer Statement The University of Nevada, Reno reserves the right to hire at any level of Lecturer (I-IV) or Teaching Assistant/Associate/Professor (terminal degree candidates) that is appropriate for the hire.\u00a0 If you have questions about title, please inquire during the interview process. \u00a0 Required Qualifications Candidates will be hired to the appropriate level based on education and experience. Lecturer (I-IV): A master\u2019s degree in Mining Engineering or a closely related discipline. Teaching Assistant/Associate/Professor: A PhD in Mining Engineering or a closely related discipline. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Ten or more years of progressive industry experience in mining, with demonstrated expertise in production, operations, or management. Relevant experience may include aggregate and quarry mining or surface and underground mining operations. A Ph.D. in Mining Engineering or a closely related field. Evidence of leadership, mentoring, or workforce development within the mining sector. Active engagement in professional organizations such as SME, NSSGA, or similar organizations. Experience teaching, training, or mentoring students or industry professionals. Demonstrated ability to connect classroom instruction with real-world mining practices. \u00a0 Compensation Lecturer (I-IV) or Teaching Assistant/Associate/Professor (terminal degree candidates) \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty. \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 Department Information The University of Nevada, Reno, has a deep-rooted history in mining, dating back 150 years to the Comstock Lode era. The Department of Mining and Metallurgical Engineering, part of the Mackay School of Earth Sciences and Engineering, has played a central role in the university\u2019s success and Nevada\u2019s economic development. The state, historically known for its gold and silver production, is now a critical player in the sustainable resource sector, boasting seven of the largest lithium deposits in the United States. This positions UNR and the Mackay School at the forefront of modern mining, contributing to the nation's transition toward a sustainable, lithium-powered future. The department remains a hub for students and professionals seeking to advance their careers while shaping the future of responsible resource development. Please find more information here: https://www.unr.edu/mining Contact Information: For inquiries, please contact: Search Committee Chair: Gregory Stokes, gstokes@unr.edu Administrative Contact: Beth Jacobsen, (775) 682-8787, jacobsenb@unr.edu The department remains a hub for students and professionals seeking to advance their careers while shaping the future of responsible resource development. Please find more information here: https://www.unr.edu/mining Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note that once you submit your application, the only attachments viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachments to the cover letter, references, and other documents sections of the application will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachments will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments before the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV detailing professional achievements, industry roles, and, if applicable, academic, teaching or training experience 2) Cover Letter describing the applicant\u2019s qualifications, relevant industry experience, and interest in the position. 3) Contact information for three professional references. References will be contacted only for finalists. Applicants may also submit the following optional materials: 4) A statement of teaching philosophy describing how the applicant would integrate industry experience into teaching and mentorship. The statement may include examples of previous teaching or training experience, approaches to connecting classroom instruction with mining practice, plans for future courses, or student evaluations, if available. 5) Supporting documents demonstrating teaching ability, industry leadership, or other relevant professional contributions. This position is open until filled. Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. \u00a0 For full consideration, applications must be received by November 7th, 2026 Posting Close Date 01/12/2027 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Film-and-Media-Production_R0153776": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Film and Media Production", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Reynolds School of Journalism, renowned for national awards in diversity and innovation, invites applications for a creative and collaborative Assistant Professor of Film and Media Production. We seek an individual who will inspire students through teaching, creative work and/or research in film and media production while contributing to the school\u2019s forward-thinking and interdisciplinary initiatives. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in film and media production, focusing on areas such as visual storytelling, documentary filmmaking, media production techniques, digital media innovation, script writing, and related fields. They will also play a pivotal role in shaping our interdisciplinary programs through collaborations with initiatives such as the Mick Hitchcock, Ph.D., Project for Visualizing Science, the Noticiero M\u00f3vil bilingual news service, the Reynolds Media Lab, and sports media production with the university\u2019s athletic department. Candidates with expertise in editing, cinematography, documentary directing, or culturally inclusive media practices (e.g., Spanish-language media, Indigenous storytelling) are strongly encouraged to apply. This is a tenure-track, nine-month, academic-year appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor. Required Qualifications A terminal degree, e.g., Ph.D., MFA, or ABD. Professional experience in film and media production is highly valued, along with a creative agenda that advances the field and contributes to interdisciplinary grant opportunities. Compensation Grade Assistant Professor To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules \u00a0and select (9-month contract) Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University. \u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day. Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow! \u00a0All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada. \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The Reynolds School has built a reputation for curricular innovation, entrepreneurial learning and a collegial climate for both scholars and practitioners. We operate the Noticiero M\u00f3vil bilingual news service, the Center for Advanced Media Studies that supports faculty scholarship, the Reynolds Media Lab production center and the Mick Hitchcock, Ph.D., Project for Visualizing Science, an interdisciplinary initiative that reimagines how science is communicated. The NPR-member stations and digital outlets of KUNR Public Radio are part of the Reynolds School. Diversity, equity and inclusion are central to the mission of the School. Our faculty, staff and students strive to foster an environment that is conducive to exploring, engaging and expressing diverse perspectives and is respectful of diverse identities. To learn more about the Reynolds School, visit journalism.unr.edu The scenic campuses of the University of Nevada, Reno, a Carnegie Research One institution, are located in Reno and Incline Village, on the north shore of Lake Tahoe. The Reno/Tahoe region is a recreational paradise, chosen by Outside Magazine as one of the nation\u2019s Best Towns, with nearby world-class skiing and snowboarding, hiking, kayaking and mountain biking. The area is home to a burgeoning tech sector, with a vibrant food and arts scene. Contact Information Adem Ferati - aferati@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Please attach the following documents to your application\u00a0 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for three professional references \u00a0 This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Pavement-Engineering-and-Science_R0153714-1": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Pavement Engineering and Science", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno is seeking a collaborative and innovative scholar for tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Pavement Engineering and Science (PES). The successful candidate will strengthen the core of the University's Pavement Engineering and Science program while contributing to its continued growth and national impact. The successful candidate will join a well-established and nationally recognized pavement engineering program with a strong record of research, teaching, and engagement with transportation agencies and industry. The position offers outstanding opportunities to develop a complementary research program that builds upon existing strengths in Pavement Engineering and Science and advances asphalt materials and pavement technologies. The successful candidate will become part of a highly active and collaborative academic environment that fosters innovation, mentorship, interdisciplinary research, and professional growth. Position Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: Develop and sustain an externally funded research program in Pavement Engineering and Science, with an emphasis on asphalt materials and pavements. Conduct high-quality fundamental and applied research that advances the state of knowledge and addresses the needs of transportation agencies and industry. Publish research findings in leading peer-reviewed journals and disseminate scholarly work to academic, public agency, and industry audiences. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in areas aligned with the candidate's expertise and the instructional needs of the School. Advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students and supervise student research. Develop collaborative research partnerships within the University and with transportation agencies, industry partners, and other research organizations. Contribute to the academic and professional community through service to the School, College, University, and the profession. Participate in activities that support the continued development, visibility, and national impact of the University's Pavement Engineering and Science program. Required Qualifications B.S. or M.S. in Civil Engineering from an ABET accredited program or from an equivalent internationally accredited program. Ph.D. in Civil Engineering, with emphasis in Pavement Engineering and Science. Record of scholarly achievement appropriate with career stage. \u00a0 Schedule and Travel Requirements Full-time, tenure-track, nine-month academic faculty appointment based at the University of Nevada, Reno. Occasional travel for field research, sponsor and partner meetings, professional conferences, and other University activities. Laboratory and field activities may include work at pavement test sites, material production facilities, airports, and construction projects. Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates with research and teaching expertise in one or more of the following areas: Asphalt materials engineering, including advanced characterization of asphalt binders and mixtures, resource-responsible use of recycled materials, life cycle assessment, and enhancement of asphalt pavement durability and resilience. Asphalt pavement mechanics and design, including mechanistic-empirical analysis, structural design, performance prediction, and computational modeling of pavement systems. Non-destructive testing (NDT) and field evaluation, including application of NDT technologies, advanced sensing systems, network-level pavement condition assessment, and pavement preservation treatment selection. Emerging digital technologies for asphalt pavements, including integration of artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), digital twins, and advanced analytics for pavement monitoring, design, and management. Compensation Assistant Professor \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty (9-month). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program College of Engineering Join the College of Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno. We value faculty and staff as partners in our mission to deliver outstanding education, research and engagement. Our college seeks individuals committed to: \u00a0 Engaging in impactful outreach Providing state-of-the-art education Fostering respect, inclusiveness and diversity \u00a0 Nevada Engineering is a fast-growing college, with research expenditure that has doubled over the past nine years. The college\u00a0 recorded $24 million in expenditures in FY26 and offers professional development resources to ensure success. We offer bachelor\u2019s and graduate degrees and certificates through three schools: \u00a0 Civil, Chemical and Environmental Engineering Computing, Electrical, and Biomedical Engineering Aerospace, Industrial, Materials, and Mechanical Engineering Specialized facilities include the Center for Civil Engineering Earthquake Research, Cybersecurity Center, a Nanofabrication Lab and the Nevada Water Innovation Institute. Located near the Tahoe-Reno Industrial Center and offering ready access to the bustling California economy, Nevada Engineering collaborates with leading tech firms and advance research in robotics, AI, battery technology, aerospace, water systems and sustainable energy. In Reno, you will find world-class outdoor recreation opportunities \u2014 including skiing, hiking, biking and water sports \u2014 in nearby Lake Tahoe and throughout the northern Nevada region. Learn more at unr.edu/engineering \u00a0 \u00a0 School of Civil, Chemical, and Environmental Engineering (SCCEE) Shape the future and advance your career at the School of Civil, Chemical and Environmental Engineering at University of Nevada, Reno College of Engineering. With a faculty and staff team of more than 40, CCEE offers ABET-accredited undergraduate programs and nationally recognized graduate programs in civil, chemical, and environmental engineering. Faculty and students advance sustainable infrastructure, environmental stewardship, biotechnology, renewable energy, water resources, and earthquake resilience. Home to the Earthquake Engineering Laboratory and leading water research centers, CCEE offer strong connections to industry and access to world-class outdoor recreation. \u00a0 Learn more: School of Civil, Chemical, and Environmental Engineering \u00a0 |\u00a0 Western Regional Superpave Center \u00a0 Build on a nationally and internationally recognized pavement engineering program while helping define its future direction. The School of Civil, Chemical, and Environmental Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor with expertise in pavement engineering and science and a primary focus on asphalt materials and pavements with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. The successful candidate will join a collaborative team of pavement engineering faculty and contribute to a vibrant research program that routinely supports 15 to 20 graduate research assistants each year. The position provides opportunities to pursue high-impact research, teach and mentor graduate and undergraduate students, and collaborate across disciplines within the University as well as with transportation agencies, industry partners, and research organizations throughout the United States and internationally. \u00a0 The Pavement Engineering and Science Program The PES program is nationally and internationally recognized for research, education, and implementation in asphalt materials and pavements. The program currently includes three full-time faculty members, a laboratory manager, and approximately 15 to 20 graduate research assistants. The new faculty member will be expected to fit well with this established team, build on current strengths, develop complementary expertise, and help expand and grow the program. Contact Information Search Committee Chair, Elie Hajj at elieh@unr.edu \u00a0 Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu \u00a0 Attach the following attachments to your application 1)Cover letter describing interest in the position and relevant qualifications. 2)Curriculum vitae. 3)Research statement describing prior work, future directions, plans for external funding, and opportunities to complement and expand the PES program. 4)Teaching statement describing teaching interests, instructional approach, and plans for student mentorship. 5)Contact information for Five Professional References. Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by December 4, 2026. \u00a0 This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search is open until a hiring decision has been made. \u00a0 Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Evolutionary-Biology_R0153735": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Evolutionary Biology", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Biology at the University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Evolutionary Biology to join us starting July 1, 2027. We seek an evolutionary biologist whose research addresses fundamental questions about evolutionary processes in non-model organisms, and welcome applicants working across a broad range of questions, approaches, and organismal systems. Candidates conducting research that connects genomic, ecological, and/or organismal approaches to evolutionary questions are of particular interest. Research involving vertebrate systems is also of particular interest, although outstanding applicants working in any area of evolutionary biology who apply integrative approaches to non-model organisms are encouraged to apply. \u00a0 We are particularly interested in candidates whose research will complement and expand existing strengths in evolutionary biology within the Department of Biology and across the university,\u00a0while bringing new questions, approaches, or areas of expertise to our research community. Successful candidates will be expected to develop an externally funded, internationally recognized research program and to contribute to collaborative research and graduate training within the department. Biology faculty at UNR conduct research spanning evolution, ecology, behavior, genetics and genomics, cell and molecular biology, neuroscience, and organismal biology. The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate and graduate education and join a broad community of faculty and students working in evolutionary biology and related fields. Opportunities for interdisciplinary graduate training and collaboration are available through the Ecology, Evolution and Conservation Biology (EECB) graduate program, with additional opportunities for research, teaching, and collaboration through the UNR Museum of Natural History. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Biology, Evolutionary Biology, or a related field and relevant postdoctoral experience. Preferred Qualifications A strong publication record; Research experience that complements and expands the research capacity of the department; potential to develop a nationally competitive research program and potential to develop strong teaching skills Compensation Assistant Professor \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty (9-month). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 Department Information The Department of Biology has around 800 biology majors, 350 neuroscience majors, 80 graduate students, 24 tenure ladder faculty, and 11 teaching professors. Extramural awards average ~ $7 million per year. The department has access to state-of-the-art facilities, including a Cell and Molecular Imaging Core , Animal research facilities , and access to university cores such as Genomics , Proteomics , and Bioinformatics . Reno is located in Northern Nevada at the intersection of the Sierra Nevada and Great Basin, providing unparalleled access to diverse mountain, desert, and aquatic ecosystems, extensive public lands and natural areas, and field sites including nearby Lake Tahoe. Contact Information: Search Committee Chair: Thomas Parchman, tparchman@unr.edu Administrative Contact: Beth Jacobsen, (775) 682-8787, jacobsenb@unr.edu \u00a0 Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Statement of research interests and plans (limit three pages) 4) Statement of teaching interests, including a philosophy for pedagogy and mentorship, as well as potential courses the candidate could teach or develop (limit two pages) 5)Contact Information for three professional references Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by October 30, 2026. \u00a0 This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search is open until a hiring decision has been made. \u00a0 Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/Extension-Clark---University-of-Nevada-Reno/Assistant-Professor--Nutrition-Specialist--Las-Vegas--NV-_R0153536": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Nutrition Specialist (Las Vegas, NV)", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Nutrition within the College of Agriculture, Biotechnology and Natural Resources at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor, Nutrition Specialist position focused on improving nutritional health and well-being of school-age children and adolescents throughout Nevada.\u00a0 This is a full-time, 12-month appointment supported by both Extension and the Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, and reports to the Department of Nutrition Chair.\u00a0 This position is located in Las Vegas, NV. The Nutrition Specialist will serve communities across the state through innovative Extension programming, collaborative research, and engagement with diverse audiences dedicated to improving youth health outcomes. This position offers a unique opportunity to combine Extension teaching and program leadership, applied research, and community partnership to address critical public health challenges, including nutrition-related health disparities, food insecurity, and the social determinants of health that influence children and families. The ideal candidate will be a collaborative scholar who is passionate about improving the nutritional health of children and adolescents through innovative research, evidence-based Extension programming, and strong community partnerships. They will have the vision to build a nationally recognized program that addresses food insecurity, health disparities, and the social determinants of health while making a measurable impact on Nevada's youth and families. Position Overview The Nutrition Specialist will develop, deliver, and evaluate evidence-based educational programs and interventions that support healthy nutrition behaviors among children and adolescents. The successful candidate will build a nationally recognized program of scholarship that advances understanding of factors affecting youth nutrition and contributes to meaningful improvements in community health. Research and scholarly activities may include: Developing innovative nutrition interventions that produce measurable outcomes. Investigating dietary risk behaviors and factors that influence nutrition and health. Examining the impact of food insecurity and other social determinants of health on children and families. Exploring the perspectives and roles of community stakeholders, including parents, guardians, teachers, healthcare providers, and community organizations. Translating research findings into practical, community-based solutions that improve health outcomes. Key Responsibilities Extension Education, Policy, Programming, and Outreach Develop and implement evidence-based nutrition education programs for school-age youth, families, and community partners. Collaborate with schools, community organizations, healthcare professionals, and other stakeholders to address nutrition-related health concerns. Lead and support policy, systems, and environmental approaches to improve access to healthy foods and create sustainable conditions that support positive nutrition and health outcomes. Evaluate program effectiveness and demonstrate measurable impacts on participants and communities. Provide leadership in statewide nutrition programming and initiatives. Supervise, mentor, and support programmatic staff, students, and/or volunteers involved in nutrition education and outreach efforts; foster a collaborative, high-performing team environment that advances program goals and impacts. Travel throughout Nevada to support Extension programming and partnerships. Research and Scholarship Establish and sustain an impactful research agenda related to children's nutritional health and well-being. Secure extramural funding to support research and Extension activities. Publish scholarly work in peer-reviewed journals and disseminate findings through professional presentations and Extension publications. Integrate research findings into Extension programming to enhance community impact. Provide mentorship and academic guidance to graduate and undergraduate students, including participation on graduate committees, supervision of student research projects, and support for experiential learning opportunities aligned with Extension and research objectives. Service Contribute to university, Extension, professional, and community service activities. Participate in interdisciplinary collaborations that support the university's land-grant mission. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Nutrition, Public Health, Community Health, Health Promotion, Nutritional Sciences, Dietetics, Family and Consumer Sciences, or a related field by the time of appointment. Demonstrated knowledge of nutrition, health promotion, public health, or related disciplines. Evidence of, or strong potential for, developing a scholarly research program and publishing in peer-reviewed venues. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively with diverse audiences through teaching, presentations, outreach, or community engagement. This position requires candidates to complete a fingerprinting/background check. Preferred Qualifications Academic training, research, or professional experience in children's nutrition, adolescent health, public health nutrition, community nutrition, food security, health disparities, or related areas. Experience developing, implementing, or evaluating evidence-based nutrition education or health promotion programs. Record of scholarly productivity, including peer-reviewed publications, presentations, or other research dissemination activities. Experience obtaining or contributing to externally funded grants, contracts, or sponsored projects. Experience working collaboratively with community partners, schools, healthcare organizations, government agencies, or other stakeholder groups. Experience conducting community-engaged, translational, participatory, or applied research. Experience working with underserved, rural, culturally diverse, or historically marginalized populations. Experience partnering with schools, youth-serving organizations, healthcare systems, governmental agencies, or community-based organizations. Demonstrated ability to collect, analyze, and interpret quantitative and/or qualitative data to assess outcomes and impacts. Experience designing and delivering educational programming using in-person, online, and hybrid delivery methods. Experience supervising professional staff, student employees, volunteers, or multidisciplinary teams. Experience integrating student learning opportunities into community-engaged research and Extension programming. Registered Dietitian Nutritionist (RDN) credential or eligibility for registration. Bilingual in English and Spanish Familiarity with the Cooperative Extension System or similar outreach and engagement models. Licensure A valid Class \u201cC\u201d driver\u2019s license or higher operator\u2019s license within 30 days of appointment. Schedule and Travel This position requires the ability and willingness to travel throughout Nevada and work flexible hours, including evening and weekends, as needed to meet statewide Extension programming responsibilities. Compensation Assistant Professor (Tenure track) To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit Salary Schedules and select (12-month contract) Academic Faculty. Perks of Working at UNR \u00a0 Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous annual, sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefits Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada.\u00a0 Dual Career Assistance Program College/Department Information The Department of Nutrition is one of four academic Departments within CABNR.\u00a0It offers both undergraduate and graduate degrees and two accredited Dietetic Programs.\u00a0Faculty include internationally renowned nutrition scholars and award-winning teachers. We value collaboration and are committed to an inclusive and equitable learning community that fosters the success of all. https://www.unr.edu/nutrition UNR Extension has a long-standing commitment to providing practical, research-based education that improves lives and strengthens communities throughout Nevada. As part of Extension, you will have the opportunity to: Make a direct impact on the health and well-being of children and families across Nevada. Develop innovative, community-engaged research with real-world application. Build meaningful partnerships with schools, healthcare providers, and community organizations. Contribute to a statewide and national Extension system dedicated to improving lives through education and engagement. Live and work in the dynamic and diverse Las Vegas community while serving communities across Nevada. To learn more about Extension, please visit: https://extension.unr.edu/default.aspx Search Contacts Search Coordinator - Lindsey Barnes, lindseybarnes@unr.edu Hiring Manager - Dr. Bradley Ferguson, Dept of Nutrition Chair, bferguson@unr.edu \u00a0 Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note that once you submit your application, the only attachments viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachments to the cover letter, references, and other documents sections of the application will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachments will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments before the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter: Please submit a cover letter describing your educational background, relevant professional experience, research and Extension interests, and qualifications for the position. 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References 4) Vision Statement (1-page maximum): Please submit a one-page statement describing your vision of an integrated Extension and research program focused on improving the nutritional health and well-being of children and adolescents. Applicants are encouraged to discuss potential areas of focus, approaches to addressing food insecurity, health disparities, and social determinants of health, opportunities for community engagement and collaboration, and the anticipated impact of their work on Nevada\u2019s youth, families and communities. Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by October 30, 2026 . This posting is open until filled. Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Associate-Full-Professor-Basque-Studies_R0153649": {"title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Basque Studies", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Center for Basque Studies at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a tenure-track or tenured faculty position in Basque Studies, open rank (Assistant (II), Associate (III), or Full (IV) Professor), to begin July 1, 2027. The field of specialization is open. We welcome applications from scholars in any humanities, arts, or social science discipline whose research and teaching engage the Basque Country, its languages and cultures, or the global Basque diaspora. The successful candidate will hold a tenure home in a College of Liberal Arts department appropriate to their discipline including Anthropology; Art, Art History and Design; Communication Studies; English; History; Music, Philosophy; Political Science; Sociology and Cultural Analysis; Theatre and Dance; or World Languages and Literatures. The candidate will also contribute to the interdisciplinary research, teaching, and public-facing mission of the Center for Basque Studies, including its Ph.D. program in Basque Studies. Basque Studies at Nevada is broadly interdisciplinary, spanning history, anthropology, literature, linguistics, political science, geography, art, sociology, and cultural analysis. We are especially interested in candidates whose scholarship builds bridges across fields and speaks to national and international audiences. The faculty member who joins the Center can expect to teach and mentor students in the Ph.D. in Basque Studies and in their home department\u2019s undergraduate and graduate programs. The standard teaching load is 2-2, with substantial involvement in graduate education, including graduate-level teaching, student mentoring and advising, and service as chair or member of graduate committees. Faculty affiliated with Basque Studies contribute to Center for Basque Studies conferences and publications, participate in its full range of public outreach and programming, and should expect to perform service and administrative roles appropriate to the stage of their career development. The rank and tenure status of the appointment will be commensurate with the successful candidate\u2019s record and qualifications. Early-career scholars, established mid-career faculty, and senior scholars are all encouraged to apply. Required Qualifications A Ph.D. in a relevant humanities or social science discipline or a similar terminal degree in the Arts. For appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor, the degree must be completed no later than July 1, 2027. Candidates for appointment at the rank of Associate or Full Professor must hold the Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree in the discipline and present a record of scholarship, teaching, and service commensurate with tenure at that rank. An active and promising research agenda that engages Basque Studies\u2014the language, cultures, history, society, arts, or diaspora of the Basque people\u2014broadly conceived. Qualifications sufficient to hold a tenure home in an appropriate department within the College of Liberal Arts. Demonstrated excellence, or clear promise of excellence, in teaching, research, and service. Reading, writing, or speaking knowledge of Euskara and/or Spanish or French. Preferred Qualifications A record of, or clear potential for, interdisciplinary and international scholarly collaboration. Experience with, or commitment to, graduate mentoring and doctoral education. A record of securing, or strong potential to secure, external research funding. Experience engaging public, community, or diaspora audiences. Scholarly strengths that complement and extend existing expertise at the Center and across the College of Liberal Arts\u2014for example, in comparative and transnational approaches applied to a range of disciplines, linguistics, digital or public humanities, environmental arts and humanities, cinema and media studies, gender and sexuality studies, migration and diaspora studies. Compensation Compensation is competitive and commensurate with rank, education, and experience, consistent with the appropriate faculty compensation grade within the Nevada System of Higher Education. This is a 9-month, \u201cB\u201d contract appointment. The salary schedule can be found here: https://www.unr.edu/hr/compensation-evaluation/salary-schedules under \u201cAcademic Faculty (nine-month contract) salaries, with salaries ranging typically from Q1 to Q2 for each rank. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University. With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day. Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow! All permanent employees on an annual \u201cA\u201d or \u201cB\u201d contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program. Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision \u2013 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options. E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life\u2019s difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner. Faculty Grant-in-Aid . Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Center Information The Center for Basque Studies, housed in the College of Liberal Arts, is the leading research and educational institute of its kind outside the European Basque homeland. Established in 1967, the Center advances interdisciplinary research on Basque history, language, culture, politics, and the global Basque diaspora, and disseminates that scholarship to local, national, and international audiences. The Center offers a Ph.D. in Basque Studies with specialization across disciplines including anthropology, world languages and literatures, geography, political science, English, and history. It is home to the Center for Basque Studies Press and maintains one of the largest Basque collections in the world outside the Basque Country\u2014approximately 55,000 volumes\u2014in the Jon Bilbao Basque Library within the Mathewson-IGT Knowledge Center. The Center sustains active partnerships with the Basque Government, Etxepare Euskal Institutua, the University Studies Abroad Consortium, and universities across the Basque Country, Europe, and the Americas. Additional information about the Center can be found at https://www.unr.edu/basque-studies. College and University Information As Nevada\u2019s flagship institution, the University of Nevada, Reno is a public Research 1 land-grant institution of the Nevada System of Higher Education with a student population of approximately 21,000, including over 3,000 graduate students with a campus also at Lake Tahoe . We are a comprehensive, doctoral university that has been designated as a Carnegie R1 institution with very high research activity and a Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Nearly half of our students are students of color, and we are considered a Hispanic Serving Institution. In accordance with the University\u2019s land grant tier-one research institution mission, our faculty are expected to contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community through research, teaching, and service in a pluralistic environment. The University has a dynamic intellectual, artistic, and sports life, and a close relationship to the city of Reno. Located in one of the nation\u2019s fastest-growing innovative economies, Reno is home to a thriving technology ecosystem, attracting leading companies in advanced manufacturing, clean energy, logistics, artificial intelligence, and data infrastructure. It is a growing tech and innovation hub with a diverse population and a rich cultural history that hosts an active art, music, and festival scene. Known for its proximity to Lake Tahoe and the Sierra Nevada mountains, Reno is a vibrant, diverse, and growing city located on the eastern slope of the Sierra Nevada, about 200 miles east of San Francisco, about 100 miles east of Sacramento, and 30 miles north of the state capital, Carson City. Combined with the neighboring city of Sparks, the Reno-Sparks metropolitan area has a population of more than 500,000. Reno is only 30\u201345 minutes from Lake Tahoe and major ski resorts and is rated one of the top locations in the United States for living and outdoor recreation. It has an international airport with direct flights to many U.S. transportation hubs and to Mexico. For more information about the city and the surrounding area, please visit www.cityofreno.com and www.visitrenotahoe.com . The College of Liberal Arts is home to the humanities, social sciences, and arts, offering 13 academic units and 8 additional programs, museums, and centers, including the Center for Constitutional Law and the Center for Basque Studies, that foster creativity, critical thinking, and engaged citizenship. As one of the largest academic units on campus with just under 4,000 students and 240 faculty, the College combines a strong liberal arts tradition with innovative and interdisciplinary teaching, research, and community engagement, preparing students for meaningful careers and lifelong learning in an interconnected world. The College emphasizes inquiry, discovery, and openness to new ideas and perspectives and supports major scholarly research and creative activity across its disciplines and offers a number of programs at the Lake Tahoe campus. Visit www.unr.edu/liberal-arts \u00a0 for more information about the college, faculty, and students. Contact Information for this position Search Chair: Jennifer (Jen) Hill, Director, Center for Basque Studies \u2014 jmhill@unr.edu Search Coordinator: Kate Camino -- kcamino@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, or additional documents sections of the application will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu. Please attach the following documents to your application: Curriculum Vitae Cover letter (including an overview of research plans, teaching experience, the proposed College of Liberal Arts tenure home, and planned contributions to the Center\u2019s and College\u2019s missions) Contact information for three professional references Research Statement (500 words) Teaching Statement (500 words) Writing sample (up to 7,000 words) **Finalists will be asked to share recent teaching evaluations. This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Applications received by November 1, 2026 will receive full consideration. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Professor-Chair--Department-of-Chemistry_R0153718": {"title": "Professor/Chair, Department of Chemistry", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) College of Science invites applications for the position of Chair, Department of Chemistry, at the rank of Professor, a tenured position, with an anticipated target hire date of July 1, 2027 . Exceptional candidates at the Associate Professor rank will also be considered. T he Department and College seek a dynamic, collaborative and visionary leader to serve as Chair to advance the Department of Chemistry\u2019s research, education and service missions. The Department is one of 10 departments in the College of Science, together with the College\u2019s public service programs, and plays a foundational role in research and education across the University. The Department includes two dozen academic and administrative faculty, whose expertise spans analytical, bio-organic, computational, inorganic, materials, organic, physical and theoretical chemistry. The Department offers a highly reputable B.S. in Chemistry with multiple areas of emphasis, and has maintained continuous American Chemical Society (ACS) certification status since 1941. It also offers M.S. and Ph.D. degrees in Chemistry, and participates with the Department of Physics in the interdisciplinary Ph.D. program in Chemical Physics. The Chemistry graduate programs support approximately 65-75 graduate students working with research-active faculty. Undergraduate and graduate students benefit from extensive opportunities for faculty-mentored research and access to advanced research infrastructure. Departmental research is supported by extramural grants from federal and private agencies, with over $10M in research funding in FY25-26. Research spans experimental and theoretical chemistry, and is supported by extensive shared instrumentation in the Shared Instrumentation Laboratory (SIL), including NMR spectrometry, X-ray diffraction, mass spectrometry, optical and laser spectroscopy, and materials characterization capabilities. The Department also operates ChemStores, a research stockroom that provides chemicals, supplies, and related resources to researchers across campus. The Department has a strong tradition of interdisciplinary research, including the Hitchcock Center for Chemical Ecology, which brings together investigators from chemistry, biology, mathematics, biochemistry, natural resources and environmental science. The interdisciplinary Ph.D. program in Chemical Physics provides an additional bridge between Chemistry and Physics. Together, these activities provide a strong foundation for expanding collaborative research across the College of Science, the University, and with external partners. The Chair will work collaboratively with faculty, staff, students, the Dean and College leadership to establish and implement a forward-looking vision for the Department. The Chair will strengthen research excellence and extramural funding, advance undergraduate and graduate education, recruit and retain outstanding faculty and students, strategically manage departmental resources and infrastructure. The Chair is expected to maintain an innovative, externally funded research program, with competitive startup support available. The Chair will help position Chemistry as a leader in interdisciplinary research and education and enhance the Department's visibility and impact within Nevada and nationally. A successful Chair will be expected to address a set of key opportunities and responsibilities, including: Develop and implement a strategic vision for the Department. Strengthen the Department's research enterprise. Promote interdisciplinary collaboration across the College of Science and University. Recruit, retain, mentor and support outstanding faculty and staff. Sustain and advance excellence in undergraduate education. Strengthen graduate education and training. Expand opportunities for undergraduate research and experiential learning. Support strategic management and development of research and instructional infrastructure. Advance a strong culture of laboratory safety and responsible research. Develop and support effective mentoring and professional-development programs. Oversee and strategically manage the Department's budget, personnel, space and other resources. Advance external engagement and resource development by working with College and University leadership. Enhance the visibility and reputation of the Department. Foster a collegial and inclusive academic environment. Align departmental priorities with the College of Science Strategic Plan and UNR's strategic initiatives. Contribute to the academic mission of the Department through teaching, research and/or scholarly engagement appropriate to the successful candidate's expertise. Required Qualifications Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Chemistry or a related field. Eligible for tenure upon arrival . Demonstrate skills in strategic planning and administration. Candidates should have demonstrated excellence in research, teaching and mentoring in chemistry or related fields. Candidates currently at the rank of Associate Professor are encouraged to apply, provided they can demonstrate a record of accomplishment sufficient to support appointment at the rank of Professor upon hire. Preferred Qualifications The University of Nevada (UNR), Reno will use a holistic review process that considers candidates' experiences, accomplishments, leadership attributes, competencies, and scholarly record to identify a strong and diverse pool of qualified applicants. Ideal candidates for the Chair of Chemistry will demonstrate aptitude in the following areas: Vision and values aligned with UNR, COS, and the Department of Chemistry. Academic leadership; prior experience as an academic leader is desirable. Teaching, curricular development, mentoring, and faculty development Relationship building, collaborative leadership, and team development. Growth of research and scholarly activity, including securing extramural, private and donor funding Interdisciplinary collaboration. Commitment to inclusive excellence and the ability to foster a welcoming and collegial academic environment. Professionalism and communication/interpersonal skills Entrepreneurship and innovation. Experience overseeing research infrastructure, laboratory operations, safety, compliance, and other regulatory requirements Ability to represent and advocate for the Department effectively within COS, UNR and with external stakeholders. Compensation Grade Professor \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select (9-month contract) Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The Department of Chemistry in the College of Science at the University of Nevada, Reno, offers B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. degrees in Chemistry. The department has 20 full-time academic faculty (18 tenure-stream and 2 non-tenure-track instructional), 3 administrative faculty, and 7 classified personnel, and is housed in a four-story building with state-of-the-art research facilities. Please find more information here: https://www.unr.edu/chemistry Contact Information Search Committee Chair: Sander Van Der Linden, avanderlinden@unr.edu Administrative Contact: Beth Jacobsen, (775) 682-8787, jacobsenb@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application: 1) Academic CV 2) Letter of Application - (Cover Letter) describing your interest in the position; highlighting relevant academic leadership, research, teaching, and administrative experience; and outlining your vision for advancing the Department of Chemistry. The letter should address opportunities to strengthen research and external funding, undergraduate and graduate education, faculty and student success, interdisciplinary collaboration, and departmental operations. 3) Research Statement - Summarizing your major research accomplishments and future scholarly directions, including how you envision maintaining an active research program and contributing to the broader research mission of the Department, College, and University while serving as Chair. (Four pages maximum) 4) Teaching Statement/Philosophy - Describing your approach to teaching mentoring, and curricular development, including your experience supporting undergraduate and graduate education and student success. (Two pages maximum) 5) Contact information for at least Three Professional References - Please include name, title, company/organization, email address and phone number. Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by November 9, 2026. \u00a0 This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. \u00a0 Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Teaching-Assistant-Professor--Psychology_R0153563": {"title": "Teaching Assistant Professor, Psychology", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Psychology at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a nine-month, continuing, non-tenure-track Teaching Assistant Professor position, with an anticipated start date of August 2027. This state-funded position is part of our commitment to excellence in undergraduate education. The successful candidate will demonstrate excellence in teaching, a commitment to student success, and the ability to contribute to undergraduate program development and administration of UNR\u2019s interdisciplinary BS degree program in Neuroscience, which is jointly offered through the Departments of Psychology and Biology for over 300 student majors. Primary responsibilities include teaching undergraduate neuroscience courses (in Behavioral, Computational and Cognitive Neuroscience), including developing and mounting a new proposed laboratory course in Human Cognitive Neuroscience, advising undergraduate students, and serving as Co-Director of the undergraduate Neuroscience degree program. The teaching load is four to five courses per academic year (depending on administrative and advising service roles). There are also opportunities to teach during winter and summer sessions for additional compensation. Graduate teaching assistant support is available for larger courses. The University provides a clear promotion pathway for teaching faculty that includes regular review, and base salary increases upon promotion. Lecturer Statement The University of Nevada, Reno reserves the right to hire at any level of Lecturer (I-IV) or Teaching Assistant/Associate/Professor (terminal degree candidates) that is appropriate for the hire.\u00a0 If you have questions about title, please inquire during the interview process. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Neuroscience, Psychology, Cognitive Science, or a closely related field, with award of the degree before the start date of the hire; Ability to teach a range of undergraduate courses in Behavioral, Computational and Cognitive Neuroscience including lab-based courses; commitment to student advising, mentoring, and academic success; Ability to work collaboratively with faculty across multiple departments to support the undergraduate Neuroscience program. Preferred Qualifications Experience advising and mentoring undergraduate students; experience with undergraduate curriculum development, assessment, or academic program administration; Experience coordinating or contributing to interdisciplinary academic programs; Experience with basic methods in Cognitive and Computational Neuroscience (e.g. psychophysics, neuroimaging, modeling); Strong quantitative and coding skills. Compensation Grade Teaching Assistant Professor, (9-month; B-Contract) Salary is competitive with a hiring range of $63,000 to $96,900 and commensurate with related education and experience. To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty. Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The successful candidate will join a supportive and collegial department, with over 30 teaching and research faculty, more than 1000 Psychology majors, and more than 100 graduate students in programs including Cognitive and Brain Sciences and the interdisciplinary graduate program in Integrative Neuroscience. The department is also the administrative home of UNR\u2019s Center for Integrative Neuroscience (an NIH COBRE grant), which provides an array of shared facilities for computational modeling and neuroimaging. Please visit the departmental home page at\u00a0 https://www.unr.edu/psychology for more information about the department, the neuroscience home page https://www.unr.edu/neuroscience to learn about the various neuroscience programs at UNR, and the university website https://www.unr.edu/academics for information about the campus. Contact Information Beth Jacobsen at 775-682-8787 or jacobsenb@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application\u00a0 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Statement of teaching experience, including evidence of teaching effectiveness and commitment to student success (1-page max) 4) Statement describing experience with student advising, mentoring, and undergraduate program development or administration (1-page max) 5) Statement describing research experience and areas of expertise (1-page max) 6) Vision statement for a neuroscience undergraduate degree program, including targeted student cohort, curricular elements and experiences, core competencies and targeted career opportunities gained through the degree (2 pages max) 7) Contact Information for at least three professional references Posting Close Time This posting will close at 12:00 am on the date listed below. The posting will no longer be available to apply to after 11:59 pm the day prior. Posting Close Date 12/16/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Hydrologic-Sciences_R0153587": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Hydrologic Sciences", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Geological Sciences and Engineering (DGSE) at the University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) invites applications for a full-time, 9-month contract tenure-track faculty position in the hydrologic sciences, broadly defined, which could include a combination of groundwater hydrology (hydrogeology), hydrogeophysics, aqueous geochemistry, geothermal applications, mining hydrology, surface water hydrology, hydraulic engineering and civil engineering that focus on interactions with the natural environment,\u00a0 or other related fields.\u00a0 The position is at the rank of Assistant Professor, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. Applicants must have a PhD at the time of appointment and demonstrate commitment to both undergraduate and graduate instruction as well as development and vision of an externally-funded program of research in their specialty. Given the pressing needs related to water that our society and Nevada currently face, we expect the hired faculty member will perform both basic and applied research. Building on strengths in field and lab-based teaching and research, the undergraduate and graduate programs offered by DGSE aim to train students to solve societally relevant problems from multi-disciplinary perspectives and to be technically proficient geoscientists for diverse industries. The applicant\u2019s resume should reflect the ability to strengthen our department, which combines the geosciences and engineering and actively participates in the interdisciplinary Graduate Program of Hydrological Sciences. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Geological Sciences, Geological Engineering or a closely related field by May 30, 2027 . Compensation Grade Assistant Professor \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select (9-month contract) Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. \u00a0 \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 Department Information The Department of Geological Sciences and Engineering. is part of the dynamic Nevada Geosciences community housed within the Mackay School of Earth Sciences and Engineering (MSESE) within the College of Science. Nevada Geosciences also includes two state-funded Earth Science units, the Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology and the Nevada Seismological Laboratory that participate in the Department\u2019s teaching mission and graduate programs. Many faculty participate in the interdisciplinary Graduate Program of Hydrologic Sciences . We offer BS degrees in Geology , Geophysics , Hydrogeology and Geological Engineering . We offer MS degrees in Geology, Geophysics, and Geological Engineering, and PhDs in Geology, Geophysics and Mineral Resource Engineering (joint with Mining). More information about the Department can be found at\u00a0 http://www.unr.edu/geology and the broader Nevada Geosciences program at https://www.unr.edu/nevada-geosciences .\u00a0 Contact Information Search Committee Chair: Scott McCoy at scottmccoy@unr.edu Administrative Contact: Beth Jacobsen at (775) 682-8787, jacobsenb@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application\u00a0 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter - Please include in your application letter a description of what you need to be successful in terms of research infrastructure and support and highlight why you want to join University of Nevada. 3) Statement of Research Interests (3 pages maximum) 4) Teaching Philosophy - Make sure to describe which classes in the current catalog would be of interest, as well as new classes you would hope to develop and your plans for reaching the diverse student audience at the University of Nevada (2 pages maximum) 5) Contact Information for three professional references Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by November 1, 2026. This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Associate-Professor-or-Full-Professor--Economic-Geology-and-Director-of-the-Ralph-J-Roberts-Center-for-Research-in-Economic-Geology--CREG-_R0153596": {"title": "Associate Professor or Full Professor, Economic Geology and Director of the Ralph J. Roberts Center for Research in Economic Geology (CREG)", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology (NBMG) at the University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) seeks a renowned individual for a tenure-track faculty position at the Associate Professor or Full Professor level to lead our Economic Geology program and become the new Director of the Ralph J. Roberts Center for Research in Economic Geology.\u00a0 We are looking for individuals with field and lab experience, including research\u00a0studies\u00a0related to\u00a0the genesis, transport, and distribution of metals in the crust, the formation of precious, base, and/or critical mineral and metal deposits, fluid-rock interaction and the circulation of fluids, magmatic and hydrothermal processes, and/or exploration methods. Research must integrate one or more of the following disciplines: economic geology, structural geology, igneous petrology, and geochemistry.\u00a0 Responsibilities for this position will include: 1) Managing the industry-funded, Ralph J. Roberts Center for Research in Economic Geology (CREG); 2) Research on mineral deposits, focusing on the western US and Nevada\u2019s enormous mineral wealth; 3) Education via supervision of graduate students and teaching courses in Economic Geology; 4) Serving as a spokesperson for the Economic Geology program at NBMG-UNR, requiring effective communication with the public and community leaders regarding the geology of Nevada and its mineral resources; 5) Working independently as well as collaborating with NBMG faculty-staff, other geoscience units at UNR and in the region, and others in industry and government in developing funded projects and conducting research; 6) Contributing to the development of datasets and reports on Nevada\u2019s mineral resources; and 7) Public outreach and service within the University and community.\u00a0 The position will be a tenure-track Associate or Full Professor faculty appointment with an academic-year base salary (9 months) that is competitive with other research universities.\u00a0 Starting date will be July 1, 2027, or shortly thereafter, depending on availability of the successful candidate. Required Qualifications Interested applicants must have a Doctorate in Geology or a related geoscience field and a demonstrated record of research on topics associated with mineral deposits as indicated by peer-reviewed publications and/or industry-related reports.\u00a0 Excellent communication skills, as demonstrated in written application materials; public service; potential for, or established record of publications; and ability to attract funding are essential. Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates with: Academic or industry experience in the analysis of mineral deposits, particularly research that combines quantitative analytical methods with field-based approaches. A clear and achievable plan for securing research funding focused on Nevada-related topics in economic geology, as described in the applicant\u2019s letter of interest. Additional preferred fields of expertise include: Precious metals Critical minerals Base metals Magmatic and hydrothermal processes Geochemistry Compensation Grade Associate Professor Full Professor \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select (9-month contract) Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 Department Information NBMG is a research and public service unit of UNR and serves as the state geological survey of Nevada.\u00a0 Managed as part of the Mackay School of Earth Sciences and Engineering in the College of Science at UNR, NBMG functions as both an academic unit and statewide agency.\u00a0 Its principal scientists are tenure-track faculty members.\u00a0 Nevada is one of the most exciting regions in the world to conduct research in the geosciences and one of the best in the U.S. for the study of mineral deposits.\u00a0 The successful applicant will have access to many state-of-the-art facilities at UNR, including a new electron microprobe, two SEMs, new fluid-inclusion and Raman facilities, and laser ablation ICP-MS.\u00a0 For further information about NBMG, please consult our website: http://www.nbmg.unr.edu \u00a0\u00a0 Contact Information Beth Jacobsen at 775-682-8787 or jacobsenb@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu \u00a0 Attach the following attachments to your application: 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Five Professional References 4) Statement of research interests and plans (limit three pages) 5) Statement of teaching interests (limit two pages) 6) Electronic copies of up to three (3) of your publications/reports Posting Close Time This posting will close at 12:00 am on the date listed below. The posting will no longer be available to apply to after 11:59 pm the day prior. \u00a0 Posting Close Date 11/4/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Educational-Studies--Instructional-Design--Technology--and-Quantitative-Methods-Focus-_R0153407-1": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Educational Studies (Instructional Design, Technology, and Quantitative Methods Focus) (Tenure Track)", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Educational Studies at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for an Assistant Professor position, beginning July 1, 2027. We seek an individual with expertise in instructional technology, learning design, and quantitative methods. The selected individual will develop an active research and publication agenda, teach undergraduate and graduate courses, provide advising support to graduate students, supervise doctoral students and dissertations, seek external funding, and provide service to the department, college, university, and the profession. This position requires an on-campus presence and is not possible to be performed remotely. Required Qualifications An earned doctorate in education or a related field by August 2027.\u00a0 Expertise that supports teaching in one or more of the following: data analytics for educational research, educational measurement and assessment, instructional design and technology, and AI in education. Evidence of scholarly productivity or potential for conducting rigorous educational research using quantitative methods. Evidence of, or potential for, effective graduate-level teaching across in-person, hybrid, and online modalities. Preferred Qualifications While areas of research and teaching are open, the search committee will prioritize candidates who have: Training and expertise in instructional design, educational technology, educational psychology, computer science/informatics, and/or cognitive science.\u00a0 Experience mentoring and advising graduate students. Experience as a teacher or administrator in school settings. Experience in curriculum development. A sustainable research agenda with demonstrated success or pursuit of external funding. Compensation Grade Assistant Professor ($80,000 - $85,000) To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits but, in order to be eligible, children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada.\u00a0 Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information https://www.unr.edu/education \u00a0 Contact Information Please direct questions to the search chair, Dr. Li-Ting Chen ( litingc@unr.edu ) Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application\u00a0 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Review of applications will begin on October 16, 2026. Posting Close Date 10/31/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Mining-and-Metallurgical-Engineering_R0153556": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Mining Engineering", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Mining and Metallurgical Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) invites applications for two full-time, 9-month contract tenure-track faculty positions in Mining Engineering at the rank of Assistant Professor, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. We seek dynamic and innovative candidates committed to excellence in teaching, research, and service, who will contribute to our department\u2019s mission of advancing knowledge in the mining field. Key Responsibilities Teaching: Develop and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in mine design, mining automation, mine safety, Ventilation, blasting, and general mining engineering. Mentor and advise undergraduate and graduate students. Contribute to curriculum development and innovative teaching methodologies, including online or hybrid course delivery. Research: Establish a strong, externally funded research program addressing critical challenges in the mining industry. Research areas may include, but are not limited to: Mine automation and data analytics Mine ventilation and safety Drilling, blasting, and explosives engineering Resource and reserve estimation Other related mining engineering topics Engage in interdisciplinary collaborations with other departments, research institutes, and industry partners. Secure research grants and publish findings in high-impact, peer-reviewed journals. Service: Serve on departmental, college, and university committees. Support the department\u2019s strategic initiatives, including program development, accreditation, and community outreach. Actively participate in professional societies such as the Society for Mining, Metallurgy & Exploration (SME). Contribute to journal editorial and peer-review processes. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Mining Engineering or a closely related field by the start date of the appointment. Preferred Qualifications Experience in mining automation, data analytics, and related digital technologies. A strong research record, demonstrated through peer-reviewed publications and successful acquisition of external funding. Experience in interdisciplinary research and industry collaborations. Professional experience in the mining industry; Professional Engineer (PE) licensure is a plus. Demonstrated excellence in teaching with experience in curriculum development and innovative instructional methods. Active engagement in professional organizations such as SME. Commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion in teaching, research, and service. Compensation Grade Assistant Professor To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select (9-month contract) Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The University of Nevada, Reno, has a deep-rooted history in mining, dating back 150 years to the Comstock Lode era. The Department of Mining and Metallurgical Engineering, part of the Mackay School of Earth Sciences and Engineering, has played a central role in the university\u2019s success and Nevada\u2019s economic development. The state, historically known for its gold and silver production, is now a critical player in the sustainable resource sector, boasting seven of the largest lithium deposits in the United States. This positions UNR and the Mackay School at the forefront of modern mining, contributing to the nation's transition toward a sustainable, lithium-powered future. The department remains a hub for students and professionals seeking to advance their careers while shaping the future of responsible resource development. See here for more information: https://www.unr.edu/mining Contact Information Search Committee Chair: Dr. Behrooz Abbasi at ba@unr.edu Administrative Contact: Beth Jacobsen at (775) 682-8787; jacobsenb@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu \u00a0 Attach the following attachments to your application\u00a0 1) Resume/CV containing a complete list of publications 2) Cover Letter detailing qualifications and interest in the position 3) Statement of teaching philosophy (up to 3 pages maximum) 4) Statement of research interests and future plans (up to 5 pages maximum) 5) Names and contact Information for at least three professional references Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by October 15, 2026. Posting Close Time This posting will close at 12:00 am on the date listed below. The posting will no longer be available to apply to after 11:59 pm the day prior. Posting Close Date 12/31/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Scholar--Electrochemistry-Electrochemical-Engineering_R0153425": {"title": "Postdoctoral Scholar, Electrochemistry/Electrochemical Engineering", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno is seeking a motivated and innovative Postdoctoral Scholar to join its College of Engineering. This position is available immediately and offers an exciting opportunity to work at the forefront of electrochemistry/electrochemical engineering. The successful candidate will contribute to cutting-edge research in areas such as electrochemical systems, electrocatalysis, electrolysis, and energy storage and conversion. In this role, you will conduct high-impact research, publish in leading journals, present findings at conferences, and collaborate with a dynamic team of faculty and students. You will also have opportunities to mentor students and contribute to proposal development, supporting the growth of a strong and externally funded research program. Candidates should have demonstrated experience in electrochemical techniques with a strong background in electrochemistry or electrochemical engineering. Additional experience with materials synthesis, or related experimental and modeling approaches will be considered a strong advantage. Required Qualifications PhD in Chemical Engineering, Materials Science, Mechanical Engineering, Chemistry, Electrochemical Engineering, or a closely related field. Completion of a doctoral degree in the appropriate discipline is required. The doctoral degree must have been completed within the five years immediately preceding the first date of appointment as a postdoctoral fellow at the University. The individual cannot have held previous positions in the professional ranks. Compensation Grade Postdoctoral Scholar To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules . Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Department Information Join the College of Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno. We value faculty and staff as partners in our mission to deliver outstanding education, research and engagement. Our college seeks individuals committed to:\u00a0 Engaging in impactful outreach\u00a0 Providing state-of-the-art education\u00a0 Fostering respect, inclusiveness and diversity\u00a0 Nevada Engineering is a fast-growing college, with research expenditures that have doubled over the past eight years. Supported by an Engineering Research Office, the college secured $27.9 million in research awards in FY25 and offers professional development resources to ensure success. We offer bachelor\u2019s, graduate degrees and certificates through three schools:\u00a0 Civil, Environmental and Chemical Engineering\u00a0 Computer Science, Electrical and Biomedical Engineering\u00a0 Mechanical, Aerospace, Industrial and Material Science Engineering\u00a0 Our specialized facilities include the Center for Civil Engineering Earthquake Research, Cybersecurity Center, the Nanofabrication Lab in the Davidson Foundation Cleanroom and the Nevada Water Innovation Institute. Located near the Tahoe-Reno Industrial Center and offering ready access to the bustling California economy, Nevada Engineering collaborates with leading tech firms and advance research in robotics, AI, battery technology, aerospace, water systems and sustainable energy.\u00a0Learn more at unr.edu/engineering \u00a0 Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Scholar--Additive-Manufacturing_R0153426": {"title": "Postdoctoral Scholar, Additive Manufacturing", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno is seeking a motivated and innovative Postdoctoral Scholar to join the college of Engineering. This position is available immediately and offers an exciting opportunity to work at the forefront of additive manufacturing research.This is a Department of Defense contract and as such there are stringent citizenship rules. This position reports to Dr. Dev Chidambaram, Nevada Regents Researcher and Professor of Materials Science and Engineering.\u00a0 The successful candidate will contribute to cutting-edge projects focused on the additive manufacturing of metals and composites. In this role, you will conduct high-impact research, publish in leading journals, present findings at conferences, and collaborate with a dynamic team of faculty and students. You will also have opportunities to mentor students and contribute to proposal development, supporting the growth of a strong and externally funded research program. Candidates with experience in additive manufacturing are required, and additional expertise in mechanical testing and materials characterization will be considered a strong advantage. Required Qualifications PhD in Materials Science, Mechanical Engineering, Additive Manufacturing, Metallurgical Engineering, Chemical Engineering or a closely related field. Completion of a doctoral degree in the appropriate discipline is required. The doctoral degree must have been completed within the five years immediately preceding the first date of appointment as a postdoctoral fellow at the University. The individual cannot have held previous positions in the professional ranks. Compensation Grade Postdoctoral Scholar To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules . Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Department Information Join the College of Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno. We value faculty and staff as partners in our mission to deliver outstanding education, research and engagement. Our college seeks individuals committed to: \u00a0 Engaging in impactful outreach \u00a0 Providing state-of-the-art education \u00a0 Fostering respect, inclusiveness and diversity \u00a0 Nevada Engineering is a fast-growing college, with research expenditures that have doubled over the past eight years. Supported by an Engineering Research Office, the college secured $27.9 million in research awards in FY25 and offers professional development resources to ensure success. We offer bachelor\u2019s, graduate degrees and certificates through three schools: \u00a0 Civil, Environmental and Chemical Engineering \u00a0 Computer Science, Electrical and Biomedical Engineering \u00a0 Mechanical, Aerospace, Industrial and Material Science Engineering \u00a0 \u00a0 Our specialized facilities include the Center for Civil Engineering Earthquake Research, Cybersecurity Center, the Nanofabrication Lab in the Davidson Foundation Cleanroom and the Nevada Water Innovation Institute. Located near the Tahoe-Reno Industrial Center and offering ready access to the bustling California economy , Nevada Engineering collaborate s with leading tech firms and advance research in robotics, AI, battery technology, aerospace, water systems and sustainable energy. \u00a0 Learn more at unr.edu/engineering \u00a0 Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Biochemistry---Molecular-Biology_R0153482-1": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Biochemistry, Molecular Biology & Biotechnology", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Biochemistry, Molecular Biology & Biotechnology at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position from candidates conducting cutting-edge, biotechnology-focused research. We seek exceptional scientists whose work aligns with the department's core mission. This is an academic-year position within the College of Agriculture, Biotechnology & Natural Resources. \u00a0 We welcome outstanding applicants ready to build a high-impact, nationally recognized, extramurally funded research program in biotechnology oriented human health & disease, plant science, or arthropod research. The successful candidate will join a collaborative department committed to research excellence, innovative teaching, and meaningful service. \u00a0 Required qualifications: Ph.D. degree in Biochemistry, Biotechnology, Molecular Biology, or a related field. A strong record of research productivity commensurate with experience level. A commitment to working successfully with a diverse student population. \u00a0 Preferred qualifications: At least two years of postdoctoral research experience. A demonstrated history of prior funding. Teaching experience in the areas of molecular biology, biotechnology, or related fields. \u00a0 Compensation Grade \u00a0 Assistant Professor To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program.\u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grant-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information Department of Biochemistry, Molecular Biology & Biotechnology | University of Nevada, Reno Contact Information Please direct questions to the search chair, Dr. Claudia Rueckert, crueckert@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application\u00a0 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter detailing required and preferred qualifications (no more than 1 page). 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References 4) A statement of research interests and vision of future research (no more than 3 pages). 5) A teaching statement (no more than 1 page). This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. For full consideration, please apply by October 31st, 2026. Applications after this date may be considered until the position is filled, but consideration of applications received after October 31st is not guaranteed. Posting Close Date 10/31/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/Medicine-Reno---University-of-Nevada-School-of-Medicine/Librarian-II--Savitt-Medical-Library_R0152978": {"title": "Librarian II, Savitt Medical Library", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778. Job Description The Savitt Medical Library at the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine seeks a collaborative and forward-thinking Librarian II to provide oversight in library technology, digital scholarship, and electronic resource management. This position plays a key role in advancing the library's mission to support education, research, and clinical work of the School of Medicine, while strengthening partnerships across the University, University Libraries, Renown Health, and future affiliates. This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced librarian with expertise in library systems, research data services, and digital scholarship to shape the future of medical library services at Nevada's flagship university. Primary Responsibilities Provide oversight for the administration, optimization, and continuous improvement of library technology systems, including Ex Libris platforms, library discovery services, authentication systems, creating and managing analytics dashboards, and administering resource assessment tools. Develop and advance research data services to support the organization, discovery, sharing, preservation, and stewardship of scholarly and research outputs utilizing authentication workflows and linking technologies. Promote researcher engagement with institutional repositories, including Dryad, ScholarWolf, to increase visibility, accessibility, compliance, and preservation of UNR School of Medicine scholarship. Collaborate with vendors and institutional partners to evaluate, manage, and streamline electronic resources supporting the Savitt Medical Library digital collections. Conduct assessments and enhancements of electronic resource collections through usage, performance, and user-needs analysis. Maintain the Savitt Medical Library website, LibGuides, and digital platforms including but not limited to Libby and Kanopy. Collaborate with public and technical services staff across the University Libraries to ensure accuracy and discoverability of metadata, holdings, and resource access points. Analyze usage metrics from Ex Libris catalog system and service data to inform collection management, planning, and digital transformation initiatives. Provide oversight of Ask-a-Librarian services as well as service request tracking systems. Strengthen engagement with library partners, including Renown Health and future affiliates, to ensure equitable access to library resources and services. Innovate research dissemination to advance digital scholarship and scholarly communication initiatives. Represent the Library through active participation with the University Libraries, School of Medicine, and other professional associations or committees. Offer teaching and instruction on digital library collections and services. Required Qualifications ALA-accredited\u202fMLS\u202for MLIS equivalent or terminal degree. Three (3) years of experience managing and administering integrated library systems (ILS) and other automated library technologies in an academic library setting. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Database coding knowledge (SQL) Website development and management Data management (extraction, synthesis, etc.) Compensation Assistant Professor To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty (12-month). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0 With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR! Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits but, in order to be eligible, children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Contact Information Janet Hobbs at janethobbs@med.unr.edu \u00a0 University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med) For over 50 years the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med) has trained physicians who are leading the way to 'A Healthy Nevada', nation, and world. Community-based and research-intensive, UNR Med is committed to our four-part mission of education, research, clinical service, and diversity. We show this commitment through our demonstrated core values: people are our strength, we innovate and improve, we serve with integrity, and we success with trust and respect. The University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine is an inclusive and engaged community. We recognize that diverse faculty and leaders add value to the educational experience and promote excellence in our teaching, patient care, and research missions. We seek applications from candidates with diverse backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to our mission and uphold our core values. Our mission has guided our growth in graduating the next generation of primary and specialty care physicians and medical researchers while maintaining personalized instruction. The School of Medicine has statewide outreach with a local touch providing patient care, health services, and training physicians in Reno, Nevada and in the state\u2019s rural communities. We are expanding provider education to train medical specialists as Medical Doctors as well as Speech Pathologists and Physician Assistants. We are actively expanding technologically-advanced infrastructure to keep pace with our world-class research to meet the needs of students, patients, and researchers. We seek to create an environment that serves all who enter our doors, from the physicians who teach our learners to our employees, patients, and students. Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachment(s) to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References Posting Close Time This posting will close at 12:00 am on the date listed below. The posting will no longer be available to apply to after 11:59 pm the day prior. \u00a0 Posting Close Date 10/30/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Educator-Preparation--Secondary-Education_R0153418": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Educator Preparation, Secondary Education", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The College of Education and Human Development at the University of Nevada, Reno (R1) invites applications for a tenure-track, full-time Assistant Professor in Secondary Education beginning July 1, 2027. The Assistant Professor will have summer teaching opportunities available. We seek a scholar with a critical research agenda in teaching and learning, teacher preparation, and STEM-related educational contexts such as mathematics education and/or computer science education. We are particularly interested in candidates whose scholarship is grounded in the Learning Sciences and investigates how people learn, how learning environments are designed and studied, and how research on learning can inform educational practice. Areas of interest may include, but are not limited to, learning and cognition; design of learning environments; technology-enhanced learning; artificial intelligence and emerging technologies in education; collaborative or informal learning; learning analytics; teacher learning; equitable and culturally responsive learning environments; or related areas. The successful candidate\u2019s work should connect learning sciences scholarship to one or more areas of strategic importance to the College, including mathematics education, computer science education, STEM education, secondary education, teacher education, or the design and study of innovative learning environments. Candidates whose research crosses disciplinary boundaries and creates opportunities for collaboration across education, STEM disciplines, computing, and other university units are especially encouraged to apply. The faculty will engage in an active, focused research program that results in publications and funded grants and contributes to the profession, university, and community service and outreach. The University\u2019s Office of Research and Innovation supports faculty in conducting research, scholarly, and creative activity. The faculty will teach courses in Secondary Education and other areas related to teacher preparation, such as courses in learning theory, mathematics education, integrated STEM, and/or general education courses. Teaching responsibilities include advisement of Master\u2019s students in the Secondary First-Time Licensure programs. There are opportunities to advise and mentor doctoral students in STEM Education and Curriculum and Instruction. Candidates should demonstrate ability to connect research, pedagogy, and teacher development at the undergraduate and graduate level.\u00a0 Required Qualifications An earned doctorate by July 1, 2027, in Learning Sciences, Educational Psychology, Mathematics Education, STEM Education, Curriculum and Instruction, Teacher Education, or a related field. At least three years of K-12 teaching experience or equivalent. Ability to teach undergraduate- and graduate-level courses in teacher preparation. Demonstrated excellence or potential for excellence in research and grant acquisition. Preferred Qualifications An externally fundable research agenda in learning environments, mathematics education, STEM education, and/or computer science education. Experience in teaching undergraduate and graduate courses related to teacher education in secondary education and clinical settings. Experience in teaching asynchronous and synchronous online courses. Experience in working with populations from diverse backgrounds in secondary education. Experience or interest in building researcher-practitioner partnerships with schools and communities. Compensation Grade Assistant Professor ($80,000 - $85,000) To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University. With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Search Information Please contact the search chair, Jenny Mahon ( jmahon@unr.edu ), with any questions. Department Information The Department of Educator Preparation is part of the College of Education and Human Development. Our mission is to advance teaching, learning, and research to make educational and social systems more equitable through: The preparation of reflective, skillful educators informed by research and the communities we serve to address critical needs at the regional, national, and global levels. Local, national, and international contributions to knowledge, policy, and practice through original research and scholarship. Outreach to and reciprocal and responsible partnerships with students, practitioners, community members, and policymakers to strengthen the well-being of all Nevada citizens. https://www.unr.edu/education/departments/department-of-educator-preparation Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application\u00a0 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Doctor-of-Physical-Therapy_R0153300": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Doctor of Physical Therapy", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno School of Public Health invites applications for a state-funded, tenure-track Assistant Professor position to support the launch and growth of its new Doctor of Physical Therapy (DPT) program. We seek candidates with expertise in one or more of the following areas: Cardiopulmonary Physical Therapy, Exercise Physiology, Physiology, Musculoskeletal Physical Therapy, Neuroscience/Neurologic Rehabilitation, Applied Clinical Pathology, and/or Anatomy. Candidates with strong research agendas in rehabilitation science, exercise physiology, neuroscience, biomechanics, applied physiology, clinical pathology, or related fields are encouraged to apply. This role offers the opportunity to help shape a developing DPT program at a Carnegie R1 research institution, build an independent research program, engage in interdisciplinary collaboration, and contribute to academic excellence in the region. Key Responsibilities Develop and sustain an independent, extramurally fundable research program aligned with one or more of the following areas: Cardiopulmonary Physical Therapy / Exercise Physiology / Physiology, Musculoskeletal Physical Therapy, Neuroscience/Neurologic Rehabilitation, Applied Clinical Pathology, and/or Anatomy. Teach in the DPT curriculum and/or related graduate programs in areas of expertise (e.g., physiology, exercise physiology, neuroscience, pathology, research methods, or related foundational sciences). Mentor DPT students, graduate students, and/or postdoctoral trainees in research and scholarly activities. Contribute to curriculum development, program assessment, and CAPTE accreditation processes as appropriate. Seek and secure extramural funding to support research activities. Disseminate research findings through peer-reviewed publications, presentations, and other scholarly outlets. Foster interdisciplinary collaborations within the School of Public Health, across UNR, and with regional clinical and research partners. Participate in university, school, and professional service activities. Required Qualifications Earned research doctoral degree (PhD, DSc, or equivalent) in Physical Therapy, Rehabilitation Science, Exercise Physiology, Neuroscience, Physiology, Biomechanics, or a closely related field. Evidence of scholarly productivity (peer-reviewed publications, presentations, and/or extramural funding) commensurate with rank. Demonstrated ability or strong potential to teach, advise or mentor effectively the Doctor of Physical Therapy students. Ability to develop and sustain an independent, externally funded research program. Commitment to collaborative, interdisciplinary work and to contributing to a new, developing DPT program. Preferred Qualifications Clinical background and a professional physical therapy degree (DPT) is not required but welcomed with a combination of an academic doctorate. Postdoctoral research experience. Track record of extramural funding (NIH, NSF, foundation, or equivalent) or strong evidence of funding potential. Experience teaching foundational or applied sciences relevant to physical therapy education (physiology, exercise physiology, neuroscience, pathology, anatomy, or related). Experience mentoring student research (undergraduate, graduate, or professional). Experience with curriculum development, interprofessional education, or hybrid instructional approaches. Demonstrated success in interdisciplinary collaboration (e.g., engineering, public health, neuroscience, medicine). Compensation Grade Assistant Professor To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. The University provides a clear promotion pathway for teaching faculty that includes regular review, and base salary increases upon promotion. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University. With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The University of Nevada, Reno\u2019s School of Public Health is dedicated to improving health and well-being through excellence in education, research, policy and community engagement. Its mission is to advance science, policy and practice to improve the health of individuals and communities in Nevada and beyond. The School includes four academic departments and seven community-based centers that foster collaboration across disciplines and sectors. Its nationally ranked graduate programs and strong ties across Nevada are reflected in the fact that approximately 80% of graduates live and work in the state, strengthening the public health workforce and improving outcomes statewide. Faculty and staff contribute to a collaborative, mission-driven environment focused on academic excellence, community partnership and lasting impact through public health leadership and innovation. Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Please attach the following documents to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter addressing research expertise, relevant content area(s), and vision for contributing to the developing DPT program 3) Contact Information for three professional references 4) Research statement describing current and future research agenda, funding history/potential, and fit with the program and School of Public Health 5) Teaching statement and, if applicable, list of courses taught or proposed 6) (Optional) Up to three representative publications This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Clinical-Psychology_R0153453": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Clinical Psychology", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Psychology at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in our APA-accredited Clinical Psychology Graduate Program ( https://www.unr.edu/psychology ). The program has been accredited since 1972 and is currently accredited through 2028. The position will begin in the fall semester of 2027. Area of research specialization is open. We are especially interested in candidates studying childhood/developmental psychopathology, pediatric/health psychology, translational neuroscience, and/or biological processes. However, all candidates with a strong program of research in any area will be fully considered. The successful candidate will have a clinical science orientation and a commitment to training students in evidence-based treatment and assessment. Required Qualifications PhD in Clinical Psychology or Clinical Science by start date Completion of an APA/CPA-accredited internship by start date; ability to supervise clinical doctoral students Licensed or license-eligible in Nevada Record of excellence in research Potential for excellence in teaching and service Compensation Grade Assistant Professor, 9-month To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty (9-month). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR! Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits but, in order to be eligible, children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada.\u00a0 Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The Clinical Psychology Graduate Program at the University of Nevada, Reno, is a part of the Department of Psychology in the College of Science. The program currently has five full-time faculty. Members of the faculty are able to conduct research at UNR in collaboration with the UNR School of Medicine and community partners such as Renown Regional Medical Center, VA Sierra Nevada Health Care System, and Northern Nevada HOPES. The clinical program provides clinical training in an in-house Psychological Services Center ( https://www.unr.edu/psychology/psychological-services ). The Department has strong programs in Cognitive & Brain Science, Behavior Analysis, Integrative Neuroscience, and Social Psychology; collaboration across program areas is encouraged. Shared research facilities include high-density EEG, 3T MRI, fMRI, fNIRS, and computational modeling and analysis core ( https://www.unr.edu/neuroscience/center ). Location & Lifestyle The city of Reno lies 45 minutes from Lake Tahoe and four hours east of San Francisco on the eastern slope of the Sierra Nevada. A highly rated region for living and outdoor recreation, the Reno area also enjoys a flourishing and diverse intellectual, artistic, and cultural community. Contact Information Search Committee Chair: Paul Kwon, pkwon@unr.edu Administrative Contact: Beth Jacobsen, (775) 682-8787, jacobsenb@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu . Application Materials: The following materials must be submitted with your application: 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover letter 3) Research statement 4) Teaching statement 5) Three representative publications 6) Contact information for three professional references Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by October 15, 2026. This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Associate-Full-Professor--Economics--Aerospace_R0152914-1": {"title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Economics, Aerospace", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Economics Department at the University of Nevada, Reno is recruiting for a tenure-track position in Aerospace Economics, at any rank, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. The successful hire will have a record of relevant published research, conduct research in the field, develop specialized courses, and teach both existing and new courses in the Economics program. The professorship is expected to expand access to high-impact, industry-relevant education and mentorship in aerospace economics, work with the regional business community, and support diverse student pathways into a rapidly growing sector of the economy. Required Qualifications: \u00a0 PhD in Economics or related field. Compensation Grade Assistant Professor Associate Professor Full Professor To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty (Nine-month). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University. \u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day. Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow! \u00a0All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada. Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Several Diversity Committees and Affinity Groups focusing on campus-wide diversity initiatives to ensure we are working to create a diverse and welcoming campus climate.\u00a0 Diversity Groups Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grant-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The Economics Department at the University of Nevada, Reno strives to provide high quality education through our degree programs at the undergraduate and graduate levels, undertake cutting-edge economics research, and assist public and private decision makers in practical ways. Consistent with our university's land-grant mission, we serve as a resource to the community through applied scholarship, outreach programs, and economic development. Examples include the University Center for Economic Development, University of Nevada Cooperative Extension, the Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Ozmen Center for Entrepreneurship, and the Institute for the Study of Gambling and Commercial Gaming. Contact Information Ashley Melendez - apmelendez@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu Include the following attachments to your application\u00a0 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact information for three professional references 4) Job market paper or other writing sample This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. To ensure full consideration, applications should be received by November 15, 2026. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Director--DeLaMare-Science---Engineering-Library--Assistant-or-Associate-Professor-_R0153411-1": {"title": "Director, DeLaMare Science & Engineering Library (Assistant or Associate Professor)", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno Libraries seeks an engaged, user-centered leader to manage the DeLaMare Science and Engineering Library (DLM), which provides growing services to the University through Data Services and GIS programs, the Mary B. Ansari Map Library, and an exploratory Makerspace. This is a full-time, tenure-track faculty position. Collections support the fields of chemistry, computer science, earth sciences, engineering, math, and physics. Reporting to the Associate Dean of the Libraries, the Director will work collaboratively to provide high-quality library services that meet the evolving needs of users in the DeLaMare Library as well as other campus libraries and partners. This is a tenure-track faculty position that requires scholarly activity and publication and service on professional and university committees. More information about the University Libraries\u2019 expectations related to faculty rank and tenure can be found in the Bylaws of the University Libraries at the University of Nevada, Reno . Responsibilities include: Providing strategic direction for all DLM services, technologies, collections, and spaces in alignment with University and University Libraries strategic plans. Managing a team of four faculty, two staff, and approximately twenty student workers, and coordinating daily operations. Assuming administrative responsibility for the unit, including assessment of spaces and services. Representing the DLM for development efforts in support of administrative goals. Partnering with faculty and campus entities to meet teaching and research objectives, integrating library activities into the curriculum and supporting student engagement efforts. Serving as a liaison librarian for assigned STEM disciplines, providing research consultations, instruction, collections support, and scholarly communications support. Maintaining familiarity with recent trends in research and teaching related to the liaison areas, such as those concerning journal publication, AI literacy, and evidence synthesis. Continuing and developing user-centered services and integrating new methods and technologies. Collaborating and communicating effectively with other library departments to meet the organizational objectives and strategic initiatives of the University Libraries. Working effectively with a diverse, highly engaged campus and local community from a variety of cultures and backgrounds. Creating and supporting a positive, engaged, and inclusive work culture. Required Qualifications ALA-accredited MLIS or equivalent terminal degree Five (5) years of professional library experience Successful managerial experience (e.g., personnel supervision, major project management, facilities operations, or research management) Preferred Qualifications We recognize that candidates may not have all the preferred qualifications\u2014we encourage you to apply if you meet some or most of them. Academic coursework and/or work experience in data services, geospatial data, STEM disciplines or academic makerspaces Knowledge of emerging technologies and trends in higher education Experience planning and assessing innovative, user-centered library services and spaces Professional experience in an academic library setting Library liaison or academic instruction experience Budget preparation and financial management Compensation The minimum salary for Assistant Professor is\u00a0$80,000 The minimum salary for Associate Professor is\u00a0$90,000 The Assistant or Associate Professor level is dependent on experience, record of scholarship,\u00a0service\u00a0and achievement. To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty (12-month). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0 \u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous annual and sick leave and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Contact Information Search Coordinator: Emme Haw at\u00a0 ehaw@unr.edu \u00a0\u00a0 Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you\u00a0submit\u00a0your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any\u00a0additional\u00a0required attachment/s to the cover letter, references,\u00a0additional\u00a0documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you\u00a0submit\u00a0your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu .\u00a0 Please attach the following documents to your application:\u00a0 1)\u00a0 Resume/CV 2)\u00a0 Cover letter 3)\u00a0 Contact information for three professional references\u00a0 This posting is open until filled. Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Lecturer-II--Human-Development-and-Counseling_R0153290": {"title": "Lecturer II, Human Development and Counseling", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Human Development, Family Science, and Counseling (HDFSC) in the College of Education and Human Development at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a continuing, full-time (non-tenure track) Lecturer II position with an anticipated start date of January 1, 2027. \u00a0The HDFSC Department is dedicated to advancing the wellbeing of individuals, families, and communities through excellence in teaching, scholarship, and community engagement. We prepare students for careers and graduate study in human development, family science, counseling, education, social services, and related fields. As part of a Carnegie R1 research university and a land-grant institution, the department is committed to fostering student success through evidence-based teaching, mentoring, and applied learning opportunities. The successful candidate will contribute to the department's instructional mission by teaching undergraduate courses in Human Development and Family Science, primarily in our online HDFS Bachelor of Science program. The candidate will serve as the coordinator and instructor of online service-learning courses for the program. Other online/in-person courses may include introductory and upper-division offerings in lifespan human development, family interaction, parenting, family resource management, diversity in families, family policy, research methods, and related topics consistent with the candidate's expertise and departmental needs. Teaching assignments may also include service courses that support students from across the university. Strong candidates will demonstrate innovative, student-centered approaches to online and in-person teaching that promote engagement, critical thinking, and academic success. Experience with online instruction that incorporates active learning, inclusive pedagogical practices, and applied learning opportunities is desirable. The expected teaching load is the equivalent of 4 courses per academic semester. Applicants are encouraged to review the department's curriculum and programs through the HDFSC departmental website and university catalog. We welcome applicants who wish to play an integral role in the online undergraduate mission of the department. In addition to classroom instruction, the faculty member will attend all department and program meetings, and be involved in student engagement activities, including mentoring, recruitment, retention initiatives, and participation in departmental assessment and curriculum development. Service responsibilities may also include committee work, program evaluation, and support of departmental events and community partnerships. Lecturer Statement The University of Nevada, Reno reserves the right to hire at any level of Lecturer (I-IV) or Teaching Assistant/Associate/Professor (terminal degree candidates) that is appropriate for the hire.\u00a0 If you have questions about title, please inquire during the interview process. Required Qualifications Lecturer II: Master's degree in Human Development and Family Science, Human Development, Family Science, Child Development, Family Studies, or a closely related field by the start date. Teaching Assistant Professor: Ph.D. in Human Development and Family Science, Human Development, Family Science, Child Development, Family Studies or a closely related field by the start date. Demonstrated commitment to excellence in online teaching, community-engaged learning, and student mentoring. Evidence of effective communication and interpersonal skills. Commitment to fostering student engagement and success in a diverse, online learning environment. Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching online undergraduate courses as the primary instructor. Experience with student advising, mentoring, or co-curricular programming. Demonstrated use of innovative, evidence-based, and inclusive teaching practices in online and in-person teaching environments. Experience working with diverse student populations and community partners through community-engaged scholarship. Compensation Lecturer II (Non-tenure track) To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University. With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Inforamtion The Department of Human Development, Family Science, and Counseling (HDFSC) offers undergraduate and graduate programs in Human Development and Family Science that emphasize lifespan development, family relationships, prevention and intervention, community engagement, and professional preparation. Located in Reno, Nevada, the University provides unique opportunities for collaboration with schools, community agencies, healthcare systems, and nonprofit organizations serving diverse populations throughout the region. Contact Information Search Chair, Melissa Burnham at mburnham@unr.edu \u00a0 Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at\u00a0 jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References This posting is open until filled. Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/Extension-Clark---University-of-Nevada-Reno/Lecturer-II---Teaching-Assistant-Professor---Assistant-Professor--Commercial-Horticulture-Specialist--Clark-County--NV-_R0153296": {"title": "Lecturer II / Teaching Assistant Professor / Assistant Professor, Commercial Horticulture Specialist (Clark County, NV)", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-28\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno Extension invites applications for a Lecturer II/Teaching Assistant Professor/Assistant Professor, Commercial Horticulture Specialist position (full-time) serving Southern Nevada's dynamic and growing commercial horticulture industry. This position is located in Las Vegas, NV and reports to the Southern Area Director.\u00a0 This faculty position provides an exciting opportunity to develop and deliver impactful educational programs, conduct applied research, and collaborate with industry professionals to advance sustainable landscape management in arid environments. The successful candidate will play a vital role in strengthening workforce knowledge, enhancing plant health, and supporting the long-term economic viability of Southern Nevada's commercial horticulture sector. About the Position The Commercial Horticulture Specialist will develop and deliver research-based educational programming and applied research that addresses current and emerging needs of the commercial horticulture industry. Programs will focus on: Workforce development and training Plant health and integrated landscape management Integrated pest management Sustainable landscape practices for arid environments Best management practices for commercial horticulture operations and food production This position serves a diverse clientele including commercial landscape professionals, arborists and urban forestry professionals, irrigation specialists, nursery and greenhouse operators, municipal and institutional landscape managers, industry organizations and stakeholders, and community members. Position Responsibilities Extension Programming and Outreach (70%) : Develop and deliver research-based educational programs that support Southern Nevada's commercial horticulture industry and promote sustainable landscape management in arid environments. Applied Research (25%) : Conduct applied research and demonstration projects that address industry needs related to workforce development, water-use efficiency, and plant health. Identify and pursue extramural funding opportunities to support commercial horticulture education and research initiatives. Publish peer-reviewed articles, Extension bulletins, and other scholarly and educational materials. Service and Professional Engagement (5%) : Contribute to university, Extension, and professional service activities while maintaining strong industry partnerships. Lecturer Statement The University of Nevada, Reno reserves the right to hire at any level of Lecturer (I-IV) or Teaching Assistant/Associate (terminal degree candidate) that is appropriate for the hire. If you have questions about the title, please inquire during the interview process. Required Qualifications Lecturer II - Master's degree in horticulture, plant sciences, agronomy, agriculture, natural resources, environmental sciences, Extension education, or a closely related field. Teaching Assistant Professor / Assistant Professor - Ph.D. degree in horticulture, plant sciences, agronomy, agriculture, natural resources, environmental sciences, Extension education, or a closely related field. Demonstrated knowledge of commercial horticulture, landscape management, irrigation systems, plant health, or related disciplines. Experience developing and delivering educational programs, training, outreach, or technical assistance to diverse audiences. Effective written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to work collaboratively with industry professionals, community partners, and stakeholders. This position requires candidates to complete a fingerprinting/background check. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. in horticulture, plant sciences, agriculture, natural resources, Extension education, or a related field. Experience in Extension, outreach, applied research, or other non-formal educational environments. Knowledge of horticultural production, food production, integrated pest management, and landscape management practices in arid and desert environments. Experience with irrigation efficiency, water conservation, urban horticulture, arboriculture, or sustainable landscape management. Demonstrated ability to secure external funding and build partnerships with industry, government agencies, and community organizations. Licensure A valid Class \u201cC\u201d driver\u2019s license or higher operator\u2019s license within 30 days of appointment. Schedule and Travel Work a flexible schedule, with periodic evening and weekend work. Occasional travel throughout the state to assist with multi-county or statewide efforts related to subject matter expertise or attend area and statewide Extension meetings. This position involves both office and field environments. Compensation Lecturer II / Teaching Assistant Professor (Non-tenure track) Assistant Professor (Tenure track) To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. The University provides a clear promotion pathway for teaching faculty that includes regular review, and base salary increases upon promotion. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University. With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program College/Department Information UNR Extension has a long-standing commitment to providing practical, research-based education that improves lives and strengthens communities throughout Nevada. As part of Extension, you will have the opportunity to: Make a meaningful impact on a critical industry sector. Lead innovative programs. Collaborate with university researchers, public agencies, and industry leaders. Serve as a trusted resource for Southern Nevada's commercial horticulture community. Advance workforce development and environmental stewardship in one of the nation's most unique growing environments. To learn more about Extension, please visit: https://extension.unr.edu/default.aspx Search Contacts Search Coordinator: Cinnamon Banda, cinnamon.banda@unr.edu Hiring Manager: Eric Killian, killeane@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note that once you submit your application, the only attachments viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachments to the cover letter, references, and other documents sections of the application will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachments will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments before the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References \u00a0 Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by end of business, Friday, September 25, 2026. This posting is open until filled. Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/Medicine-Reno---University-of-Nevada-School-of-Medicine/Biostatistician--Research-Scientist--Assistant--Associate--or-Full-Professor-_R0153270": {"title": "Biostatistician (Research Scientist, Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor)", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-27\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778. Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med) seeks a doctoral-level Biostatistician (Research Scientist, Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor), non-tenure-track. to serve as a faculty member within the Translational Research Center. This position will play a critical role in advancing the institution\u2019s clinical, translational, and medical education research mission by providing expertise in study design, statistical analysis, and research methodology, while contributing to the education and mentorship of medical students, residents, and faculty investigators. \u00a0 The successful candidate will integrate biostatistical rigor into clinical, translational, and medical education research initiatives, support student scholarship, and contribute to the delivery and continuous improvement of the biostatistics curriculum within the MD program. Primary Responsibilities: Teaching and Curriculum Development Contribute to the design, delivery, and evaluation of biostatistics instruction within the MD curriculum, including pre-clerkship and clerkship phases Participate in the Evidence and Systems-Based Practice (ESP) or equivalent curriculum thread Develop teaching materials, case-based learning modules, and applied statistical exercises Support integration of biostatistics into clinically relevant learning activities and assessments Evaluate student performance and contribute to curriculum improvement efforts Research Collaboration and Support Provide biostatistical expertise for clinical, translational and medical education research projects across department Collaborate with clinical faculty to: \u200bDesign research protocols Develop statistical analysis plans Interpret findings for publication and presentation Serve as a core resource for: \u200bStudy design and methodology consultation Data analysis and statistical modeling Facilitate manuscript and grant preparation Partner with the Translational Research Center and affiliated research infrastructure to support investigator-initiated and sponsored studies Collaborate with faculty on educational research and participate in journal clubs to support faculty professional development Mentorship and Scholarly Development Mentor medical students, residents, fellows, and junior faculty in research design and statistical analysis Develop a longitudinal experience in incorporating evidence-based medicine for medical students throughout the clerkships Support student scholarly concentrations, research projects, and capstone experiences Provide guidance on: Data analysis workflows Statistical interpretation Research dissemination (abstracts, posters, manuscripts) Contribute to development of a sustainable research mentorship ecosystem Independent and Collaborative Scholarship Maintain an active program of scholarly activity in biostatistics, clinical research, or related areas Contribute to peer-reviewed publications and external grant applications Participate as a co-investigator or methodological expert on funded research projects Present findings at regional and national scientific meetings Institutional and Service Contributions Participate in academic and administrative committees related to research and education Contribute to development of institutional research infrastructure and strategy Support compliance with IRB, regulatory, and research governance processes Engage in collaborative initiatives with Renown Health and other partner organizations Required Qualifications PhD, DrPH in Biostatistics, Statistics, Epidemiology, or closely related field OR Master\u2019s trained with relevant experience working with a medical school. Demonstrated expertise in statistical methods relevant to clinical, translational or medical education research Experience in collaborative research environments supporting clinicians in developing research projects Strong communication skills, with the ability to explain statistical concepts to non-experts Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching biostatistics or epidemiology to medical or health professions students Proficiency in statistical software (e.g., R, SAS, Stata, or equivalent) Track record of collaborative research Experience mentoring trainees in academic medicine Familiarity with clinical data environments (e.g., EHR-derived datasets, OMOP, or similar) Compensation Grade Research Scientist - I Assistant Professor \u2013 II Associate Professor - III Full Professor - IV To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules and select School of Medicine - Medical Academic Assistant Professor, reading along the Basic Science line. Associate Professor salary can be found on subsequent drop down menu. Compensation is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous annual and sick leave and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits No state income tax! E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate Remarkable Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0 With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada. Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Contact Information Search Coordinator, Jenny Trinh at jennyt@unr.edu \u00a0 Search Coordinator, Samantha Sipusic at ssipusic@unr.edu \u00a0 University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med) For over 50 years the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med) has trained physicians who are leading the way to 'A Healthy Nevada', nation, and world. Community-based and research-intensive, UNR Med is committed to our four-part mission of education, research, clinical service, and diversity. We show this commitment through our demonstrated core values: people are our strength, we innovate and improve, we serve with integrity, and we success with trust and respect. The University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine is an inclusive and engaged community. We recognize that diverse faculty and leaders add value to the educational experience and promote excellence in our teaching, patient care, and research missions. We seek applications from candidates with diverse backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to our mission and uphold our core values. Our mission has guided our growth in graduating the next generation of primary and specialty care physicians and medical researchers while maintaining personalized instruction. The School of Medicine has statewide outreach with a local touch providing patient care, health services, and training physicians in Reno, Nevada and in the state\u2019s rural communities. We are expanding provider education to train medical specialists as Medical Doctors as well as Speech Pathologists and Physician Assistants. We are actively expanding technologically-advanced infrastructure to keep pace with our world-class research to meet the needs of students, patients, and researchers. We seek to create an environment that serves all who enter our doors, from the physicians who teach our learners to our employees, patients, and students. Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note that once you submit your application, the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, or additional documents sections of the application will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, before the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Lecturer-Teaching-Assistant-Professor--Criminal-Justice_R0153143": {"title": "Lecturer/Teaching Assistant Professor, Criminal Justice", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Criminal Justice at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a Lecturer or Teaching Assistant Professor to begin July 1, 2027. This is a teaching-focused position with a 4-4 teaching load, with service and professional development responsibilities and no research obligations. We seek a candidate with broad teaching interests to contribute primarily to our on-ground and online undergraduate programs. The successful candidate must demonstrate the potential for excellence in teaching and service. Required Qualifications Lecturer - M.A. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or a related field. Candidates possessing a J.D. will also be considered. Teaching Assistant Professor - PhD. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or a related field. Preferred Qualifications M.A. or Ph.D. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or a related field. Evidence of prior teaching experience and the ability to teach Criminal Law, Criminal Justice Policy, and other core courses across the Criminal Justice curriculum. Compensation Grade/Rank Lecturer/Teaching Assistant Professor (9-month, B-contract). Salary is competitive with a hiring range of $63,000-$64,500 and commensurate with related education and experience. Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - \u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013 \u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0 \u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0 supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Several Diversity Committees and Affinity Groups focusing on campus-wide diversity initiatives to ensure we are working to create a diverse and welcoming campus climate. \u00a0 Diversity Groups Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. \u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The UNR Department of Criminal Justice is an interdisciplinary department of 13 full-time faculty and several part-time faculty with experience in the field. We offer a B.A in Criminal Justice, both on-ground and online, M.A. in Criminal Justice, M.A. in Justice Management, and a Certificate in Forensics Studies. Faculty also contribute to the interdisciplinary Ph.D. program in Social Psychology and Ph.D. program in Judicial Studies. With more than 700 majors and minors, we are the largest undergraduate major in the College of Liberal Arts. Additional information about the Department can be found at: www.unr.edu/criminal-justice College and University Information The University of Nevada, Reno is a public Research 1 land-grant institution of the Nevada System of Higher Education with a student population of approximately 21,000, including over 3,000 graduate students with a campus also at Lake Tahoe . We are a comprehensive, doctoral university that has been designated as a Carnegie R1 institution with very high research activity and a Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Nearly half of our students are students of color, and we are considered a Hispanic Serving Institution. In accordance with the University's land grant tier-one research institution mission, our faculty are expected to contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community through research, teaching, and service in a pluralistic environment. The University has dynamic intellectual, artistic and sports life, and a close relationship to the city of Reno Located in one of the nation\u2019s fastest-growing innovative economies, Reno is home to a thriving technology ecosystem, attracting leading companies in advanced manufacturing, clean energy, logistics, artificial intelligence, and data infrastructure. It is a growing tech and innovation hub with a diverse population and a rich cultural history that hosts an active art, music and festival scene. Known for its proximity to Lake Tahoe and the Sierra Nevada mountains, Reno is a vibrant, diverse and growing city located on the eastern slope of the Sierra Nevada, about 200 miles east of San Francisco, about 100 miles east of Sacramento and 30 miles north of the state capital, Carson City. Combined with the neighboring city of Sparks, the Reno-Sparks metropolitan area has a population of more than 500,000. Reno is only 30-45 minutes from Lake Tahoe and major ski resorts and is rated one of the top locations in the United States for living and outdoor recreation. It has an international airport with direct flights to many U.S. transportation hubs and to Mexico. For more information about the city and the surrounding area, please visit www.cityofreno.com and www.visitrenotahoe.com . The College of Liberal Arts is home to the humanities, social sciences, and arts, offering 13 academic units and 8 additional programs, museums, and centers, including the Center for Constitutional Law and the Center for Basque Studies, that foster creativity, critical thinking, and engaged citizenship. As one of the largest academic units on campus with just under 4,000 students and 240 faculty, the College combines a strong liberal arts tradition with innovative and interdisciplinary teaching, research, and community engagement, preparing students for meaningful careers and lifelong learning in an interconnected world. The College emphasizes inquiry, discovery, and openness to new ideas and perspectives and supports major scholarly research and creative activity in the three disciplines and offers a number of programs at the Lake Tahoe campus. Visit www.unr.edu/liberal-arts for more information about the college , faculty, and students . Contact Information for this position Search Chair: Lauren Anderson andersonlauren@unr.edu Search Coordinator: Brenda Barte bbarte@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Please attach the following documents to your application: 1. A complete Curriculum Vita 2. Cover letter outlining qualifications, teaching experience, and how you would contribute toward the college\u2019s mission . 3. Statement of teaching philosophy and experience **Finalists will be asked to share recent teaching evaluations and contact information for three professional references Open Until Filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received, or a hiring decision has been made. First review of applications will begin Oct. 1, 2026. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/R0153230-Lecturer-II---Teaching-Assistant-Professor--Natural-Resources-and-Environmental-Science_R0153230": {"title": "Lecturer II / Teaching Assistant Professor, Natural Resources and Environmental Science", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Natural Resources and Environmental Science (NRES) at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a continuing, full-time (non-tenure track) Lecturer (II) or Teaching Assistant Professor with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. The NRES Department has diverse faculty and students dedicated to solving critical environmental challenges with cutting-edge research. We are a research-intensive Department with a focus on field- and lab-based teaching, aiming to provide students with a foundation in scientific concepts and applied skills for careers in research, outreach and resource management. Reno\u2019s location at the intersection of the northern Sierra and the Great Basin provides close proximity to diverse landscapes for class field trips and other outdoors learning experiences. We offer both undergraduate and graduate programs, with undergraduate degrees in Environmental Science, Forest Ecology and Management, and Wildlife Ecology and Conservation. The successful candidate will contribute to this mission by teaching a range of lower-division undergraduate courses. Potential courses may include introductory ecology, environmental science, natural resource management, environmental ethics and cultural perspectives on resource management. Teaching may include upper-division courses in the candidate\u2019s area of expertise and service courses for non-majors.\u00a0 Strong candidates will demonstrate innovative approaches to teaching, resulting in high student engagement and interaction with course materials and have experience training students in scientific writing, quantitative analysis and critical thinking skills. The expected teaching load is 5 to 6 in- person courses per year. Applicants are encouraged to consult our undergraduate curricula , linked to from the NRES departmental website . We welcome applicants who wish to play an integral role in the undergraduate mission of the department. In addition to classroom instruction, the faculty member will be involved with student engagement, which will include student advising and activities related to student recruitment and retention. Other service duties may include revising curriculum, program and course assessment and course scheduling.\u00a0\u00a0 Lecturer Statement The University of Nevada, Reno reserves the right to hire at any level of Lecturer (I-II) or Teaching Assistant/Teaching Associate Professor (terminal degree candidates) that is appropriate for the hire. If you have questions about title, please inquire during the interview process. Required Qualifications Lecturer II \u2013 Master\u2019s degree with at least one higher-education degree in a Natural Resources or Environmental Science discipline. Teaching Assistant Professor - Ph.D. with at least one higher-education degree in a Natural Resources or Environmental Science discipline. Applicants must be strongly committed to excellence in teaching and student mentoring as well as having a demonstrated interest in innovative teaching.\u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates who have taught at least one undergraduate course as primary instructor. Compensation Grade Lecturer II (Salary range: $54,800 - $84,400) Teaching Assistant Professor (Salary range: $63,000 - $96,900) To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. The University provides a clear promotion pathway for teaching faculty that includes regular review, and base salary increases upon promotion. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University. With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options .\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The Department of Natural Resources & Environmental Science (NRES) is a multidisciplinary department within the College of Agriculture, Biotechnology, & Natural Resources (CABNR) that is known for its high research activity and engagement in both basic and applied research. Faculty are recognized as regional, national, and international experts in the fields of wildlife and fish biology, forest and fire ecology, ecohydrology, soils, and environmental science. The Department is committed to high quality education that prepares students for natural resource careers, offering undergraduate degrees in Environmental Science, Wildlife Ecology & Conservation, and Forest Ecology & Management. Students may additionally pursue MS or PhD programs within NRES or in one of three interdisciplinary graduate programs on UNR\u2019s campus (Ecology, Evolution and Conservation Biology; Environmental Sciences and Health; and Hydrologic Sciences). NRES currently supports ~400 undergraduate students as well as 27 MS and 30 PhD students. Contact Information Leslie Worden (Search Coordinator) at 775-784-4020 or lworden@unr.edu Peter Weisberg (Search Chair) at pweisberg@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Please attach the following documents to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter. Please include in your application letter a list of courses you are most interested in teaching and a statement describing how you would contribute to our mission of fostering a supportive learning environment through your teaching and/or service. 3) Contact Information for three professional references 4) Teaching Statement Full Consideration For full consideration, applications must be received by 11/15/2026. After that date, the position will remain open until filled. This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date 11/16/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Criminal-Justice_R0153145": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Criminal Justice", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Criminal Justice within the College of Liberal Arts at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position to begin July 1, 2027. The position is open with respect to sub-field, with a preference for candidates whose teaching and research expertise are centered broadly in the area of corrections. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an active program of research and contribute to the department\u2019s undergraduate and graduate programs. The standard teaching load is 2-2, with substantial involvement in graduate education, including graduate-level teaching, student mentoring and advising, and service as chair or member of graduate committees. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or a related field in hand by July 1, 2027. ABDs will be considered. Candidates possessing only a JD will not be considered. The successful candidate must demonstrate the potential for excellence in teaching, research, and service. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or a related field in hand by the position start date. Evidence of prior teaching excellence and experience and publication in the field of corrections and the ability to teach Criminological Theory and contribute to our undergraduate, graduate, and online programs.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Compensation Grade Assistant Professor (9-month, B-contract) Salary is competitive with a hiring range of $79,000-$83,000 and commensurate with related education and experience. Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - \u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013 \u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0 \u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0 supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Several Diversity Committees and Affinity Groups focusing on campus-wide diversity initiatives to ensure we are working to create a diverse and welcoming campus climate. \u00a0 Diversity Groups Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. \u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The UNR Department of Criminal Justice is an interdisciplinary department of 13 full-time faculty and several part-time faculty with experience in the field. We offer a B.A in Criminal Justice, both on-ground and online, M.A. in Criminal Justice, M.A. in Justice Management, and a Certificate in Forensics Studies. Faculty also contribute to the interdisciplinary Ph.D. program in Social Psychology and Ph.D. program in Judicial Studies. With more than 700 majors and minors, we are the largest undergraduate major in the College of Liberal Arts. Additional information about the Department can be found at: www.unr.edu/criminal-justice College and University Information The University of Nevada, Reno is a public Research 1 land-grant institution of the Nevada System of Higher Education with a student population of approximately 21,000, including over 3,000 graduate students with a campus also at Lake Tahoe . We are a comprehensive, doctoral university that has been designated as a Carnegie R1 institution with very high research activity and a Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Nearly half of our students are students of color, and we are considered a Hispanic Serving Institution. In accordance with the University's land grant tier-one research institution mission, our faculty are expected to contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community through research, teaching, and service in a pluralistic environment. The University has dynamic intellectual, artistic and sports life, and a close relationship to the city of Reno Located in one of the nation\u2019s fastest-growing innovative economies, Reno is home to a thriving technology ecosystem, attracting leading companies in advanced manufacturing, clean energy, logistics, artificial intelligence, and data infrastructure. It is a growing tech and innovation hub with a diverse population and a rich cultural history that hosts an active art, music and festival scene. Known for its proximity to Lake Tahoe and the Sierra Nevada mountains, Reno is a vibrant, diverse and growing city located on the eastern slope of the Sierra Nevada, about 200 miles east of San Francisco, about 100 miles east of Sacramento and 30 miles north of the state capital, Carson City. Combined with the neighboring city of Sparks, the Reno-Sparks metropolitan area has a population of more than 500,000. Reno is only 30-45 minutes from Lake Tahoe and major ski resorts and is rated one of the top locations in the United States for living and outdoor recreation. It has an international airport with direct flights to many U.S. transportation hubs and to Mexico. For more information about the city and the surrounding area, please visit www.cityofreno.com and www.visitrenotahoe.com . The College of Liberal Arts is home to the humanities, social sciences, and arts, offering 13 academic units and 8 additional programs, museums, and centers, including the Center for Constitutional Law and the Center for Basque Studies, that foster creativity, critical thinking, and engaged citizenship. As one of the largest academic units on campus with just under 4,000 students and 240 faculty, the College combines a strong liberal arts tradition with innovative and interdisciplinary teaching, research, and community engagement, preparing students for meaningful careers and lifelong learning in an interconnected world. The College emphasizes inquiry, discovery, and openness to new ideas and perspectives and supports major scholarly research and creative activity in the three disciplines and offers a number of programs at the Lake Tahoe campus. Visit www.unr.edu/liberal-arts for more information about the college , faculty, and students . Contact Information for this position Search Chair: Emmanuel Barthe emmnanuelb@unr.edu Search Coordinator: Brenda Barte bbarte@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Please attach the following documents to your application: 1. A complete Curriculum Vita 2. Cover letter outlining qualifications, experience, research plans, teaching experience, and how you would contribute toward the college\u2019s mission . 3. Statement of teaching philosophy and experience 4. Statement of research agenda **Finalists will be asked to share recent teaching evaluations and contact information for three professional references Open Until Filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received, or a hiring decision has been made. First review of applications will begin Oct. 1, 2026. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Political-Science--Public-Policy-_R0153278": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Political Science (Public Policy)", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Political Science within the College of Liberal Arts at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a full-time tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor in the subfield of Public Policy. An international or comparative public policy focus is welcome but not required. The successful candidate will be expected to develop a robust and teaching research program; substantive research and teaching areas may include, but are not limited to, energy, climate, environment, or related salient policy areas within the field of public policy. We welcome applicants from any theoretical or methodological tradition. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Political Science, Public Policy, Public Affairs/Administration, or a related field in hand by July 1, 2027. A developing or established research agenda in public policy. A research agenda with emphasis in energy, climate, environment or related salient policy areas. A record of, or potential for, publishing in top-tier journals and presses in relevant fields. Demonstrated excellence, or demonstrated potential for excellence, in teaching graduate and undergraduate courses in energy, climate, environment or related salient public policy areas. Demonstrated excellence, or demonstrated potential for excellence, in teaching graduate-level public policy courses (e.g. Seminar in Public Policy, Policy Analysis). Demonstrated excellence, or demonstrated potential for excellence, in teaching undergraduate-level policy classes (e.g. Introduction to American Public Policy, Policy Analysis). Preferred Qualifications Evidence of or potential for strong external grant activity. A research agenda with emphasis in energy, climate, environment, health or related salient public policy area with an international or comparative focus. Evidence of successful teaching at the undergraduate and/or graduate levels. Compensation Grade Assistant Professor (9-month, B-contract) Salary is competitive with a range of $65,000 to $80,500 and commensurate with related education and experience. Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Several Diversity Committees and Affinity Groups focusing on campus-wide diversity initiatives to ensure we are working to create a diverse and welcoming campus climate. Diversity Groups Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The UNR Department of Political Science is an energetic and collegial community of political scientists and public policy and administration scholars with a commitment to excellence in research and teaching in the Department, the University, and the diverse northern Nevada community. The Department is home to three graduate programs that confer MA, MPA and PhD degrees. It offers a BA and a BS in Political Science and coordinates the International Affairs major and various minors. Faculty participate in a variety of College and University-wide initiatives, such as the Ozmen Institute for Global Studies and the Graduate Program of Hydrologic Sciences . Additional information about the Department can be found at Political Science . College and University Information The University of Nevada, Reno is a public Research 1 land-grant institution of the Nevada System of Higher Education with a student population of approximately 21,000, including over 3,000 graduate students with a campus also at Lake Tahoe . We are a comprehensive, doctoral university that has been designated as a Carnegie R1 institution with very high research activity and a Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Nearly half of our students are students of color, and we are considered a Hispanic Serving Institution. In accordance with the University's land grant tier-one research institution mission, our faculty are expected to contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community through research, teaching, and service in a pluralistic environment. The University has dynamic intellectual, artistic and sports life, and a close relationship to the city of Reno. Located in one of the nation\u2019s fastest-growing innovative economies, Reno is home to a thriving technology ecosystem, attracting leading companies in advanced manufacturing, clean energy, logistics, artificial intelligence, and data infrastructure. It is a growing tech and innovation hub with a diverse population and has a rich cultural history that hosts an active art, music and festival scene. Known for its proximity to Lake Tahoe and the Sierra Nevada mountains, Reno is a vibrant, diverse and growing city located on the eastern slope of the Sierra Nevada, about 200 miles east of San Francisco, about 100 miles east of Sacramento and 30 miles north of the state capital, Carson City. Combined with the neighboring city of Sparks, the Reno-Sparks metropolitan area has a population of more than 500,000. Reno is only 30-45 minutes from Lake Tahoe and major ski resorts and is rated one of the top locations in the United States for living and outdoor recreation. It has an international airport with direct flights to many U.S. transportation hubs and to Mexico. For more information about the city and the surrounding area, please visit www.cityofreno.com and www.visitrenotahoe.com . The College of Liberal Arts is home to the humanities, social sciences, and arts, offering 13 academic units and 8 additional programs, museums, and centers, including the Center for Constitutional Law and the Center for Basque Studies, that foster creativity, critical thinking, and engaged citizenship. As one of the largest academic units on campus with just under 4,000 students and 240 faculty, the College combines a strong liberal arts tradition with innovative and interdisciplinary teaching, research, and community engagement, preparing students for meaningful careers and lifelong learning in an interconnected world. The College emphasizes inquiry, discovery, and openness to new ideas and perspectives and supports major scholarly research and creative activity in the three disciplines and offers a number of programs at the Lake Tahoe campus. Visit www.unr.edu/liberal-arts for more information about the college , faculty, and students . Contact Information for this position Search Chair: Prof. Elizabeth Koebele, ekoebele@unr.edu Search Coordinator: Karen Braun, kbraun@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachments to your application 1. Complete Curriculum Vita. 2. Cover letter (2-3 pages) outlining qualifications and experience, research plans, teaching experience, and how you would contribute to the college\u2019s \u00a0 mission . 3. A sample syllabus that you have constructed for a course appropriate to this position. 4. A research-based writing sample, such as a publication, dissertation chapter, or similar. 5. Names and Contact information for three professional references; letters will only be solicited from candidates who advance in the search. ** Finalists will be asked to share recent teaching evaluations. Open Until Filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received, or a hiring decision has been made. The search committee will begin reviewing applications on Oct. 1, 2026. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Political-Science--Public-Administration-_R0153282": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Political Science (Public Administration)", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Political Science within the College of Liberal Arts at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor in the area of Public Administration. We seek candidates with broad competence in Public Administration and welcome all qualified applicants, including those with expertise in tribal governance, behavioral public administration, state-level public administration, nonprofit management, or other specialties that complement existing research areas in the department. We welcome applicants from any empirical research methodological tradition but expect a high-level of sophistication and rigor. The Department offers a Masters of Public Administration (MPA) program, which includes a public policy component.\u00a0The successful candidate will be core faculty in the MPA program and will have the opportunity to teach and advise at all levels of the curriculum (lower and upper division undergraduate, MA, MPA, and PhD), including courses required for degree programs. The standard teaching load is 2/2. \u00a0More information about the department can be found here: https://www.unr.edu/political-science Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Public Administration, Public Policy, Public Affairs, Political Science or a related field in hand by July 1, 2027. A developing or established research agenda in Public Administration, broadly defined. A record of, or potential for, publishing in top-tier journals and presses in relevant fields. A rigorous, empirical research methodology. Demonstrated excellence, or clear evidence of potential for excellence, in teaching in the field of public administration. Ability to teach core courses in the MPA program and related courses across graduate and undergraduate levels. Ability to teach and mentor MA and PhD students in Public Administration and Public Policy. Preferred Qualifications Evidence of or potential for strong external grant activity. Evidence of successful teaching at the undergraduate and/or graduate levels Compensation Grade Assistant Professor (9-month, B-contract) Salary is competitive with a range of $65,000 to $80,500 and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program. Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Several Diversity Committees and Affinity Groups focusing on campus-wide diversity initiatives to ensure we are working to create a diverse and welcoming campus climate. Diversity Groups Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The UNR Department of Political Science is an energetic and collegial community of political scientists and public policy and administration scholars with a commitment to excellence in research and teaching in the Department, the University, and the diverse northern Nevada community. The Department is home to three graduate programs that confer MA, MPA and PhD degrees. It offers a BA and a BS in Political Science and coordinates the International Affairs major and various minors. Faculty participate in a variety of College and University-wide initiatives, such as the Ozmen Institute for Global Studies and the Graduate Program of Hydrologic Sciences . Additional information about the Department can be found at Political Science . College and University Information The University of Nevada, Reno is a public Research 1 land-grant institution of the Nevada System of Higher Education with a student population of approximately 21,000, including over 3,000 graduate students with a campus also at Lake Tahoe . We are a comprehensive, doctoral university that has been designated as a Carnegie R1 institution with very high research activity and a Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Nearly half of our students are students of color, and we are considered a Hispanic Serving Institution. In accordance with the University's land grant tier-one research institution mission, our faculty are expected to contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community through research, teaching, and service in a pluralistic environment. The University has dynamic intellectual, artistic and sports life, and a close relationship to the city of Reno Located in one of the nation\u2019s fastest-growing innovative economies, Reno is home to a thriving technology ecosystem, attracting leading companies in advanced manufacturing, clean energy, logistics, artificial intelligence, and data infrastructure. It is a growing tech and innovation hub with a diverse population and a rich cultural history that hosts an active art, music and festival scene. Known for its proximity to Lake Tahoe and the Sierra Nevada mountains, Reno is a vibrant, diverse and growing city located on the eastern slope of the Sierra Nevada, about 200 miles east of San Francisco, about 100 miles east of Sacramento and 30 miles north of the state capital, Carson City. Combined with the neighboring city of Sparks, the Reno-Sparks metropolitan area has a population of more than 500,000. Reno is only 30-45 minutes from Lake Tahoe and major ski resorts and is rated one of the top locations in the United States for living and outdoor recreation. It has an international airport with direct flights to many U.S. transportation hubs and to Mexico. For more information about the city and the surrounding area, please visit www.cityofreno.com and www.visitrenotahoe.com . The College of Liberal Arts is home to the humanities, social sciences, and arts, offering 13 academic units and 8 additional programs, museums, and centers, including the Center for Constitutional Law and the Center for Basque Studies, that foster creativity, critical thinking, and engaged citizenship. As one of the largest academic units on campus with just under 4,000 students and 240 faculty, the College combines a strong liberal arts tradition with innovative and interdisciplinary teaching, research, and community engagement, preparing students for meaningful careers and lifelong learning in an interconnected world. The College emphasizes inquiry, discovery, and openness to new ideas and perspectives and supports major scholarly research and creative activity in the three disciplines and offers a number of programs at the Lake Tahoe campus. Visit www.unr.edu/liberal-arts for more information about the college , faculty, and students . Contact Information for this position Search Chair: Prof. Jeremy Gelman, jgelman@unr.edu Search Coordinator: Karen Braun, kbraun@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Please attach the following documents to your application: 1. A complete Curriculum Vita with a list of three references. 2. A cover letter outlining qualifications and experience, research plans, and teaching experience in Public Administration, and how your qualifications would contribute to the college\u2019s mission . 3. A research-based writing sample. 4. A sample syllabus that you have constructed for a course appropriate to this position. **Finalists will be asked to share recent teaching evaluations. Open Until Filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received, or a hiring decision has been made. First review of applications will begin Oct. 1, 2026. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/XMLNAME--Nursing-Scientist--Assistant---Associate-Professor_R0153176": {"title": "(Nursing Scientist) Assistant / Associate Professor", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Orvis School of Nursing at the Carnegie R1-designated University of Nevada, Reno seeks an inaugural embedded Nurse Scientist within its academic-practice partnership with Renown Health.\u00a0The Nurse Scientist will work alongside clinical nurses, nursing leaders, and interprofessional teams to generate new knowledge, investigate the impact of nursing care, and advance nursing science. This is a role for a builder. The successful candidate will develop the relationships, infrastructure, and culture that make nursing inquiry integral to clinical practice and organizational decision-making, while advancing an independent program of scholarship.\u00a0 We welcome candidates across career stages who demonstrate a clear scholarly focus, strong methodological preparation, and the capacity to develop a funded program of research. The successful candidate will bring the vision and collaborative approach needed to build a new program, whether developing an emerging research portfolio or bringing an established body of scholarship to this opportunity.\u00a0 \u00a0 Key Responsibilities \u00a0 Build the nursing research enterprise \u00a0 Develop and implement a strategic agenda for nursing research across the partnership.\u00a0 Build sustainable infrastructure for nurse-initiated research, including consultation, project development, data access, and grant support.\u00a0 Contribute leadership to nursing research councils, committees, and strategic initiatives.\u00a0 \u00a0 Advance nursing science and outcomes research \u00a0 Develop and sustain a focused program of research aligned with your outcomes-focused area of expertise.\u00a0 Translate findings into clinical protocols, care models, policies, and delivery improvements.\u00a0 Partner with nurses and clinical leaders \u00a0 Collaborate with frontline nurses, advanced practice registered nurses, educators, nursing leaders, and quality teams to develop clinically relevant research questions.\u00a0 Serve as a trusted methodological partner, designing rigorous evaluations of nursing interventions, workforce programs, and quality initiatives.\u00a0 Develop capacity and external funding \u00a0 Mentor nurses, students, faculty, clinicians, and emerging investigators, and build collaborative research teams across clinical, academic, and community settings.\u00a0 Pursue extramural funding from federal, foundation, industry, and other sources appropriate to your career stage.\u00a0 \u00a0 Appointment and Support \u00a0 This is an open-rank faculty position with two pathways. Rank, pathway, and workload are determined by the candidate's qualifications, experience, scholarly trajectory, and career goals.\u00a0 Tenure-track. Develop or maintain an active program of research and scholarship while contributing to the teaching, mentoring, and service missions of the school. Expectations align with rank and career stage.\u00a0 Research-focused non-tenure-track. Contribute primarily through research, scholarship, capacity building, and service to the partnership. Teaching responsibilities, if any, are determined by mutual agreement and organizational need.\u00a0 Consideration for an endowed professorship may be available for an appropriately qualified candidate.\u00a0 The Nurse Scientist works in close partnership with the Chief Nurse Executive and nursing leaders across Renown Health, serves as a leader within the school's Center for Nursing Outcomes Research and Office of Research and Scholarly Impact, and has direct access to clinical leaders, frontline nurses, quality and safety teams, and research opportunities across diverse healthcare settings.\u00a0 \u00a0 Required Qualifications \u00a0 Candidates must meet all of the following:\u00a0 Earned research doctorate (PhD, DNSc, ScD or equivalent) in nursing or a related discipline\u00a0 Baccalaureate or master\u2019s degree in nursing\u00a0 Eligibility for Registered Nurse licensure in Nevada.\u00a0 Evidence of an established and focused area of scholarly inquiry focusing on nursing outcomes, or a clearly articulated emerging program of research focusing on nursing outcomes.\u00a0 Strong preparation in quantitative, qualitative, mixed-methods, implementation science, outcomes research, or related methodologies.\u00a0 Evidence of scholarly potential or productivity appropriate to career stage, such as peer-reviewed publications, presentations, research participation, pilot funding, or funded projects.\u00a0 Ability to collaborate effectively across academic and clinical environments.\u00a0 Strong analytical, project management, and communication skills.\u00a0 Demonstrated interest in building nursing research programs, partnerships, or capacity.\u00a0 \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications \u00a0 Postdoctoral fellowship or comparable advanced research experience.\u00a0 Clinically-focused graduate degree in nursing (MSN or DNP)\u00a0 Five or more years of clinical nursing practice as a licensed registered nurse or advanced practice registered nurse.\u00a0 Experience conducting research within an academic health system, integrated health system, academic-practice partnership, or large healthcare organization.\u00a0 Experience partnering with clinical nurses and nursing leaders on research, evidence-based practice, quality improvement, or program evaluation.\u00a0 Experience evaluating nursing-sensitive, patient, workforce, quality, safety, or organizational outcomes.\u00a0 Evidence of success or potential to compete for federal, foundation, industry, or other extramural funding.\u00a0 Leadership experience in nursing research, clinical scholarship, or program development.\u00a0 Faculty experience within a research-intensive university.\u00a0 \u00a0 Compensation Grade Assistant / Associate Professor \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. \u00a0 Total Compensation The total compensation package includes a competitive salary, moving allowance (if applicable), a rich retirement plan, sick leave, health insurance options that include dental and vision, life insurance, long term disability along with many other benefits. Additionally, there is a grant-in-aid educational benefit for faculty and dependents.\u00a0For more information, please visit:\u00a0 UNR Benefits \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance\u00a0Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired\u00a0faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada.\u00a0 Dual Career Program \u00a0 Faculty Appointment This position is open rank and may be appointed on either a tenure-track (TT) or non-tenure-track (NTT) pathway, commensurate with the candidate's qualifications, experience, and career goals. Faculty appointed on the tenure-track pathway will maintain an active program of research and scholarship while contributing to the teaching, mentoring, and service missions of the Orvis School of Nursing. Faculty appointed on a research-focused non-tenure-track pathway will primarily contribute through research, scholarship, research capacity building, and service to the academic practice partnership. Teaching responsibilities, if any, will be determined through mutual agreement and organizational need. \u00a0 About the Opportunity This position is a cornerstone of the Orvis School of Nursing's commitment to advancing nursing science, improving patient outcomes, and demonstrating the measurable impact of nursing care. As a leader within the Center for Nursing Outcomes Research and the Office of Research and Scholarly Impact, the Nurse Scientist will develop a nationally recognized program of scholarship while building the capacity of nurses across the health system to engage in research, evaluate outcomes, and contribute to the generation of new nursing knowledge. \u00a0 The successful candidate will be both a productive nurse scientist and a catalyst for nursing inquiry across the healthcare system, supporting the generation of new knowledge within their own area of expertise while helping nurses and nurse leaders evaluate the effectiveness and impact of nursing care across diverse clinical settings. \u00a0 Faculty rank and appointment structure will be determined based on qualifications and experience, including consideration for an endowed professorship where appropriate. Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Please attach the following documents to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter addressing qualifications and interest in the position 3) Contact Information for three professional references 4) Statement of research interests and trajectory This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/Medicine-Reno---University-of-Nevada-School-of-Medicine/Physician-Assistant-Associate-Full-Professor--PA-Studies-Department_R0153260-1": {"title": "Physician Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, PA Studies Department", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778. Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine Department of Physician Assistant (PA) Studies, located in Reno, Nevada, is seeking an individual interested in joining a fast-paced, highly motivated, energetic, and passionate team as a member of the Principal Academic Faculty. This academic position is a 12-month faculty (non-tenure), 1.0 FTE position in the Department of PA Studies. As an integral member of the PA faculty, this position has a unique opportunity to be part of a growing program with the support of the Program Director, the University of Nevada, Reno of School of Medicine, and engaged community partners. The preferred candidate has knowledge of current accreditation (ARC-PA) standards, a background in education, including creative and innovative interprofessional content delivery, and data-informed student assessment with curricular analysis to drive continuous improvement. The preferred candidate ideally has experience in PA education, clinical practice experience, and the ability to work independently and in coordination with other faculty, staff, and community partners. The Principal Faculty member must support the program\u2019s mission and adhere to the required ARC-PA accreditation process through its defined standards. The Faculty position reports to the Program Director and responsibilities may include, but to: Developing, reviewing, and revising as necessary the mission statement, goals, and competencies of the program Selecting applicants for admission to the PA program Directing courses and providing student instruction necessary to meet program goals and outcomes Evaluating student performance Academic counseling of students Participating in the availability of remedial instruction and facilitation of students\u2019 academic progress Designing, implementing, coordinating, and evaluating the curriculum Program administration, accreditation activities, continuous development, review, planning, outcomes evaluation, and overall effectiveness of the PA program Assisting in the coordination of the clinical phase, including recruitment of new clinical preceptors Participating in the recruitment of new adjunct and full-time faculty; Participating, if appropriate, in clinical activities in the Department of Family and Community Medicine or another clinical department Active participation in program committees and workgroups Service on school and university committees and councils, and, Other activities to support and enhance the program\u2019s success. Required Qualifications Possess a terminal degree (Master\u2019s or higher) in Science or Physician Assistant Studies or related field from an accredited university. Minimum of three (3) years of academic and/or clinical practice experience (>5 strongly preferred) in their field. Demonstrated leadership skills with the ability to motivate students to achieve goals and objectives. Preferred Qualifications NCCPA certified PA, eligible for licensure in Nevada. Graduate of an accredited PA Program. Prior experience in a PA faculty role for an accredited PA program is highly desired. Demonstrated ability to work collegially in a multi-disciplinary setting, working with various constituents, including students, scholars, and professionals across the healthcare fields. Demonstrated experience in curriculum development, teaching, and administration of medical education programs with a commitment to excellence and innovation. Dynamic interpersonal skills, excellent oral and written communication skills, and flexibility in responding to changing demands. Advanced capability using technology and at a minimum: PowerPoint, Excel, Microsoft Word, and Zoom. Willingness to accept the challenge of developing new initiatives and evolving practices and techniques to meet real-world challenges. Compensation Grade University Assistant/Associate/Full Professor To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules and select Academic Engineering Faculty (12-month). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0 With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR! Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits but, in order to be eligible, children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Contact Information Rubina Hackbusch at rubinah@med.unr.edu University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med) For over 50 years the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med) has trained physicians who are leading the way to 'A Healthy Nevada', nation, and world. Community-based and research-intensive, UNR Med is committed to our four-part mission of education, research, clinical service, and diversity. We show this commitment through our demonstrated core values: people are our strength, we innovate and improve, we serve with integrity, and we success with trust and respect. The University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine is an inclusive and engaged community. We recognize that diverse faculty and leaders add value to the educational experience and promote excellence in our teaching, patient care, and research missions. We seek applications from candidates with diverse backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to our mission and uphold our core values. Our mission has guided our growth in graduating the next generation of primary and specialty care physicians and medical researchers while maintaining personalized instruction. The School of Medicine has statewide outreach with a local touch providing patient care, health services, and training physicians in Reno, Nevada and in the state\u2019s rural communities. We are expanding provider education to train medical specialists as Medical Doctors as well as Speech Pathologists and Physician Assistants. We are actively expanding technologically-advanced infrastructure to keep pace with our world-class research to meet the needs of students, patients, and researchers. We seek to create an environment that serves all who enter our doors, from the physicians who teach our learners to our employees, patients, and students. Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachment(s) to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/Medicine-Reno---University-of-Nevada-School-of-Medicine/Postdoctoral-Scholar--Physiology-and-Cell-Biology_R0153228": {"title": "Postdoctoral Scholar, Physiology and Cell Biology", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778. Job Description The Dr. Sal Baker Laboratory at the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate to join our multidisciplinary research team investigating the cellular and molecular mechanisms that regulate gastrointestinal (GI) motility. Our research focuses on the pacemaker system of the gastrointestinal tract, with particular emphasis on Ca\u00b2\u207a signaling in interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC) and the mechanisms by which enteric neurons and gastrointestinal hormones regulate pacemaker activity, mechanotransduction, and smooth muscle function. We employ state-of-the-art approaches, including advanced optical imaging, genetically encoded Ca\u00b2\u207a indicators, electrophysiology, molecular biology, optogenetics/chemogenetics, and transgenic mouse models to understand how cellular signaling networks coordinate normal gut motility and contribute to gastrointestinal disorders. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to lead independent research projects while collaborating with an interdisciplinary team of physiologists, neuroscientists, and imaging specialists. Required Qualifications A Ph.D. in Physiology, Neuroscience, Cell Biology, Biomedical Engineering, Pharmacology, or a related discipline. A strong publication record in peer-reviewed journals. Experience in one or more of the following: Ca\u00b2\u207a imaging or fluorescence microscopy Electrophysiology Gastrointestinal physiology Neuroscience Molecular and cellular biology Mouse models Image analysis and quantitative data analysis Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Completion of a doctoral degree in the appropriate discipline is required. The doctoral degree must have been completed within the five years immediately preceding the first date of appointment as a postdoctoral fellow at the University. The individual cannot have held previous positions in the professional ranks. Preferred Qualifications We welcome applicants who are enthusiastic about advancing our understanding of gastrointestinal pacemaker biology and developing innovative approaches to study calcium signaling, neuronal regulation, and hormonal control of gut function. Compensation Grade Postdoctoral Scholar The budgeted salary for this position is $62,232 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules . Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Department Information Researching smooth muscle physiology and motor neurotransmission from peripheral nerves, with a focus on gastrointestinal (GI) and bladder muscle function. The research examines the role of interstitial pacemaker cells in generating and regulating motility, as well as the mechanisms underlying calcium signaling, ion channels, mechanotransduction, neuroeffector pathways, and cell-to-cell communication. A primary emphasis is placed on understanding diseases and disorders such as overactive bladder, incontinence, irritable bowel syndrome, gastroparesis, and constipation. https://med.unr.edu/directory/research-labs/baker-lab Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/Curriculum vitae 2) A cover letter describing research interests and career goals 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References 4.) Representative publications (optional) This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. Recruitment will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received, or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Graphic-Design_R0153139": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Graphic Design", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-17\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno, Department of Art, Art History & Design announces a full-time, tenure-track faculty position for an Assistant Professor, with a specialization in Graphic Design to begin July 1, 2027. We are seeking an individual with strong experience in areas of web, UI/UX, and interaction design. The ideal candidate will also have a commitment to teaching excellence, with an interest in adapting design pedagogy to meet emerging demands in the profession. Experience in the latest theory and practice in the field is highly desirable, as is participation in organizations or conferences such as AIGA The Professional Association for Design. The individual will work closely with existing faculty to teach and administer Graphic Design courses that support and connect interdisciplinary approaches, and the varied curriculum taught in the department, while bringing industry experience to the classroom. Tenure-track faculty members in the department will maintain an active research agenda and typically have a 2-2 teaching load: 40% of the time spent will be dedicated to teaching, 40% dedicated to research & creative activities; and 20% to service. The position will work as part of a team in shaping the vision of an evolving and popular program. You will work with Graphic Design BFA students on their individual studies and internship courses and help interface with local companies to maintain internship opportunities and to grow our roster of contacts for student employment. Required Qualifications A terminal degree (MFA or PhD) in Graphic Design or related field A commitment to inclusive teaching A substantial portfolio of Graphic Design work relevant to the position description Specialized skillsets in web, UI/UX, and interaction design. Familiarity with industry-standard tools such as Figma and other prototyping platforms Ability to teach Graphic Design courses at the introductory, intermediate, and advanced levels Active record of creative activity and client work Preferred Qualifications Industry experience in the Graphic Design field Successful college-level teaching experience Knowledge of other animation and modeling design softwares and processes Experience with AI tools and emerging technologies within design processes and the classroom environment. Participation in organizations/conferences dealing in theory and pedagogy surrounding teaching Graphic Design courses and industry skillsets Membership to professional organizations directly related to the Graphic Design field Compensation Assistant Professor (9-month, B-contract) Salary is competitive with a range of $68,000 to $74,000 and commensurate with related education and experience. Any tasks carried out during off contract days will be by agreement with the chair and compensated either through course release(s), overload pay or a stipend. For more information please visit: Salary Schedules Remarkable Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0 With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR (no annual for 9-month AC staff) Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options .\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The Department of Art, Art History & Design at the University of Nevada, Reno, offers a B.A. and a minor in Art History as well as undergraduate and graduate degree programs in a range of fine art emphases including Book Arts, Ceramics, Digital Media, Drawing, Graphic Arts, Painting, Photography, Printmaking, and Sculpture. The Department of Art, Art History & Design functions within the School of the Arts and the College of Liberal Arts, which provides a focused environment emphasizing critical perspectives and historical knowledge leading to an informed appreciation of the world\u2019s artistic potential. Additional opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration within the College of Liberal Arts include work with the John and Geraldine Lilley Museum of Art, the Museum Studies minor, and the Ozmen Institute for Global Studies. The Department of Art, Art History and Design has approximately 300 majors across undergraduate and graduate programs of which nearly a third are in Graphic Design. The University of Nevada, Reno is a public Research 1 land-grant institution of the Nevada System of Higher Education with a student population of approximately 21,000, including over 3,000 graduate students with a campus also at Lake Tahoe. We are a comprehensive, doctoral university that has been designated as a Carnegie R1 institution with very high research activity and a Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Nearly half of our students are students of color, and we are considered a Hispanic Serving Institution. In accordance with the University's land grant tier-one research institution mission, our faculty are expected to contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community through research, teaching, and service in a pluralistic environment. The University has dynamic intellectual, artistic and sports life, and a close relationship to the city of Reno Located in one of the nation\u2019s fastest-growing innovative economies, Reno is home to a thriving technology ecosystem, attracting leading companies in advanced manufacturing, clean energy, logistics, artificial intelligence, and data infrastructure. It is a growing tech and innovation hub with a diverse population and a rich cultural history that hosts an active art, music and festival scene. Known for its proximity to Lake Tahoe and the Sierra Nevada mountains, Reno is a vibrant, diverse and growing city located on the eastern slope of the Sierra Nevada, about 200 miles east of San Francisco, about 100 miles east of Sacramento and 30 miles north of the state capital, Carson City. Combined with the neighboring city of Sparks, the Reno-Sparks metropolitan area has a population of more than 500,000. Reno is only 30-45 minutes from Lake Tahoe and major ski resorts and is rated one of the top locations in the United States for living and outdoor recreation. It has an international airport with direct flights to many U.S. transportation hubs and to Mexico. For more information about the city and the surrounding area, please visit www.cityofreno.com and www.visitrenotahoe.com. The College of Liberal Arts is home to the humanities, social sciences, and arts, offering 13 academic units and 8 additional programs, museums, and centers, including the Center for Constitutional Law and the Center for Basque Studies, that foster creativity, critical thinking, and engaged citizenship. As one of the largest academic units on campus with just under 4,000 students and 240 faculty, the College combines a strong liberal arts tradition with innovative and interdisciplinary teaching, research, and community engagement, preparing students for meaningful careers and lifelong learning in an interconnected world. The College emphasizes inquiry, discovery, and openness to new ideas and perspectives and supports major scholarly research and creative activity in the three disciplines and offers a number of programs at the Lake Tahoe campus. Visit www.unr.edu/liberal-arts for more information about the college, faculty, and students. Contact information Search Committee Chair: Professor Kelly Chorpening kchorpening@unr.edu Search Coordinator: Deirdre Poggi, Administrative Assistant, (775) 784-6682 deirdrep@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu. Please attach the following documents in .pdf form to your application: 1) Cover letter outlining qualifications, experience, research plans, teaching experience, and how they would contribute toward the college\u2019s mission 2) Current curriculum vitae 3) Portfolio of work (20 images) 4) Two sample syllabi, and 5) List of three professional references 6) Finalists will be asked to share recent student evaluations of teaching Open Until Filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply. Position is open until filled. First review of applications will begin October 16, 2026. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Visiting-Associate-Professor---Professor-of-Constitutional-Law--Trachok-Center-for-Constitutional-Law_R0150090": {"title": "Visiting Professorship Trachok Center for Constitutional Law", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-06\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The College of Liberal Arts at the University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) invites applications for a visiting professorship (rank open) in Constitutional Law to begin Fall of 2027. This is a full-time one-year appointment with the possibility of renewal based on performance. The successful candidate will have a 2-2 course load, teaching undergraduates in constitutional law, theories of justice, and related areas, which form part of the Legal Studies major Interdisciplinary Degree in Law, Business, and Society. Both programs sit under the newly established Center for Constitutional Law, housed within the College. This Center works closely with the Boyd Law School in Las Vegas. \u00a0 The candidate will also work with the Executive Director and the Director of the Center for Constitutional Law in the implementation of the Center\u2019s mission. Prospective applicants should familiarize themselves with the mission and activities of the Center, as characterized on the Center\u2019s website: \u00a0 https://www.unr.edu/constitutional-law \u00a0 \u00a0 Required Qualifications PhD in political science, law, history, philosophy, or related academic discipline; OR a JD Demonstrated excellence in teaching constitutional law or theories of justice & related at the college level or an ABA law school For senior applicants (associate or full professor), a record of scholarly excellence in Constitutional Law, Political Science, Philosophy, or Legal Studies; for other applicants, evidence of scholarly promise in these areas \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Strong communication skills to engage with faculty, administrators, and external stakeholders Expertise both in law and in an academic discipline relevant to the focus of the Center \u00a0 Compensation The salary range for this position is $70,300 to $106,500. This is a 9-month contract. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. \u00a0 Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University. With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 \u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR\u00a0 Health insurance options including dental and vision - \u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013 \u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 \u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP \u00a0 supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. \u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 \u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. \u00a0 Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 College Information\u00a0\u00a0 The position will be housed in the Center for Constitutional Law within the College of Liberal Arts is home dynamic programs in the humanities, social sciences, and the arts include 15 academic departments, 4 museums, 3 centers, and 2 general education programs that foster creativity, critical thinking, and engaged citizenship. As one of the largest academic units on campus, the College combines a strong liberal arts tradition with innovative and interdisciplinary teaching, research, and community engagement, preparing students for meaningful careers and lifelong learning in an interconnected world. The College emphasizes inquiry, discovery, and openness to new ideas and perspectives and supports major scholarly research and creative activity in the three disciplines. \u00a0 The \u00a0 Center for Constitutional Law \u00a0 serves as a hub for research, education, and public dialogue on issues of constitutional governance and the rule of law. Visit \u00a0 https://www.unr.edu/constitutional-law \u00a0 for more information about the Center. \u00a0 Contact Information Search Chair: Associate Dean Chris Williams, \u00a0 ctw@unr.edu Search Coordinator: Elena Bubnova, \u00a0 elenab@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application, the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at \u00a0 jobs@unr.edu . \u00a0 Please attach the following attachments to your application: 1) A complete curriculum vitae that highlights qualifications for this visiting professorship 2) A one-page cover letter outlining qualifications for this position and which courses the candidate will be interested in or willing to teach 3) Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., evaluations, syllabi) 4) Contact information for three professional references \u00a0 Posting Close Time This posting will close at 12:00 am on the date listed below. The posting will no longer be available to apply to after 11:59 pm the day prior. Posting Close Date 11/6/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Part-Time-Standardized-Patient--Orvis-School-of-Nursing--LOA-_R0152824": {"title": "Part-Time Standardized Patient, Orvis School of Nursing (LOA)", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-27\nPart time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778.\u00a0 Job Description The Orvis School of Nursing at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for the position of LOA Hourly \u2013 Standardized Patient (SP), also known as a Standardized or Simulated Participant. A Standardized Patient is an individual trained to \u00a0 accurately and consistently portray a patient , family member, healthcare provider, or other participant role \u00a0 for the purpose of simulation-based education and assessment. \u00a0 Standardized Patients play an essential role in preparing nursing and healthcare students for clinical practice by creating realistic learning experiences that promote the development of clinical, communication, interpersonal, and critical-thinking skills. Through participation in simulation activities, SPs support the Orvis School of Nursing's commitment to developing compassionate, competent, and patient-centered healthcare professionals. \u00a0 Simulation activities may include health assessments, communication skills training, Objective Structured Clinical Examinations (OSCEs), physical examinations, procedural simulations, interprofessional education, and other experiential learning opportunities. \u00a0 Special Conditions of Employment \u00a0 Simulation scenarios may involve emotionally sensitive or challenging healthcare situations, including illness, grief, mental health, trauma, or end-of-life topics. \u00a0 Sessions may be \u00a0 video \u00a0 or audio recorded for educational and quality assurance purposes. \u00a0 Some scenarios may involve non-invasive physical \u00a0 examination \u00a0 contact by learners under faculty supervision. \u00a0 SPs are expected to \u00a0 maintain \u00a0 the integrity of simulation cases and not \u00a0 disclose \u00a0 scenario content to students outside of approved educational settings. \u00a0 Responsibilities Accurately portray assigned patient, family member, healthcare provider, or other simulation roles, including patient history, current health concerns, emotional responses, behaviors, physical findings, and communication styles in a standardized and reliable manner. \u00a0 Maintain consistency in role portrayal across multiple learner encounters and assessment activities. \u00a0 Provide face-to-face verbal feedback to students and faculty using structured feedback techniques and established program guidelines. \u00a0 Complete learner evaluation checklists, assessment tools, and \u00a0 other \u00a0 program documentation as assigned. \u00a0 Accept coaching and constructive feedback from faculty and simulation staff and incorporate revisions into future role portrayals. \u00a0 Participate in required training sessions, rehearsals, and simulation preparation activities (all paid at the hourly rate). \u00a0 Respond to University and simulation program communications in \u00a0 a timely \u00a0 and professional manner. \u00a0 Arrive on time, prepared, and ready to \u00a0 participate \u00a0 in all scheduled training sessions, simulations, and assessments. \u00a0 Maintain confidentiality and professionalism \u00a0 regarding \u00a0 simulation \u00a0 activities, student performance, and program operations. \u00a0 Serve as a positive representative of the University of Nevada, Reno and support the mission and values of the Orvis School of Nursing. \u00a0 Required Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent. \u00a0 Must be at least 18 years of age. \u00a0 Ability to \u00a0 work \u00a0 a flexible schedule as assigned and communicated in advance. \u00a0 Reliable transportation for in-person sessions. \u00a0 Strong communication \u00a0 and interpersonal skills . \u00a0 Ability to memorize and consistently portray assigned roles and scenarios. \u200b\u200b Please note: Students currently enrolled in the nursing program at the University of Nevada, Reno, and students enrolled in pre-requisite courses who plan to apply to the Orvis School of Nursing \u00a0 nursing \u00a0 program, are ineligible for this position. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Formal training or experience in acting, theater, communications, education, or a related field. \u00a0 Previous \u00a0 experience serving as a Standardized Patient or \u00a0 participating \u00a0 in simulation-based education. \u00a0 Experience working in healthcare, customer service, training, education, or related environments. \u00a0 Ability to provide constructive feedback and interact effectively with diverse learner populations. \u00a0 Schedule Up to \u00a0 8 hours per day , not \u00a0 to exceed \u00a0 20 hours per week . \u00a0 Work schedules vary based on program needs and may include weekdays, evenings, and occasional weekends. \u00a0 Hours are scheduled in advance according to simulation and educational programming requirements. \u00a0 Remote and in person. In-person simulation activities take place at the Orvis School of Nursing Simulation Center, University of Nevada, Reno; some training, preparation, or simulation activities may be conducted remotely. \u00a0 Compensation Grade LOA Hourly Patient Model $20.00/hour All required training, rehearsals, and case-preparation time \u00a0 is \u00a0 paid at the hourly rate. \u00a0 Contact Information Haley McNeil - hmcneil@unr.edu Impact of the Role \u00a0 Standardized Patients are integral partners in healthcare education. By creating authentic, human-centered learning experiences, SPs help \u00a0 nursing \u00a0 and healthcare students develop the clinical judgment, communication skills, empathy, and professionalism necessary to provide high-quality, patient-centered care. Through their contributions, SPs support the Orvis School of Nursing's mission to prepare the next generation of healthcare professionals to serve the needs of Nevada and beyond. Exempt No Full-Time Equivalent 50.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachment(s) to your application Resume/CV \u2013 (required) List a detailed description of the major duties that you performed as part of each job. You must demonstrate that you qualify for the position and your education and experience must be clearly documented. Cover Letter \u2013 (optional) Contact Information for Two References \u2013 (optional) Please include name, contact information, and relationship. This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. Recruitment will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date 07/27/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/Medicine-Reno---University-of-Nevada-School-of-Medicine/Assistant-Associate-Professor--Microbiology---Immunology--Tenure-Track-_R0147433": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Microbiology & Immunology (Tenure Track)", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-13\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778. Job Description The Department of Microbiology and Immunology at the University of Nevada, Reno, School of Medicine (UNR Med) seeks applicants for Assistant or Associate professor position(s) at a full-time, 12-month, tenure-track rank. As we look to expand the research landscape at UNR Med, researchers with a focus of various study areas are encouraged to apply. Areas of interest include cancer research, biosafety, infectious disease and pathogenesis, immune response, TB immunology, and microbiome research as well as cytometry. The department is situated in the recently constructed Center for Molecular Medicine that includes a state-of-the-art BSL3 facility that can support select agent research and potentially ABSL3 studies. Individuals with a strong record in cancer research are of particular interest for this position. Successful candidates will be considered for the Gloria Zhang, Ph.D., and Gordon Yan, Ph.D. endowed Professorship in Immunology. The successful applicant will have the opportunity to collaborate with clinicians and scientists at the William N. Pennington Cancer Institute of Renown Health and will be expected to lead a thriving, cutting-edge, externally funded research program that provides supportive environments for training the next generation of scientists and physicians. Incoming faculty in these roles will have a majority research focus alongside moderate teaching expectations. Responsibilities include teaching and mentoring a wide array of trainees at the graduate and professional levels, as well as and engaging in university, community, and professional service. The ideal candidate will have a demonstrated record of mentorship and a thorough understanding of the processes involved in academic biomedical research, as well as an interest in and enthusiasm for collaboration with clinician-scientists. Qualities of the ideal candidate include collegiality, resilience, adaptability, and innovativeness. Required Qualifications Ph.D., M.D., D.V.M., or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree from an accredited institution and at least 2 years of postdoctoral experience in basic or clinical immunology, infectious disease, or cancer research. Ability to develop and maintain a cutting-edge, independent research program, including securing external research support. Demonstrated ability or potential to provide first-rate teaching to graduate and medical students. Compensation Grade Assistant Professor \u2013 II Associate Professor - III To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules and select School of Medicine - Medical Academic Assistant Professor, reading along the Basic Science line. Associate Professor salary can be found on subsequent drop down menu. Compensation is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Total Compensation The total compensation package includes a negotiable competitive salary, moving allowance (if applicable), a generous retirement plan, health insurance options that include dental and vision, life insurance, long-term disability, annual and sick leave, along with many other benefits. Additionally, there is a grant-in-aid educational benefit for faculty and dependents. For more information, please visit:\u00a0 https://www.unr.edu/hr/benefits Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous annual and sick leave and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits No state income tax! E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate Remarkable Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0 With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada. Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program College and Department Information For more information on the University and the School of Medicine, please visit\u00a0 https://med.unr.edu/about . For more information about the Department of Microbiology and Immunology, please visit\u00a0 https://med.unr.edu/microbiology Reno/Tahoe Area:\u00a0 \u00a0 Located just a three-and-a-half-hour drive from San Francisco, the Reno/Tahoe area is one of the country\u2019s premier outdoor destinations. Within less than an hour's drive, you will find several renowned winter sports resorts, such as Palisades Tahoe, the host of the 1960 Winter Olympic Games, along with hundreds of mountain biking and hiking trails, including the 165-mile Tahoe Rim Trail, for year-round enjoyment. The region offers an array of festivals and cultural activities, including museums and performing arts centers, and attracts world-class entertainers. \u00a0 The 15 Happiest Places to Live in the U.S. \u2013 Outdoor magazine . \u00a0 For more information on Reno/Tahoe, please visit: \u00a0 City of Reno \u00a0 Tahoe.com \u00a0 Livability Reno \u00a0 Visit Reno Tahoe \u00a0 Davidson Academy at UNR Department Contacts Michelle Whitaker Search Coordinator mswhitaker@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) 1. Resume/CV 2. Cover Letter 3. References \u2013 Please include the names of three references along with their e-mail addresses and phone numbers in your application. University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med)\u00a0 \u00a0 For over 50 years the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med) has trained physicians who are leading the way to 'A Healthy Nevada', nation, and world. Community-based and research-intensive, UNR Med is committed to our mission. We show this commitment through our demonstrated core values: people are our strength, we innovate and improve, we serve with integrity, and we success with trust and respect. \u00a0 \u00a0 The University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine seek s applications from candidates with all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to our mission and uphold our core values. \u00a0 \u00a0 Our mission has guided our growth in graduating the next generation of primary and specialty care physicians and medical researchers while maintaining personalized instruction. The School of Medicine has statewide outreach with a local touch providing patient care, health services, and training physicians in Reno, Nevada and in the state\u2019s rural communities. We are expanding provider education to train medical specialists as Medical Doctors as well as Speech Pathologists and Physician Assistants. We are actively expanding technologically-advanced infrastructure to keep pace with our world-class research to meet the needs of students, patients, and researchers. We seek to create an environment that serves all who enter our doors, from the physicians who teach our learners to our employees, patients, and students.\u00a0 \u00a0 Molecular Biosciences Interdisciplinary Graduate Program The Microbiology and Immunology department is a key component of the interdisciplinary \u00a0 Molecular Biosciences graduate program , which brings together faculty and resources from across the university to foster collaborative research and training. This umbrella structure provides faculty in Microbiology and Immunology with access to a diverse and highly qualified pool of Ph.D. students pursuing research in areas spanning molecular biology, cell biology, genetics, microbiology, and immunology. The integrated nature of the Molecular Biosciences program enhances opportunities for mentorship, interdisciplinary collaboration, and participation in innovative graduate training initiatives, thereby supporting the development of robust, externally funded research programs. \u00a0 Renown Health & Pennington Cancer Institute Renown Health, established in 1862 as Washoe Health System, is the region\u2019s largest, locally governed, not-for-profit integrated healthcare network serving Nevada, Lake Tahoe, and northeast California. With a diverse workforce of more than 7,000 employees, Renown has fostered a longstanding culture of excellence, determination, and innovation. The organization comprises a trauma center, two acute care hospitals, a children\u2019s hospital, a rehabilitation hospital, a medical group and urgent care network, and the region\u2019s largest, locally owned not-for-profit insurance company, Hometown Health. Clinical institutes include Cancer, Heart and Vascular Heath, and Neurosciences and Robotic Surgery. Renown is currently enrolling participants in the world\u2019s largest community-based genetic population health study, the Healthy Nevada Project\u00ae. \u00a0 In July 2022, The William N. Pennington Foundation announced a $15.5 million gift to the Renown Health Foundation to establish the William N. Pennington Cancer Institute at Renown. This gift has bolstered Renown\u2019s capabilities to recruit top notch cancer providers and attract additional clinical expertise to improve the lives of people and families fighting cancer across northern Nevada. At present, over 15 new cancer providers have been added. We currently have 5 medical oncologists, 4 radiation oncologists, 2 full time breast surgeons, 2 colorectal surgeons, 2 complex GI cancer surgeons, 2 general oncologic surgeons, 2 cancer focused urologists, 1 orthopedic oncologic surgeon, 1 endocrine surgeon specializing in thyroid tumors, 1 wellness physician, and 1 palliative care physician. The plan is to use this gift to transform cancer care in Northern Nevada and to ultimately establish an NCI designated cancer center which incorporates superb care, clinical and translational research, laboratory research, effective screening and prevention and education. The recent affiliation with the University of Nevada Reno School of Medicine forms the perfect foundation for this goal. At present, we have over 60 interventional trials open at our center, 40% of them involving immunotherapy (11 in lung cancer, 8 in GI cancer, and 5 in breast cancer). Posting Close Date 12/31/2026 Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Lecturer-II--Assistant---Associate-Professor-Nursing--Non-Tenure-Track-or-Tenure-Track--All-Specialties-Considered_R0152611": {"title": "Lecturer II, Assistant / Associate Professor Nursing (Non-Tenure Track or Tenure Track) All Specialties Considered", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-07\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778.\u00a0 Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno Orvis School of Nursing invites applicants to apply for 9-month or 12-month non-tenure track position/s or tenure track position/s.\u00a0 This posting is for future openings for all specialties.\u00a0Once positions become available, applications will be reviewed. Positions Include: -Lecturer II -Assistant Professor, non-tenure track / tenure track -Associate Professor, non-tenure track / tenure track Responsibilities include teaching successfully in undergraduate and/or graduate programs according to educational preparation, expertise, and school needs. \u00a0The successful candidate will support the mission, philosophy, and objectives of the Orvis School of Nursing and the University of Nevada, Reno. Lecturer Statement The University of Nevada, Reno reserves the right to hire at any level of Lecturer (I-IV) that is appropriate for the hire.\u00a0 If you have questions about title, please inquire during the interview process. Onsite Status Position are onsite positions; though, we reserve the right to hire online instructors on rare occasion. Required Qualifications Lecturer II \u2013 Master\u2019s in Nursing or a BSN with a related field Master\u2019s.\u00a0 Assistant Professor (non-tenure track or tenure track ) \u2013 Bachelor\u2019s or Master\u2019s degree in nursing and doctoral degree (DNP, PhD, or DNSc) in nursing or related field. Associate Professor (non-tenure track or tenure track) - Bachelor\u2019s or Master\u2019s degree in nursing and doctoral degree (DNP, PhD, or DNSc) in nursing or related field. All - Registered Nurse (RN) license in the state of Nevada or eligible for licensure in Nevada. Compensation Lecturer II, 9-month or 12-month Assistant Professor, 9-month or 12-month Associate Professor, 9-month or 12-month To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules and select Administrative Faculty by Grade. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR! Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Life insurance\u00a0 \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The mission of the Orvis School of Nursing is to prepare individuals for both entry level and advanced nursing roles by providing excellent academic programs integrating recognized educational standards. The curriculum emphasizes nursing care of individuals, families & populations throughout the lifespan; among diverse cultures & beliefs; and across all socioeconomic groups in the context of their environments by providing a strong foundation for evidence-based practice, critical thinking, and leadership. As an essential part of its mission as a land grant institution, Orvis School of Nursing provides service to the state of Nevada and to the professional community at large. For more information about the Orvis School of Nursing, please visit: https://www.unr.edu/nursing Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Complete Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter outlining qualifications and future teaching and research/creative activity plans 3) Contact information for three professional/academic references This posting is open until filled For full consideration, applications must be received by November 21, 2024. Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received, or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/Extension-Humboldt---University-of-Nevada-Reno/Lecturer-II---Teaching-Assistant-Professor---Assistant-Professor--Statewide-Natural-Resources-Specialist--Extension---Winnemucca--Nevada_R0151168-1": {"title": "Lecturer II / Teaching Assistant Professor / Assistant Professor, Statewide Natural Resources Specialist, Extension - Winnemucca, Nevada", "description": "Posted: 2026-04-06\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778.\u00a0 Job Description The University of Nevada, Reno Extension is seeking a dynamic, collaborative, and innovative Extension Specialist in Natural Resources to provide statewide development, delivery, and evaluation of applied research and educational programs focused on Nevada\u2019s natural resource needs. This full-time position is based out of the Humboldt County Extension Office in Winnemucca, Nevada, while supporting statewide Extension initiatives. The successful candidate will address critical natural resource topics such as natural resource policy, rangeland restoration, vegetation, riparian, wildlife habitat management, and sustainable land stewardship. The position involves building partnerships with county offices, state and federal agencies, and local land managers to deliver impactful, research-based public educational programming. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Educational Programming Design, deliver, and evaluate evidence-based educational programs on applied natural resource topics. Utilize diverse teaching methods including workshops, demonstration sites, publications, webinars, and digital media. Provide leadership for statewide Extension initiatives, program teams, and strategic planning efforts related to natural resources. Develop collaborative relationships with county, state, tribal, and federal partners. Research and Scholarly Engage with interdisciplinary teams and external stakeholders to conduct applied research relevant to Nevada\u2019s natural resource priorities. Translate scientific research into practical tools and recommendations for diverse audiences. Identify and pursue extramural funding opportunities to support natural resource education and research initiatives. Manage grants, contracts, and reporting in compliance with University and sponsor guidelines. Publish peer-reviewed articles, Extension bulletins, and other scholarly and educational materials. Develop and provide impact reporting for Extension activities and stakeholder communications. University and Community Service Participate in University and Extension committees and working groups. Contribute to UNR Extension\u2019s mission of fostering thriving communities through engaged scholarship and education. Lecturer Statement The University of Nevada, Reno reserves the right to hire at any level of Lecturer (I-IV) or Teaching Assistant/Associate (terminal degree candidates) that is appropriate for the hire. If you have questions about the title, please inquire during the interview process. Required Qualifications Master\u2019s or Ph.D. degree from an accredited institution in natural resources, environmental science, rangeland management, ecology, or a related field. Demonstrated experience in developing and delivering educational or outreach programs. Knowledge of natural resource issues relevant to Nevada. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. in a related field. Experience working within Extension or similar outreach/education environments. Knowledge of rangeland health in arid environments. Demonstrated success in securing grants or contracts. Experience working with diverse and underserved populations, including tribal communities. Licensure A valid Class \"C\" driver's license or higher operator's license within 30 days of employment. Schedule and Travel Work a flexible schedule, with periodic evening and weekend work. Periodically travel throughout the state to assist with multi-county or statewide efforts related to subject matter expertise or attend area and statewide Extension meetings. This position involves working in both office settings and field environments. Travel within Nevada will be required Compensation Lecturer II / Teaching Assistant Professor Assistant Professor To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty. Our Benefits Great Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. The University generously matches your 19.25% contribution.\u00a0 \u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 \u201cI had no idea the values of the faculty retirement plan when I joined UNR. I discovered I was fully vested in my contributions and the company\u2019s 19.25% matching on my first day. I have watched my retirement grow over the years, not only in contributions but with investments.\u00a0 It is hard to describe how appreciative I am to feel so secure in my future\u201d \u2013 Michelle B. Perks of Working at UNR! Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous annual and sick leave and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Faculty dependents have unlimited credits, but to be eligible, children must be unmarried and under 24. They must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or the employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them in identifying career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information https://extension.unr.edu/default.aspx Search Contacts Holly Gatzke, Northern Area Director and Hiring Manager: gatzkeh@unr.edu Leslie Lynn, Search Coordinator: lynnle@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu \u00a0 Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References 4) Statement of Extension philosophy (1 page) - required This posting is open until filled. Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Full-Professor---Chair--Nutrition-Department_R0149023": {"title": "Full Professor / Chair, Nutrition Department", "description": "Posted: 2025-09-12\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778.\u00a0 Job Description The Department of Nutrition at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for the position of Department Chair at the Associate/Full Professor level. The purpose of this position is to build on the Department\u2019s strengths in nutrition. We are seeking a candidate with demonstrated success as a leader in higher education, and a distinguished record of teaching and scholarship within the area(s) of Nutrition. The Department of Nutrition is one of four academic units within the College of Agriculture, Biotechnology, and Natural Resources. The college also includes the Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station and Extension. The Department offers both BS and MS degrees in Nutrition; a Nutrition minor; and two accredited dietetic programs. The Department\u2019s cutting-edge scholarly efforts span the continuum of basic, translational, and applied research in nutrition and dietetics. The interdisciplinary nature of nutrition is reflected in the Department\u2019s successful collaborations with the School of Medicine, School of Public Health, Extension, and other academic units. The Department faculty currently consists of state-funded tenured/tenure-track positions (7.0 FTE) and lecturers (3.0 FTE); sponsor-funded positions (2.5 FTE); and part-time instructors (1.0 FTE). Opportunities and resources will become available for the incoming Chair to build the Department\u2019s PhD program in nutrition. Future goals include the building of a Food Science program in the Department. Major Responsibilities Provide visionary leadership to the Department that serves to effectively broaden its mission. Lead departmental efforts to achieve short-and long-term goals for instruction, research, and outreach consistent with that of a Carnegie R1 institution. Provide administrative oversight to all programs, including both accredited dietetic programs (DI and DPD). Support, foster, and encourage the Department\u2019s culture to continue to promote diversity, equity, and inclusion. Manage human, financial, and physical resources to maximize effectiveness and efficiencies. Work collaboratively with college and university leadership; faculty and staff; and other stakeholders to advance the mission of the Department. Represent and advocate for the Department faculty, staff, students, and programs. Recruit, develop, and retain a team of faculty, staff, and students that exhibits and values excellence and innovation. Cultivate impactful relationships with community leaders, industry, and other stakeholders. Maintain a successful research program. Required Qualifications A Ph.D. in Nutrition or a closely related discipline A record of scholarly achievement appropriate for the level of associate or full professor at an R-1 institution Evidence of success in teaching Nutrition courses at a college/university Demonstrated ability to effectively lead and manage in an academic environment Compensation Grade Associate Professor Full Professor To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty. Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0 With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow!\u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR! Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits but, in order to be eligible, children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information https://extension.unr.edu/default.aspx Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for Three Professional References This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Industrial-Engineering--Operations-Research-_R0154005": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Industrial Engineering (Operations Research)", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The School of Mechanical, Industrial, and Materials Engineering (MIME) at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Industrial Engineering, with a primary focus on Operations Research and Optimization. Anticipated start date is July 1, 2027. \u00a0 Industrial Engineering is one of the University\u2019s newest and fastest-growing engineering programs, currently with three tenure-track faculty. The successful candidate will have a rare opportunity to help shape the program\u2019s research direction, curriculum, laboratories, and industry partnerships from an early stage. \u00a0 Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: Establish an externally funded, nationally recognized research program in operations research and publish in high-impact venues. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses and develop new courses that strengthen the curriculum. Advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students. Collaborate across the School, College, and with industry and national laboratory partners. Contribute to service at the School, College, University, and professional levels. \u00a0 Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in one or more of the following areas: Operations Research, Optimization, Stochastic Processes, Simulation, Supply Chain and Logistics, Decision and Data Analytics, Engineering Economics, and Manufacturing Systems; and develop new courses that strengthen the curriculum. \u00a0 Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Industrial Engineering, Operations Research, Systems Engineering, or a closely related field. Demonstrated potential to establish an externally funded research program. Demonstrated potential for excellence in teaching and mentoring at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Effective written and oral communication skills. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Expertise in one or more of the following areas: mathematical optimization; stochastic modeling and optimization under uncertainty; simulation and digital twins; integration of machine learning and AI with optimization, including prescriptive analytics; supply chain, logistics, and transportation systems; or scheduling, resource allocation, risk analysis, and system resilience. Research that applies these methods to manufacturing, energy, critical materials, or aerospace, which are sectors central to Northern Nevada\u2019s economy. A record of scholarly publication appropriate to career stage. Experience preparing research proposals to federal agencies, industry, or other sponsors. Postdoctoral, industrial, or national laboratory experience. Interest in interdisciplinary collaboration across engineering and related fields. \u00a0 Exceptional candidates in other areas of operations research are also encouraged to apply. \u00a0 Compensation Grade Assistant Professor \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules \u00a0and select Academic Faculty (9-month). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. \u00a0 Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0 \u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow! \u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 Department Information Join the College of Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno! \u00a0 We value faculty and staff as partners in our mission to deliver outstanding education, research and engagement. Our college seeks individuals committed to: \u00a0 Engaging in impactful outreach Providing state-of-the-art education Fostering respect, inclusiveness and diversity \u00a0 Nevada Engineering is a fast-growing college, with research expenditure that has doubled over the past nine years. Supported by an Engineering Research Office, the college recorded $24 million in expenditures in FY26 and offers professional development resources to ensure success. We offer bachelor\u2019s, graduate degrees and certificates through three schools: \u00a0 Civil, Chemical and Environmental Engineering Computer Science, Electrical, and Biomedical Engineering Mechanical, Industrial, and Materials Engineering \u00a0 Our specialized facilities include the Center for Civil Engineering Earthquake Research, Cybersecurity Center, the Nanofabrication Lab in the Davidson Foundation Cleanroom and the Nevada Water Innovation Institute. Located near the Tahoe-Reno Industrial Center and offering ready access to the bustling California economy, Nevada Engineering collaborates with leading tech firms and advance research in robotics, AI, battery technology, aerospace, water systems and sustainable energy. \u00a0 In Reno, you will find world-class outdoor recreation opportunities \u2014 including skiing, hiking, biking and water sports \u2014 in nearby Lake Tahoe and throughout the northern Nevada region. \u00a0 About the School and Program Join a faculty of 32 in the School of Mechanical, Industrial, and Materials Engineering focused on advancing national efforts in space exploration, manufacturing, robotics, autonomous vehicles, as well as developing the systems and processes to best utilize our natural resources, including critical materials. Our school is home to George W. Gillemot Aerospace Program, established in 2023 with a $36 million gift. The new $100-million William N. Pennington Engineering Building houses an ISO-5 (CLASS 100) cleanroom. School resources include access to the University\u2019s High Performance Computing facility and secured space for the new aerospace and industrial engineering programs. In addition, the school is developing new Industrial Engineering facilities to support a Smart Factory/Flexible Manufacturing System learning environment, including space for industrial automation equipment, student work areas, vision systems, and related manufacturing and logistics training platforms.\u00a0 \u00a0 The Industrial Engineering program prepares students to design and optimize complex systems involving people, materials, information, equipment, and energy. The School is building a Smart Factory / Flexible Manufacturing Systems laboratory with industrial automation, machine vision, and manufacturing and logistics equipment to support hands-on learning and research in operations research, industrial analytics, and supply chains. \u00a0 Contact Information Questions about the position may be directed to: Dr. Pradeep L. Menezes, Professor and Search Committee Chair School of Mechanical, Industrial, and Materials Engineering, University of Nevada, Reno at pmenezes@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . \u00a0 Please attach the following documents to your application Resume/CV Cover Letter Contact Information for three professional references Research statement describing accomplishments, future plans, and potential funding sources Teaching statement describing teaching philosophy, courses the applicant is prepared to teach, and ideas for new course development Statement on mentoring and student success \u00a0 Full Consideration Review of applications will begin immediately. For full consideration, applications should be received by December 15, 2026 . The position will remain open until filled. \u00a0 This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Lecturer---Teaching-Assistant-Professor--Industrial-Engineering_R0153935": {"title": "Lecturer / Teaching Assistant Professor, Industrial Engineering", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Industrial Engineering Program in the College of Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a non-tenure-track position at the level of Lecturer I, Lecturer II, or Teaching Assistant Professor with anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. As part of a newly established and growing Industrial Engineering program offering B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. degrees, this position provides an exciting opportunity to contribute to the development and delivery of Industrial Engineering curricula and to help build a growing academic program. \u00a0 We seek candidates with a strong commitment to excellence in teaching and the ability to contribute broadly across the Industrial Engineering curriculum. Candidates should be prepared to teach the IE senior capstone experience along with core Industrial Engineering courses and labs in areas such as operations research and optimization, simulation, supply chain and logistics, quality engineering, and engineering economic analysis. \u00a0 Lecturer Statement The University of Nevada, Reno reserves the right to hire at any level of Lecturer (I-IV) or Teaching\u00a0Assistant/Associate/Professor\u00a0(terminal degree candidates) that is appropriate for the hire.\u00a0 If you have questions about the title, please inquire during\u00a0the\u00a0interview\u00a0process. \u00a0 Appointment level will be determined by qualifications and experience. \u00a0 Required Qualifications Lecturer I \u2013 Master\u2019s degree in Industrial Engineering or closely related field Lecturer II \u2013 Master\u2019s degree in Industrial Engineering or closely related field Teaching Assistant Professor - PhD in Industrial Engineering or closely related field \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications: Ph.D. in Industrial Engineering or a closely related field Significant industrial experience - at least 5 years \u00a0 Compensation Lecturer I, 9-month, non-tenure track \u00a0\u00a0 Lecturer II, 9-month, non-tenure track Teaching Assistant Professor, 9-month, non-tenure track \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules and select Base Salary for Academic Faculty, Engineering (9- month). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University.\u00a0 \u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested your first day.\u00a0Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow! \u00a0 All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada.\u00a0 Perks of Working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options .\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 Department Information Join a faculty of 32 in the School of Mechanical, Industrial, and Materials Engineering focused on advancing national efforts in space exploration, manufacturing, robotics, autonomous vehicles, as well as developing the systems and processes to best utilize our natural resources, including critical materials. Our school is home to George W. Gillemot Aerospace Program, established in 2023 with a $36 million gift. The new $100-million William N. Pennington Engineering Building houses an ISO-5 (CLASS 100) cleanroom. School resources include access to the University\u2019s High Performance Computing facility and secured space for the new aerospace and industrial engineering programs. In addition, the school is developing new Industrial Engineering facilities to support a Smart Factory/Flexible Manufacturing System learning environment, including space for industrial automation equipment, student work areas, vision systems, and related manufacturing and logistics training platforms. \u00a0 College of Engineering Join the College of Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno. We value faculty and staff as partners in our mission to deliver outstanding education, research, and engagement. Our college seeks individuals committed to: \u00a0 Engaging in impactful outreach Providing state-of-the-art education Fostering respect, inclusiveness, and diversity \u00a0 Nevada Engineering is a fast-growing college, with research expenditure that has doubled over the past nine years. The college recorded $24 million in expenditures in FY26 and offers professional development resources to ensure success. We offer bachelor\u2019s and graduate degrees and certificates through three schools: \u00a0 Civil, Chemical, and Environmental Engineering Computing, Electrical, and Biomedical Engineering Mechanical, Industrial, and Materials Engineering \u00a0 Specialized facilities include the Center for Civil Engineering Earthquake Research, Cybersecurity Center, a Nanofabrication Lab and the Nevada Water Innovation Institute. Located near the Tahoe-Reno Industrial Center and offering ready access to the bustling California economy, Nevada Engineering collaborates with leading tech firms and advance research in robotics, AI, battery technology, aerospace, water systems and sustainable energy. In Reno, you will find world-class outdoor recreation opportunities \u2014 including skiing, hiking, biking and water sports \u2014 in nearby Lake Tahoe and throughout the northern Nevada region. Learn more at unr.edu/engineering \u00a0 \u00a0 Contact Information for this position: Search Chair: Angelina Padilla, angelinapadilla@unr.ed Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . \u00a0 Please attach the following documents to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Contact Information for three professional references 4) Teaching Statement \u00a0 Review of applications will begin on December 4, 2026 and continue until the positions are filled. \u00a0 \u00a0 This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/Medicine-Reno---University-of-Nevada-School-of-Medicine/Assistant-Professor--Physiology-and-Cell-Biology_R0153882": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Physiology and Cell Biology", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778. Job Description The Department of P hysiology and Cell Biology at the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med) invites applications for two tenure track faculty positions at the Assistant Professor level. These are full-time (12-month) state-funded positions that represent an exceptional opportunity for outstanding candidates to join UNR Med's robust research community that extends across multiple research and clinical departments. \u00a0 The ideal candidate\u2019s research will utilize state-of-the-art techniques to advance our understanding of the cellular and molecular basis of human health and disease in areas that complement the longstanding tradition of excellence in gastrointestinal and cardiovascular biomedical science in the department. Areas of particular interest include neuroimmune crosstalk, cancer, women\u2019s health, neural control of organ systems, glial biology, and vascular biology. The successful applicant is expected to establish and maintain an active externally-funded research program, produce high quality publications in peer-reviewed journals, engage in University, community, and professional service, and contribute to the highly interactive and collaborative environment within the Physiology and Cell Biology Department at UNR Med.\u00a0 \u202f\u00a0 The successful applicant must establish and maintain a vigorous, extramurally funded research program in their area of expertise, and contribute to teaching graduate and medical students. Laboratory space, start-up funds, and salary will be competitive and commensurate with academic rank and experience.\u202f\u202f\u00a0 Required Qualifications Applicants must hold a PhD, DO, MD, DVM or equivalent degree, with postdoctoral experience Strong record of research productivity including lead author publications in peer-reviewed journals Candidates must provide evidence of their ability to obtain stage-appropriate funding and have clear plans for establishing an independent research program. Compensation Grade Assistant Professor \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit:\u00a0 Salary Schedules and select School of Medicine - Medical Academic Assistant Professor, reading along the Basic Science line. Associate Professor salary can be found on subsequent drop-down menu. Compensation is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. \u00a0 \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Contact Information For questions rearding this position, please contact Dr. Brian Gulbransen, Chair of the Department of Physiology and Cell Biology and Search Committee Chair, at bgulbransen@unr.edu . \u00a0 University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med) For over 50 years the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine (UNR Med) has trained physicians who are leading the way to 'A Healthy Nevada', nation, and world. Community-based and research-intensive, UNR Med is committed to our four-part mission of education, research, clinical service, and diversity. We show this commitment through our demonstrated core values: people are our strength, we innovate and improve, we serve with integrity, and we success with trust and respect. The University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine is an inclusive and engaged community. We recognize that diverse faculty and leaders add value to the educational experience and promote excellence in our teaching, patient care, and research missions. We seek applications from candidates with diverse backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to our mission and uphold our core values. Our mission has guided our growth in graduating the next generation of primary and specialty care physicians and medical researchers while maintaining personalized instruction. The School of Medicine has statewide outreach with a local touch providing patient care, health services, and training physicians in Reno, Nevada and in the state\u2019s rural communities. We are expanding provider education to train medical specialists as Medical Doctors as well as Speech Pathologists and Physician Assistants. We are actively expanding technologically-advanced infrastructure to keep pace with our world-class research to meet the needs of students, patients, and researchers. We seek to create an environment that serves all who enter our doors, from the physicians who teach our learners to our employees, patients, and students. \u00a0 \u00a0 Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note that once you submit your application, the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, or additional documents sections of the application will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, before the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Research Statement - statement of past accomplishments and goals for independent research program (three-pages maximum) 4) Contact Information for Three Professional References This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/Medicine-Reno---University-of-Nevada-School-of-Medicine/Postdoctoral-Scholar--Neurovascular_R0154052": {"title": "Postdoctoral Scholar, Neurovascular", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778. Job Description We are seeking for a postdoctoral fellow to join a neurovascular imaging laboratory at the University of Nevada Reno School of Medicine. The applicant will have deep understanding of how neural cells communicating with the vasculature to regulate blood flow matching metabolic demands. The successful applicant will be responsible for conducting independent experiments in the study of neurovascular coupling and cerebral blood flow regulation. The applicant will perform experiments involving delicate surgeries, complex pharmacological manipulations and highly technical imaging. We are looking for someone who has extensive knowledge of vascular biology and/or neuroscience. The applicant will require to write manuscripts and present data at national and international conferences. The position requires extensive technical skills, ability to adapt, troubleshoot experimental protocols, and some scientific creativity. \u00ad\u00ad\u00ad\u00ad\u00ad\u00adSome computer skills including Matlab is highly recommended. Our lab utilizes two-photon fluorescent light microscope (TPFLM) to understand how neural cells communicate with vascular cells to regulate blood flow to match with metabolic needs. The unique strengths of our approach include state-of-the art TPFLM, awake in vivo mouse model, and advanced techniques to probe different physiological parameters to understand the interaction among members of the neurovascular units and how local signals are integrated to globally regulate blood flow distribution to match moment-to-moment brain activity. Required Qualifications Ph.D. degree in vascular biology or neuroscience. The applicant must have demonstrated ability to perform in vitro and/or in vivo experiments; demonstrated experience in imaging and electrophysiology. Must have the ability to troubleshoot complex technical experiments and change procedures as required. Must be able to work independently and think critically about the projects. Must have demonstrated ability to work with animal models. Demonstrated strong communication skills. Completion of a doctoral degree in the appropriate discipline is required. The doctoral degree must have been completed within the five years immediately preceding the first date of appointment as a postdoctoral fellow at the University. The individual cannot have held previous positions in the professional ranks. Compensation Grade Postdoctoral Scholar To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules . Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Department Information The Tran Lab examines the cellular communication between different members of the neurovascular unit and the role it plays in regulating blood flow control and the functionality of neural cells in the brain. Through their research, the lab aims to uncover potential targets for diagnosis and treatment of diseases such as stroke and dementia. https://med.unr.edu/directory/research-labs/tran-lab Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Curriculum vitae 2) Cover letter describing previous research experience, scientific interests, and career goals 3)\u00a0Contact information for three professional references (name, email and phone) \u00a0 This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/Medicine-Reno---University-of-Nevada-School-of-Medicine/Postdoctoral-Scholar--Physiology-and-Cell-Biology--Gut-Brain-Science-Laboratory-_R0153881": {"title": "Postdoctoral Scholar, Physiology and Cell Biology (Gut\u2013Brain Science Laboratory)", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . For UNR Med professional job postings, please contact the Office of Professional Recruitment at (775) 784-6778. Job Description The Gut\u2013Brain Science Laboratory, led by Dr. Rajan Singh in the Department of Physiology and Cell Biology at the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine, is seeking a highly motivated and collaborative Postdoctoral Scholar to join an NIH-funded research program investigating the cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying gastroparesis, gastrointestinal dysmotility, intestinal barrier dysfunction, and disorders of gut\u2013brain interaction. The successful candidate will contribute to a newly funded NIH R01 investigating how gut-resident macrophages regulate gastric neuromuscular function and epithelial barrier integrity in gastroparesis. The project integrates mechanistic studies in genetically engineered mouse models with translational studies using human gastric and duodenal biopsies. The research program combines neurogastroenterology, mucosal immunology, gastrointestinal physiology, molecular biology, single-cell transcriptomics, advanced imaging, and translational human tissue studies to identify disease mechanisms and develop macrophage-directed therapeutic strategies. The postdoctoral scholar will have opportunities to develop an independent research direction within the overall program, publish high-impact manuscripts, present findings at national and international scientific meetings, participate in multidisciplinary collaborations, and develop skills needed for progression toward an independent research career. \u00a0 Research Responsibilities The successful candidate will work closely with Dr. Singh and collaborating investigators to: Design and conduct mechanistic studies examining the role of muscularis and mucosal macrophages in gastroparesis, gastrointestinal motility, and epithelial barrier dysfunction. Conduct in vivo gastrointestinal physiology studies, including gastric emptying and gastrointestinal transit measurements. Perform intestinal and gastric barrier-function studies using approaches including Ussing chamber/TEER and permeability assays. Isolate and characterize gut-resident immune-cell populations from gastrointestinal tissues. Perform flow cytometry, immunohistochemistry, confocal microscopy, qPCR, Western blotting, ELISA and related molecular and cellular assays. Participate in single-cell RNA sequencing, miRNA profiling and CyTOF-based immune phenotyping, including preparation of biological samples and interpretation of resulting datasets. Participate in translational studies involving coded human gastric and duodenal biopsy specimens obtained through collaborating clinical investigators. Prepare manuscripts, abstracts, and present research findings at laboratory meetings and scientific conferences. Collaborate with faculty, research staff, students, core facilities, and external investigators involved in the program. \u00a0 Required Qualifications Ph.D., M.D./Ph.D., or equivalent doctoral degree in physiology, cell and molecular biology, immunology, neuroscience, gastroenterology, pharmacology, biomedical sciences or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated experience in experimental biomedical research. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary research environment. Evidence of scientific productivity, such as peer-reviewed publications, manuscripts or substantial doctoral research accomplishments. Completion of a doctoral degree in the appropriate discipline is required. The doctoral degree must have been completed within the five years immediately preceding the first date of appointment as a postdoctoral fellow at the University. The individual cannot have held previous positions in the professional ranks. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Experience in one or more of the following areas is highly desirable: Gastrointestinal physiology, neurogastroenterology or GI motility Macrophage biology, mucosal immunology or neuroimmune interactions Transgenic/conditional mouse models and mouse colony management In vivo mouse experimentation Flow cytometry, immunohistochemistry and confocal fluorescence microscopy, single-cell RNA sequencing, CyTOF or high-dimensional immune profiling, Ussing chamber, TEER or epithelial permeability measurements Molecular biology techniques including RNA isolation, qPCR, Western blotting and ELISA \u00a0 Candidates are not expected to possess expertise in every technique listed above. Applicants with strong training in gastrointestinal physiology, immunology, neuroscience, molecular biology or related areas who are interested in developing expertise in neurogastroenterology are particularly encouraged to apply. \u00a0 Compensation Grade Postdoctoral Scholar To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules . Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Department Information https://med.unr.edu/directory/departments/physiology-cell-biology Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Curriculum vitae 2) Cover letter describing previous research experience, scientific interests, and career goals 3)\u00a0Contact information for three professional references 4) Representative publications This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Lecturer-Teaching-Assistant-Professor--Sociology-and-Cultural-Analysis_R0153919": {"title": "Lecturer/Teaching Assistant Professor, Sociology and Cultural Analysis", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The Department of Sociology and Cultural Analysis within the College of Liberal Arts at the University of Nevada, Reno invites applications for a Lecturer or Teaching Assistant Professor position to begin July 1, 2027. This is a 9-month, non-tenure-track, continuing appointment with opportunities for promotion. This position carries an 80% teaching responsibility, with the remaining 20% dedicated to service and professional development. \u00a0 We are seeking candidates with a demonstrated record in teaching excellence to teach core courses in LGBTQ+, Latinx-identity, Native American Culture, Sociology, and/or Women\u2019s Studies. We seek candidates to teach our foundation courses: WMST 101: Intro Women\u2019s Studies; ETS 280: Native American Culture; GRI 103: Power, Identity, & Social Justice; GRI 230: Intro to LGBTQ Studies; GRI 216: Thinking Sex and Gender; GRI/SOC 272: Latinx Identities and Cultures; GRI 360 LGBTQ Community Histories; SOC 101 Prin of Sociology; SOC 235 Climate Change; SOC 250 Sociology of Sexualities; and/or CH 203: American Experiences and Constitutional Change. The position carries a 4/4 teaching load, though there may be course release opportunities for significant service duties. There are also opportunities for off-term teaching for additional compensation. The Department of Sociology and Cultural Analysis equips students with the critical thinking, persuasive writing, and analytical skills necessary to engage thoughtfully with issues of public concern through rigorous sociological and interdisciplinary inquiry. Our curriculum prepares students for careers in education, social services, public policy, nonprofit leadership, law, health, and community advocacy. The department offers courses in sociology, gender and women\u2019s studies, LGBTQ+ studies, ethnic studies, and race and identity, with particular attention to power, inequality, and social justice. Our courses draw on a range of theoretical frameworks and methodological approaches, and many center the histories, cultures, and lived experiences of communities that have been underrepresented in higher education. Faculty in the department teach and conduct research in social movements, religion and globalization, health and well-being, families and masculinities, gender-based violence and reproductive justice, race, ethnicity, and immigration, Indigenous and decolonial studies, law and justice, and social research methods. Lecturer Statement The University of Nevada, Reno reserves the right to hire at any level of Lecturer (II) or Teaching Assistant (terminal degree candidates) that is appropriate for the hire.\u00a0 If you have questions about title, please inquire during the interview process. \u00a0 Required Qualifications Lecturer - MA in Feminist Studies, American Studies, Ethnic Studies, Sociology, Native American Studies or a related field; OR Teaching Assistant Professor - PhD. in Feminist Studies, American Studies, Ethnic Studies, Sociology, Native American Studies or a related field Demonstrated excellence or clear evidence of potential for excellence in teaching at least some courses or equivalent: WMST 101: Intro Women\u2019s Studies, ETS 280: Native American Culture, GRI 103: Power, Identity, & Social Justice, GRI 230: Intro to LGBTQ Studies, GRI 216: Thinking Sex and Gender, GRI/SOC 272: Latinx Identities and Cultures, GRI 360 LGBTQ Community Histories, SOC 101 Prin of Sociology, SOC 235 Climate Change, SOC 250 Sociology of Sexualities; and/or CH 203: American Experiences and Constitutional Change. Candidates must possess experience working with diverse and underrepresented students at the college level. Successful candidates must demonstrate an ability to collaborate with others on curriculum design and implementation while supporting the university\u2019s commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, access, and social justice. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Experience and promise as an excellent teacher Interest in developing a pedagogical agenda A track record of campus programming and/or community engagement Experience working with and teaching diverse groups of students, including evidence of a commitment to a broad involvement with diversity work on campus Compensation Grade Lecturer/Teaching Assistant Professor (9-month, B-contract). Salary is competitive with a range of $63,000 to $65,000 and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement! Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University. \u00a0With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day. Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow! \u00a0All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada. \u00a0 Perks of working at UNR Health insurance options including dental and vision - Health Insurance Generous sick leave, long term disability and life insurance \u2013 Faculty Benefits E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers annual or semester memberships and spouse/domestic partner membership options.\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Reno is proud to be a University town! Many local businesses offer discounts to WolfCard holders Mountain EAP supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University. Faculty Senate No state income tax! Grants-in-aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program Department Information The Department of Sociology and Cultural Analysis examines how societies are organized, how they change, and how systems of power and inequality shape everyday life. Bringing together two longstanding strengths of the College of Liberal Arts \u2013 the empirical study of social life and the critical study of identity and power \u2013 we offer students an interdisciplinary approach to understanding the contemporary world. \u00a0 Sociology and Cultural Analysis, together, allow students to ask not only how the social world is organized but also how it is experienced, interpreted, challenged and changed. We believe scholarship should extend beyond the university and contribute meaningfully to public life. \u00a0 Our students learn to study society with both rigor and care. They develop research skills to investigate inequality and critical frameworks to analyze culture and power, and communication skills to engage diverse audiences in classrooms, communities and policy settings. Graduates leave prepared for careers and advanced study in education, law, healthcare, research, public policy, advocacy, community organizing and many other fields. Additional information about the Department can be found at https://www.unr.edu/sociology-cultural-analysis . \u00a0 College and University Information As Nevada\u2019s flagship institution, the University of Nevada, Reno is a public Research 1 land-grant institution of the Nevada System of Higher Education with a student population of approximately 21,000, including over 3,000 graduate students with a campus also at Lake Tahoe . We are a comprehensive, doctoral university that has been designated as a Carnegie R1 institution with very high research activity and a Carnegie Community Engagement Classification. Nearly half of our students are students of color, and we are considered a Hispanic Serving Institution. In accordance with the University\u2019s land grant tier-one research institution mission, our faculty are expected to contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community through research, teaching, and service in a pluralistic environment. The University has dynamic Intellectual, artistic and sports life, and a close relationship to the city of Reno Located in one of the nation\u2019s fastest-growing innovative economies, Reno is home to a thriving technology ecosystem, attracting leading companies in advanced manufacturing, clean energy, logistics, artificial intelligence, and data infrastructure. It is a growing tech and innovation hub with a diverse population and a rich cultural history that hosts an active art, music and festival scene. Known for its proximity to Lake Tahoe and the Sierra Nevada mountains, Reno is a vibrant, diverse and growing city located on the eastern slope of the Sierra Nevada, about 200 miles east of San Francisco, about 100 miles east of Sacramento and 30 miles north of the state capital, Carson City. Combined with the neighboring city of Sparks, the Reno-Sparks metropolitan area has a population of more than 500,000. Reno is only 30-45 minutes from Lake Tahoe and major ski resorts and is rated one of the top locations in the United States for living and outdoor recreation. It has an international airport with direct flights to many U.S. transportation hubs and to Mexico. For more information about the city and the surrounding area, please visit www.cityofreno.com and www.visitrenotahoe.com . \u00a0 The College of Liberal Arts is home to the humanities, social sciences, and arts, offering 13 academic units and 8 additional programs, museums, and centers, including the Center for Constitutional Law and the Center for Basque Studies, that foster creativity, critical thinking, and engaged citizenship. As one of the largest academic units on campus with just under 4,000 students and 240 faculty, the College combines a strong liberal arts tradition with innovative and interdisciplinary teaching, research, and community engagement, preparing students for meaningful careers and lifelong learning in an interconnected world. The College emphasizes inquiry, discovery, and openness to new ideas and perspectives and supports major scholarly research and creative activity in the three disciplines and offers a number of programs at the Lake Tahoe campus. Visit www.unr.edu/liberal-arts for more information about the college, faculty, and students. \u00a0 Contact Information for this position Search Chair: Prof. Lydia Huerta Moreno lhuertam@unr.edu \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Search Coordinator: Tanya Nawrocki tnawrocki@unr.edu Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application. Any additional required attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . \u00a0 Please attach the following documents to your application: A complete Curriculum Vita Cover letter outlining qualifications, experience, teaching experience, and how they would contribute toward the college\u2019s mission . Reference contact information for three professional references. Teaching Philosophy Statement outlining evidence of teaching excellence and engagement with diverse student populations, and community engagement. Four Sample Course Syllabi Finalists will be asked to share recent teaching evaluations. \u00a0 This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. First review of applications will begin December 11, 2026. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University of Nevada, Reno"}, "https://nshe.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UNR-external/job/University-of-Nevada-Reno---Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Aerospace-Engineering_R0154006": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Aerospace Engineering", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nThe University of Nevada, Reno (UNR) appreciates your interest in employment at our growing institution. We want your application process to go smoothly and quickly. Final applications must be submitted prior to the close of the recruitment. If you need assistance or have questions regarding the application process, please contact our recruitment helpline at (775) 784-1495 or jobs@unr.edu . Job Description The School of Mechanical, Industrial, and Materials Engineering and the George W. Gillemot Aerospace Program at the University of Nevada, Reno seeks candidates for a tenure track faculty opening in Aerospace Engineering. This tenure-track position will be responsible for teaching, research, and service responsibilities, as described below. The anticipated starting date is July 1, 2027. Based on the University\u2019s mission as a land grant institution, faculty duties will include undergraduate and graduate teaching, supervision of graduate students, performance of research, development of externally funded research projects, and university and professional service. With a recent $36M donation from the Gillemot Foundation , the department expanded its aerospace research and education, including the creation of comprehensive BS, MS, and PhD degree programs in Aerospace Engineering and hiring of four AE faculty since Spring 2025.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Required Qualifications Doctoral Degree in Aerospace Engineering or another closely related field. Candidates whose specialization and interests complement existing research and instructional strengths in the department and affiliated programs will be preferred. Preferred Qualifications We are seeking candidates working across a broad range of aerospace engineering areas. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: aeronautics, including aerodynamics, propulsion, fluid mechanics, and flight dynamics and control; aerospace structures and materials; and space systems, including orbital mechanics and spacecraft guidance, navigation, and control.\u00a0 Candidates working across disciplinary boundaries are especially encouraged to apply. Candidates are sought who can perform outstanding research and teach relevant AE and ME courses. \u00a0 Compensation Grade Assistant Professor, tenure-track, 9-month \u00a0 To view the salary schedule for this position, please visit: Salary Schedules and select Academic Faculty (Engineering 9-month contract). Salary is competitive and commensurate with related education and experience. Remarkable Retirement Our retirement plan is beyond compare. Your 19.25% contribution is generously matched by the University. With the faculty retirement plan, you are 100% vested on your first day. Sit back and watch your retirement dollars grow! All permanent employees on an annual \"A\" or \"B\" contract who are employed at least 50% full-time are eligible to participate in the NSHE retirement program unless they are members of PERS of Nevada. Perks of Working at UNR! Health insurance options including dental and vision -\u00a0 Health Insurance Generous sick leave, life insurance, and long-term disability \u2013\u00a0 Faculty Benefit E. L. Wiegand Fitness Center offers an annual or semester membership and family membership options.\u00a0\u00a0 E.L. Wiegand Fitness Center Mountain EAP \u00a0supports employees (and eligible dependents) through life's difficult moments. Mountain EAP is located in Reno and specializes in counseling and advising services for personal or interpersonal issues. Faculty Senate is the principal representing body for faculty. Its membership includes representatives from each academic and administrative major unit of the University.\u00a0 Faculty Senate No state income tax! \u00a0 Grants-in-Aid for Faculty Employees The University is proud to provide a reduced-rate tuition benefit to faculty and qualified dependents. Faculty can take up to six credits per semester at a reduced rate. Dependents of faculty have unlimited credits, but in order to be eligible children must be unmarried and under the age of 24 and must receive at least 50% of their financial support from the employee and/or employee\u2019s spouse or domestic partner.\u00a0 Faculty Grants-in-Aid \u00a0 Faculty Dual Career Assistance Program The University of Nevada, Reno recognizes the importance of addressing dual-career couples\u2019 professional needs. We offer a dual career assistance program to newly hired faculty spouses/partners that provides resources and assists them to identify career opportunities in Northern Nevada. Dual Career Assistance Program \u00a0 School of Mechanical, Industrial, and Materials Engineering The George W. Gillemot Aerospace Program was established in 2023 with a $36 million gift from the George W. Gillemot Foundation. It is housed in the School of Mechanical, Industrial, and Materials Engineering where our faculty focuses on advancing national efforts in space exploration, manufacturing, robotics, autonomous vehicles, as well as developing the systems and processes to best utilize our natural resources, including critical materials. School resources include access to the University\u2019s High Performance Computing facility and the new $100-million William N. Pennington Engineering Building houses an ISO-5 (CLASS 100) cleanroom. In addition, the School is developing new Industrial Engineering facilities to support a Smart Factory/Flexible Manufacturing System learning environment, including space for industrial automation equipment, student work areas, vision systems, and related manufacturing and logistics training platforms.\u00a0 \u00a0 About the College of Engineering Join the College of Engineering at the University of Nevada, Reno. We value faculty and staff as partners in our mission to deliver outstanding education, research and engagement. Our college seeks individuals committed to: Engaging in impactful outreach Providing state-of-the-art education Fostering respect, inclusiveness, and diversity Nevada Engineering is a fast-growing college, with research expenditure that has doubled over the past nine years. Supported by an Engineering Research Office, the college recorded $24 million in expenditures in FY26 and offers professional development resources to ensure success. We offer bachelor\u2019s, graduate degrees and certificates through three schools: Civil, Chemical, and Environmental Engineering Computer Science, Electrical, and Biomedical Engineering Mechanical, Industrial, and Materials Engineering \u00a0 Our specialized facilities include the Center for Civil Engineering Earthquake Research, Cybersecurity Center, the Nanofabrication Lab in the Davidson Foundation Cleanroom and the Nevada Water Innovation Institute. Located near the Tahoe-Reno Industrial Center and offering ready access to the bustling California economy, Nevada Engineering collaborates with leading tech firms and advance research in robotics, AI, battery technology, aerospace, water systems and sustainable energy. In Reno, you will find world-class outdoor recreation opportunities, including skiing, hiking, biking and water sports in nearby Lake Tahoe and throughout the northern Nevada region. Learn more at unr.edu/engineering \u00a0 Reno Area Information The Reno area has an ethnically diverse population of over 500,000, with growing engineering, aerospace, high-tech, and arts communities, and an international airport 10 minutes from the University. Situated on the banks of the Truckee River on the eastern slope of the Sierra Nevada, the city is 45 minutes from Lake Tahoe and its magnificent recreational opportunities, 3 hours from Yosemite National Park, and 4 hours from San Francisco and the Pacific coast. \u00a0 Contact Information Search Committee Chair, Matteo Aureli at maureli@unr.edu \u00a0 \u00a0 Exempt Yes Full-Time Equivalent 100.0% Required Attachment(s) Please note, once you submit your application, the only attachment/s viewable to you will be the attachment/s to the resume/CV section of the application Any additional required attachment/s attachment/s to the cover letter, references, additional documents sections of the application, will not be viewable to you after you submit your application. All uploaded attachment/s will be on the application for the committee to review. To request updates to attachments, prior to the committee review of applications, please contact the candidate helpdesk at jobs@unr.edu . \u00a0 Attach the following attachments to your application 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter with statement of qualifications 3) Statement of research philosophy and future research plans 4) Statement of teaching philosophy and interests. Include description of the courses of the aerospace engineering curriculum interested in teaching and the development of any new courses. 5) Contact information for Three Professional References\u00a0 Full Consideration For full consideration, applications will be reviewed beginning January 1, 2027. This posting is open until filled Qualified individuals are encouraged to apply immediately. This search will close without notice when a sufficient number of applications are received or a hiring decision has been made. Posting Close Date Note to Applicant A background check will be conducted on the candidate(s) selected for hire. \u00a0\u00a0 HR will attempt to verify academic credentials upon receipt of hiring documents. If the academic credentials cannot be verified, HR will notify the faculty member that an official transcript of their highest degree must be submitted within thirty days of the faculty member\u2019s first day of employment. \u00a0\u00a0 References will be contacted at the appropriate phase of the recruitment process. Applicants hired on a federal contract may be subject to E-Verify. \u00a0\u00a0 As part of the hiring process, applicants for positions in the Nevada System of Higher Education may be required to demonstrate the ability to perform job-related tasks. \u00a0\u00a0 For positions that require driving, evidence of a valid driver's license will be required at the time of employment and as a condition of continued employment. \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Schedules are subject to change based on organizational needs. The University of Nevada, Reno is committed to providing a place of work and learning free of discrimination on the basis of a person\u2019s age (40 or older), disability, whether actual or perceived by others (including service-connected disabilities), gender (including pregnancy related conditions), military status or military obligations, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, national origin, race (including hair texture and protected hairstyles such as natural hairstyles, afros, bantu knots, curls, braids, locks and twists), color, or religion (protected classes).\u00a0 About Us The University of Nevada, Reno is a leading public research university committed to the promise of a future powered by knowledge. Founded in 1874 as Nevada\u2019s original land-grant university, the University serves more than 23,000 undergraduate and graduate students from all 50 states and 63 countries. Classified by the Carnegie\u00ae Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as an R1 (\u201cVery High Research\u201d) university, it is also recognized in the Carnegie\u00ae Community Engagement classification. The University is also ranked by U.S. News & World Report among the \u201cBest National Universities\u201d and \u201cBest National Public Universities.\u201d It also ranks in the top tier of the WSJ/Times Higher Education World University Rankings and the New York Times\u2019 \u201cTop Colleges for Economic Diversity.\u201d Since 2009, nearly $1 billion has been has invested in advanced labs, facilities, and residence halls on the main campus. The University is home to Nevada\u2019s first medical school \u2013 the University of Nevada, Reno School of Medicine and the Wayne L. Prim Campus at Lake Tahoe, where education, research, and creative work thrive in a stunning mountain setting. The University delivers on its original land-grant mission with outreach across the state through the University of Nevada, Reno Extension, Nevada Agricultural Experiment Station, Nevada Bureau of Mines and Geology, Nevada Small Business Development Center, the Nevada Seismological Laboratory, and Wolf Pack Athletics. The main campus is in Reno, Nevada, a burgeoning global technology hub with a vibrant midtown and downtown. Found where the high desert of the Great Basin meets the High Sierra and Lake Tahoe, the beautiful, 290-acre main campus is also a Nevada State Arboretum. The University\u2019s physical infrastructure is expanding with ongoing construction of the newly named John Tullock Business Building, set to open in fall 2025. Groundbreaking is also planned for a new State Public Health Laboratory Building on campus, alongside the recent acquisition of 16 parcels near campus, known as \u201cUniversity Village,\u201d to provide affordable housing for graduate students and early career faculty. Collaborating with world-renowned organizations, the University fosters innovation through initiatives such as the Digital Wolf Pack partnership with Apple, which ensures equitable access to technology for students, providing new incoming freshman or transfer students with an iPad Air and accessories. Google\u2019s TechWise initiate is an 18-month program that fully supports undergraduate students in becoming entry-level software engineers by graduation. In 2023, the U.S. Department of Commerce\u2019s Economic Development Administration (EDA) announced that Nevada \u2013 led by the University of Nevada, Reno \u2013 was named one of the inaugural 31 Regional\u00a0Technology and Innovation\u00a0Hubs (TechHubs).\u00a0Nevada was selected from a competitive pool of 489 applicants from across the United States and its territories.\u00a0Nevada\u2019s Tech Hub will strengthen America\u2019s lithium batteries, critical elements and other electric vehicle materials industry sectors within the state. Through its commitment to high-impact education, world-improving research and creative activity, and outreach that\u2019s transforming Nevada\u2019s communities and businesses, the University continues its 150-year tradition of benefitting our state, nation and world. The University recognizes that diversity promotes excellence in education and research. The inclusive and engaged community on campus recognizes the added value that students, faculty, and staff from different backgrounds bring to the educational experience. Today, the University delivers on its original land-grant mission of access to education and knowledge by investing in the academics, facilities, support, engagement and vibrant campus life that promote our diverse students\u2019 cognitive growth and academic achievement \u2013 all while remaining one of the best values in American higher education. For more information, please visit\u00a0the University\u2019s website . University 